window.PREVIEW_MOCKS = window.PREVIEW_MOCKS || {};
window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["cip-2020"] = {"id": "cip-2020", "title": "CIP Ranchi 2020", "fullTitle": "CIP Ranchi 2020, M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper)", "sub": "100 real questions · key from the Panwar pack, checked · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "______ is the process which involves detecting, translating, and transmitting from the external environment to the brain.", "options": {"a": "adaptation", "b": "sensation", "c": "selective attention", "d": "perception"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: sensation is the process of detecting, translating and transmitting energy from the external environment to the brain. Sensation has three steps. First, specialised receptor cells detect a physical stimulus: light in the eye, sound waves in the ear, chemicals on the tongue and in the nose, pressure and temperature on the skin. Second, the receptors translate that energy into nerve impulses, a step called transduction. Third, sensory nerves transmit the impulses to the brain, where most senses are relayed through the thalamus to the cortex. The scientific study of this link between physical energy and experience is psychophysics, founded by Gustav Theodor Fechner in Elements of Psychophysics (1860), building on Ernst Heinrich Weber's work on the just noticeable difference. Example: when you step into sunlight, light strikes the rods and cones of your retina, which pass signals through the bipolar cells to the ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve; recognising that the shape ahead is a friend's face is the next stage, perception. Option b is right because detecting, transducing and transmitting raw stimulus energy is the textbook definition of sensation. Exam trap: sensation is the raw input from receptors to brain, while perception is the organising and interpreting of that input; the words \"to the brain\" mark sensation. Key note: the coaching book prints a here, but its own explanation describes sensation, so b is used.", "topic": "Sensation: detection, transduction, transmission"}, {"q": "Which option is not commonly diagnosed in criminals?", "options": {"a": "Depersonalization Disorder", "b": "Schizophrenia", "c": "Narcissistic Personality", "d": "Anti-Social Personality"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: depersonalization disorder is not commonly diagnosed in criminals. Depersonalization/derealization disorder (DSM-5-TR) involves persistent or recurrent experiences of feeling detached from one's own thoughts, feelings, body or actions, as if watching oneself from outside (depersonalization), or of the surroundings seeming dreamlike or unreal (derealization). Reality testing stays intact: the person says \"it is as if I am in a dream\", knows it is not so, and is usually frightened by the feeling. It is a dissociative disorder with no special link to offending. By contrast, forensic research shows antisocial personality disorder is the most common diagnosis among offenders; a large review of prison surveys by Seena Fazel and John Danesh (2002) found that nearly half of male prisoners met its criteria. Narcissistic traits are over-represented among violent offenders, and psychotic disorders such as schizophrenia, though present in only a minority of prisoners, occur at several times the general-population rate. Clinical example: a college student who, under exam stress, feels her hands belong to someone else and her room looks like a film set has depersonalization and derealization, not a criminal tendency. Option a is right because depersonalization is an inward, distressing dissociative experience with no established link to crime. Exam trap: do not pick schizophrenia just because most people with schizophrenia are not violent; the question asks about rates among criminals, where it is elevated.", "topic": "Depersonalization disorder and criminality"}, {"q": "People lose touch with reality when they are...", "options": {"a": "Psychotic", "b": "Manic", "c": "Neurotic", "d": "Psychopathic"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: people lose touch with reality when they are psychotic. Psychosis is a loss of contact with reality, shown by delusions (fixed false beliefs), hallucinations (perceptions without an external stimulus) and grossly disorganised speech or behaviour. Reality testing, the ability to tell inner experience from the outside world, breaks down, and insight is usually poor: the person does not recognise that anything is wrong. The classic contrast is with neurosis, a word coined by the Scottish physician William Cullen in 1769 and later narrowed by Sigmund Freud to non-psychotic disorders of unconscious conflict in which insight is preserved. Older classifications divided most mental illness into these two groups. Schizophrenia, delusional disorder, brief psychotic disorder and mood disorders with psychotic features are the main psychotic conditions today. Clinical example: a man who insists that his neighbours have planted a chip in his brain and hears them commenting on his actions is psychotic; a woman who fears contamination and washes her hands for hours while knowing the fear is excessive is not, because her reality testing is intact. Option a is right because loss of reality contact is the defining feature of psychosis. Exam trap: \"psychopathic\" sounds like \"psychotic\" but means a callous, remorseless personality style with reality testing intact, and \"neurotic\" means distress with insight retained.", "topic": "Psychosis as loss of reality contact"}, {"q": "The MMPI is used to measure:", "options": {"a": "Leadership potential", "b": "Personality and psychological disorders", "c": "The Big Five traits", "d": "Unconscious drives"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the MMPI measures personality and psychological disorders. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was developed at the University of Minnesota by the psychologist Starke R. Hathaway and the psychiatrist J. Charnley McKinley and published in 1943. They used empirical criterion keying: an item was kept if a psychiatric patient group (for example, people with depression) answered it differently from ordinary people, whatever the item seemed to be about. The result was ten clinical scales, including Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Paranoia, Schizophrenia and Social Introversion (added by Drake in 1946), plus validity scales (L for lying, F for infrequent responding, and K for defensiveness, added by Meehl and Hathaway in 1946) that show whether the person answered honestly. The MMPI-2 (1989) has 567 true/false items; the MMPI-2-RF and MMPI-3 are shorter revisions, and the MMPI-A is for adolescents. Clinical example: a psychologist assessing a patient with vague physical complaints may find Hypochondriasis and Hysteria raised well above Depression, the classic \"conversion V\" profile, which guides diagnosis and treatment planning. Option b is right because the MMPI is the most widely used objective inventory for assessing personality and psychopathology. Exam trap: the MMPI is an objective self-report inventory, not a projective test, and it was built empirically, not from the Big Five or any theory of traits.", "topic": "MMPI: purpose and construction"}, {"q": "Insight learning is basically learning by", "options": {"a": "Cognition", "b": "Conditions", "c": "Trial and Error", "d": "Imitation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: insight learning is basically learning by cognition. Insight learning was described by the Gestalt psychologist Wolfgang Kohler, who studied chimpanzees at the anthropoid station on Tenerife between 1913 and 1920 and reported the work in The Mentality of Apes (1917 in German, 1925 in English). A banana was hung out of reach or placed outside the cage. After failed attempts, a chimpanzee named Sultan would suddenly join two short sticks into a long one (the keeper saw him hit on it while idly playing with the sticks, then run straight to the bars), and in the box tests the apes paced, stopped short and dragged or stacked boxes to get the fruit. Kohler argued that the animal had mentally restructured the perceptual field, seeing the relationship between the parts and the goal. Insight has typical marks: the solution comes suddenly (the \"aha\" experience), it is performed smoothly once found, it is remembered, and it transfers to similar problems. Everyday example: you struggle with a riddle, stop, and the answer suddenly clicks, after which you can solve others like it. Option a is right because insight depends on thinking and understanding relationships, a cognitive process, rather than on reinforced associations. Exam trap: insight is the opposite of Edward L. Thorndike's gradual trial-and-error learning in the puzzle box, and Kohler's work was a Gestalt challenge to behaviourism.", "topic": "Köhler's insight learning"}, {"q": "“Bell” in Pavlov's classical conditioning is", "options": {"a": "UCS", "b": "NS", "c": "UCR", "d": "CR"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: in Pavlov's classical conditioning, the bell is the neutral stimulus (NS). Ivan Petrovich Pavlov, a Russian physiologist who won the 1904 Nobel Prize for his work on digestion, noticed that his dogs began salivating before food reached their mouths, and he set out to study this systematically. Food (meat powder) is the unconditioned stimulus (UCS) because it produces salivation without any learning; that salivation is the unconditioned response (UCR). A sound such as a bell (Pavlov's laboratory also used metronomes, buzzers and tones) at first produces no salivation, only an orienting response, so it is a neutral stimulus. After the sound is repeatedly presented just before the food, it alone triggers salivation. At that point the bell has become the conditioned stimulus (CS), and salivation to it is the conditioned response (CR). Everyday example: a child who is regularly given an injection after smelling a hospital's antiseptic may start crying at that smell alone. Option b is right because, before conditioning, the bell is a stimulus that naturally elicits no salivation, which is the definition of a neutral stimulus. Exam trap: after conditioning the bell becomes the CS, which is not offered here, so choose NS; and never label a stimulus as a response (UCR or CR).", "topic": "Pavlov's bell as neutral stimulus"}, {"q": "When a behaviour occurs consistently in the presence of a discriminative stimulus, it is said to be under", "options": {"a": "Stimulus control", "b": "Delayed process", "c": "Extinction", "d": "Classical conditioning process"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: a behaviour that occurs consistently in the presence of a discriminative stimulus is under stimulus control. The idea comes from B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning. A discriminative stimulus (SD) is a cue signalling that a particular response will now be reinforced; a cue signalling no reinforcement is called an S-delta. When an organism learns to respond in the presence of the SD and not in its absence, the behaviour is under stimulus control. Together with the behaviour and its consequence, the SD forms the three-term contingency, often written A-B-C: antecedent, behaviour, consequence. In the laboratory, a pigeon learns to peck a key only when a green light is on, because only then are pecks rewarded with grain. In daily life, a chai stall's \"Open\" board signals that asking for tea will be rewarded, children ask a lenient grandparent rather than a strict parent for sweets, and drivers stop at red lights. Clinically, Richard Bootzin's stimulus control therapy for insomnia keeps the bed for sleep only, so that the bed becomes a cue for sleeping rather than worrying. Option a is right because consistent responding tied to a discriminative stimulus is exactly what stimulus control means. Exam trap: a discriminative stimulus does not elicit the response the way a conditioned stimulus does in classical conditioning; it only sets the occasion for an operant response.", "topic": "Discriminative stimulus and stimulus control"}, {"q": "Role learning is part of", "options": {"a": "Learning", "b": "Socialization", "c": "Rebuke condition", "d": "Reduction Change"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: role learning is part of socialization. Socialization is the lifelong process by which a person learns the norms, values, skills and roles of a society until they become part of the self. A role is the set of expectations, rights and duties attached to a social position (status), such as daughter, student, teacher or doctor, and learning these roles is one of socialization's main tasks. Charles Horton Cooley described the looking-glass self, in which we come to see ourselves through others' reactions. George Herbert Mead explained how children take roles: in the play stage a child acts out particular others (playing \"mother\" or \"doctor\"), and in the game stage the child grasps the generalised other, the expectations of the whole group. Primary socialization happens in the family; secondary socialization continues through school, peers, work and the media. Robert K. Merton added anticipatory socialization, rehearsing the role of a group one hopes to join. Example: a medical aspirant who already talks and dresses like a doctor, or a new bride learning what her in-laws expect of her, is engaged in role learning. Option b is right because acquiring socially defined roles is a central component of socialization. Exam trap: role learning does involve learning, but the question asks which wider social process it belongs to, and that process is socialization.", "topic": "Role learning within socialization"}, {"q": "The impact of child-rearing practices on personality development was discovered by", "options": {"a": "Freud", "b": "Gestalt", "c": "Berkowitz", "d": "Watson"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: Watson. John B. Watson, who launched behaviourism with his 1913 paper \"Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It\", held that personality is the sum of learned habits and that the environment parents create shapes the child. With Rosalie Rayner he conditioned fear in the infant Little Albert (1920) by pairing a white rat with a loud noise, which he took as proof that emotional reactions are built by conditioning. His 1925 boast, \"Give me a dozen healthy infants\", claimed he could train any child into any type of adult, whatever its heredity. In Psychological Care of Infant and Child (1928), written with Rayner, he advised strict feeding schedules and little hugging or kissing, arguing that rearing practices directly mould a child's character. Example: on Watson's view, a child whose tantrums are rewarded with attention develops a demanding personality because of what the parents reinforced, not because of inborn temperament. Option d is right because the paper credits Watson's behaviourist claim that parents, by controlling the child's stimulus-response associations, determine personality. Exam trap: \"Gestalt\" is a school, not a person, and Berkowitz is an aggression researcher, so the real contest is between Watson and Freud. Key note: Sigmund Freud's theory that feeding, weaning and toilet training shape adult character is a strong rival answer, so this key is arguable; it follows the coaching book's behaviourist reading.", "topic": "Watson on child rearing and personality"}, {"q": "An organism tends to repeat those behaviours that bring about satisfaction, and it tends to discard those that bring about dissatisfaction", "options": {"a": "Law of similarity", "b": "Law of proximity of Hull", "c": "Thorndike's Law of Effect", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: Thorndike's Law of Effect. Edward L. Thorndike, an American psychologist, studied hungry cats in puzzle boxes for his 1898 doctoral work, Animal Intelligence. A cat could escape and reach food only by pulling a loop or pressing a lever. At first it scratched and clawed at random until it hit the latch by chance. Over trials the useless responses dropped out and the correct one came sooner, a gradual curve showing trial-and-error learning rather than sudden understanding. Thorndike explained this with the Law of Effect, stated formally in 1911: responses followed by a satisfying state of affairs are \"stamped in\" (the stimulus-response connection is strengthened), and responses followed by an annoying state are \"stamped out\". He also proposed the laws of exercise and readiness, and his theory is called connectionism. In the 1930s he revised the law, concluding that punishment weakens responses far less than reward strengthens them. Everyday example: a child praised for tidying the room tidies more often, while one whose joke is met with silence stops telling it. Option c is right because the statement is almost word for word Thorndike's definition of the Law of Effect. Exam trap: this law is the forerunner of B. F. Skinner's principle of reinforcement, while \"proximity\" and \"similarity\" are Gestalt laws of perceptual grouping, not laws of learning.", "topic": "Thorndike's Law of Effect"}, {"q": "With the help of ______, the behaviour of children can easily be modified", "options": {"a": "Classical conditioning", "b": "Operant Conditioning", "c": "Simple Conditioning", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: with the help of operant conditioning, the behaviour of children can easily be modified. Operant conditioning, developed by B. F. Skinner from Edward L. Thorndike's Law of Effect, holds that behaviour is controlled by its consequences. Reinforcement, either positive (adding something pleasant) or negative (removing something unpleasant), makes a behaviour more likely; punishment and extinction make it less likely. Because parents and teachers control many of the consequences in a child's life, these principles form the basis of behaviour modification and applied behaviour analysis. Common techniques include shaping (reinforcing successive approximations, as when a child with autism is rewarded first for eye contact, then for any sound, then for a word), token economies (stars or points exchanged for rewards, developed by Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin), the Premack principle (\"finish your homework, then you can play\"), time-out and response cost. Clinical example: a seven-year-old who refuses homework earns a sticker for each completed page and a weekend outing for ten stickers, and homework completion rises within weeks. Option b is right because changing voluntary behaviour through planned consequences is the core application of operant conditioning. Exam trap: classical conditioning also has uses with children, such as the bell-and-pad alarm for bedwetting, but it changes involuntary reflexive responses, not everyday voluntary behaviour.", "topic": "Operant conditioning for child behaviour"}, {"q": "What technique is used by TAT?", "options": {"a": "Inventory", "b": "Interviewing", "c": "Analytical", "d": "Projective"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the TAT uses the projective technique. The Thematic Apperception Test was developed by Christiana D. Morgan and Henry A. Murray at the Harvard Psychological Clinic and first described in 1935. It has 31 cards: 30 pictures of ambiguous scenes and one blank card, on which the person imagines a picture. The examiner shows a selection of cards and asks for a story about each: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters think and feel, and how it ends. The projective hypothesis, named by Lawrence K. Frank in 1939, holds that when a stimulus is ambiguous, people fill it with their own needs, conflicts and attitudes. Murray read the stories through his need-press theory: the hero's needs (such as achievement or affiliation) and the environmental press acting on the hero. David McClelland later used TAT-type pictures to measure need for achievement, and Uma Chowdhury adapted the test for India. Clinical example: a client whose heroes are repeatedly abandoned and helpless may be revealing themes of loss and dependency. Option d is right because the TAT presents ambiguous material and infers hidden motives from what the person projects onto it. Exam trap: the TAT relies on apperception (interpreting through past experience), not mere perception, and the Children's Apperception Test of Leopold and Sonya Bellak uses animal figures.", "topic": "TAT as a projective technique"}, {"q": "Learned helplessness underlines psychological cases of ________.", "options": {"a": "Conflict", "b": "Frustration", "c": "Anxiety", "d": "Depression"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: learned helplessness underlies psychological cases of depression. Martin E. P. Seligman and Steven F. Maier reported learned helplessness in 1967. Dogs first exposed to electric shocks they could not escape were later placed in a shuttle box where simply jumping a low barrier would end the shock. Instead of escaping, most lay down and whimpered; they had learned that their actions made no difference. Dogs that had not received uncontrollable shocks learned to escape quickly. In Helplessness (1975), Seligman proposed this as a model of depression: repeated uncontrollable bad events produce passivity, low motivation, sadness and difficulty seeing that one's actions can work. In 1978 Lyn Abramson, Seligman and John Teasdale reformulated the theory, arguing that depression follows when people explain bad events with internal, stable and global causes (\"It is my fault, it will always be like this, and it ruins everything\"). This later grew into hopelessness theory. Clinical example: a student who has failed several times despite studying hard stops attending classes, saying nothing she does will ever matter. Option d is right because learned helplessness is one of the classic psychological theories of depression. Exam trap: learned helplessness is about lack of control producing passivity; do not confuse it with frustration, which is goal-blocking that tends to produce aggression.", "topic": "Learned helplessness and depression"}, {"q": "The concept of “Programmed learning” was introduced by", "options": {"a": "Skinner", "b": "Hull", "c": "Watson", "d": "Thorndike"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Skinner introduced the concept of programmed learning. B. F. Skinner, after watching his daughter's arithmetic class in 1953, felt that a teacher could not reinforce each pupil's correct answers quickly enough. In his 1954 paper \"The Science of Learning and the Art of Teaching\" he applied operant conditioning to education through programmed instruction, delivered by teaching machines or programmed textbooks. The material is broken into small steps called frames. The learner makes an active response to each frame, receives immediate feedback that acts as reinforcement, and moves on at their own pace, with steps small enough that errors are rare. This is linear programming, since every learner follows the same sequence; Norman Crowder later developed branching programmes, which send a learner who errs to a remedial frame. Sidney Pressey had built an early teaching machine in the 1920s, but it served mainly for testing; Skinner supplied the reinforcement theory that made programmed learning a method. Everyday example: language apps that teach in short exercises, mark each answer instantly and unlock the next level are descendants of programmed instruction. Option a is right because programmed learning is Skinner's direct application of operant principles to teaching. Exam trap: Pressey built the first teaching machine and Thorndike imagined self-paced books, but the concept of programmed learning is credited to Skinner.", "topic": "Skinner's programmed learning"}, {"q": "Psychoanalytical theory about human personality was proposed by", "options": {"a": "Carl Jung", "b": "Sigmund Freud", "c": "Skinner", "d": "Alfred Adler"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: psychoanalytic theory of personality was proposed by Sigmund Freud. Freud (1856 to 1939), a Viennese neurologist, developed psychoanalysis from his work with hysterical patients, first with Josef Breuer in Studies on Hysteria (1895) and then in The Interpretation of Dreams (1900). He held that much of mental life is unconscious and that behaviour is driven by instincts, chiefly the sexual drive (libido) and later aggression. His topographic model divides the mind into conscious, preconscious and unconscious levels; his structural model (The Ego and the Id, 1923) divides personality into the pleasure-seeking id, the superego or internalised conscience, and the ego, which mediates between them and reality. Anxiety signals danger, and the ego protects itself with defence mechanisms such as repression and projection. Personality develops through the oral, anal, phallic, latency and genital psychosexual stages, and unresolved conflict at a stage can leave a fixation. His methods were free association, dream analysis and the study of slips of the tongue. Example: a man who calls his wife by his mother's name may, in Freudian terms, be revealing an unconscious wish. Option b is right because Freud founded and named psychoanalysis. Exam trap: Carl Jung's theory is analytical psychology and Alfred Adler's is individual psychology; both are psychodynamic, but only Freud's is psychoanalysis.", "topic": "Freud's psychoanalytic theory of personality"}, {"q": "A Null Hypothesis is", "options": {"a": "Hypothesis of no difference", "b": "Hypothesis of zero significance", "c": "Hypothesis that assigns the value of zero to the variable", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: a null hypothesis is a hypothesis of no difference. The term was coined by Ronald A. Fisher in The Design of Experiments (1935), and Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson later paired it with the alternative hypothesis and defined Type I and Type II errors. The null hypothesis, written H0, states that there is no difference between groups, or no relationship between variables, in the population, so any difference seen in the sample is due to chance. For example, H0 may say that mean anxiety in a yoga group equals mean anxiety in a control group, or that the correlation between sleep and marks is zero. The alternative hypothesis (H1) states that a difference or relationship exists. A statistical test calculates how likely the observed data would be if H0 were true; if that probability (the p value) falls below the chosen level, usually .05, the researcher rejects H0 and accepts H1. Otherwise the researcher \"fails to reject\" H0, which is not the same as proving it true. Clinical example: in a trial of a new antidepressant, H0 says the drug and placebo produce the same improvement. Option a is right because \"no difference\" (or no relationship) is the standard definition of the null hypothesis. Exam trap: H0 sets the difference or relationship at zero, not the variable itself.", "topic": "Null hypothesis: no difference"}, {"q": "The advantages of Sampling is", "options": {"a": "Time-saving", "b": "Increased accuracy", "c": "Capital-saving", "d": "Both “a” and “b”."}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: both a and b, time-saving and increased accuracy. Sampling means studying a part of a population to draw conclusions about the whole. In India, Prasanta Chandra Mahalanobis of the Indian Statistical Institute championed large-scale sample surveys and designed the National Sample Survey, showing that a well-drawn sample gives reliable estimates faster and more cheaply than a census. Standard texts list the advantages. Sampling saves time, because fewer units are studied and results come sooner. It saves money. It can be more accurate, because a few trained investigators collect data more carefully than an army of hasty ones, which reduces non-sampling errors such as careless recording. It allows more detailed study of each case, and it is the only option when the population is infinite or testing destroys what is tested, as in checking the life of light bulbs. Example: a psychologist estimating exam anxiety among a city's college students can survey a random sample of 1,000 instead of every student and finish in weeks, not years. Option d is right because it combines two genuine advantages of sampling. Exam trap: the accuracy gain comes from fewer non-sampling errors; sampling error itself exists only in samples. Key note: the coaching book itself says c (capital or cost saving) is also correct, but with no \"all of the above\" option, d is kept as the intended answer.", "topic": "Advantages of sampling"}, {"q": "The method of Randomization involves", "options": {"a": "Lottery", "b": "Tippit's table of random digits", "c": "Coin method", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: all of the above, since the lottery, Tippett's table of random digits and the coin method are all ways of randomizing. Randomization means letting chance alone decide which units enter a sample, or which participants go to which group, so that every unit has an equal and independent chance. Ronald A. Fisher made random assignment a cornerstone of experimental design because it spreads unknown extraneous variables evenly across groups. In the lottery or fishbowl method, names or numbers are written on identical slips, mixed and drawn blind. A table of random numbers is a long list of digits with no pattern; the first published table was by the British statistician L. H. C. Tippett in 1927, with 41,600 digits taken from census figures, later followed by tables from Fisher and Frank Yates and the RAND Corporation's million random digits. The paper spells the name \"Tippit\"; the correct spelling is Tippett. The coin method uses tosses (heads to the treatment group, tails to the control group) when there are two groups. Today computers generate random numbers. Clinical example: 40 patients with panic disorder can be assigned to CBT or a waitlist by tossing a coin for each. Option d is right because all three listed methods are recognised randomization techniques. Exam trap: random does not mean haphazard or convenient; picking whoever walks in first is not randomization.", "topic": "Methods of randomization"}, {"q": "Scientific methods are used in", "options": {"a": "Only research projects in pure sciences", "b": "Social science research", "c": "Both “a” and “b”", "d": "Neither “a” nor “b”"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: scientific methods are used both in research in the pure sciences and in social science research. The scientific method is a way of gaining knowledge that is systematic, empirical (based on observation), controlled, objective, public and self-correcting. Fred N. Kerlinger, in Foundations of Behavioral Research, described scientific research as systematic, controlled, empirical and critical investigation of hypothetical propositions about the relations among natural phenomena. He drew on Charles Sanders Peirce's four methods of knowing (tenacity, authority, a priori reasoning and science) to show that only science has built-in checks against error. The same steps apply in any field: identify a problem, review the literature, form a testable hypothesis, design the study, collect and analyse data, and draw conclusions that others can replicate. Psychology, sociology and economics use experiments, surveys, observation and statistics just as physics and chemistry use laboratory measurement. Social sciences face extra difficulties, such as human variability and ethical limits on control, but the logic of inquiry is the same. Example: a psychologist who tests whether mindfulness lowers exam anxiety by randomly assigning students to training or a waitlist follows the same reasoning as a chemist testing a catalyst. Option c is right because the scientific method is not tied to any subject matter. Exam trap: the word \"only\" in option a makes it false.", "topic": "Scientific method across sciences"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a type of Hypothesis?", "options": {"a": "Interrogative Hypothesis", "b": "Declarative Hypothesis", "c": "Directional Hypothesis", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: all of the above, since interrogative, declarative and directional are all listed as types (forms) of hypothesis. A hypothesis is a tentative, testable statement about the relationship between two or more variables; Fred N. Kerlinger called it a conjectural statement of the relation between variables. Many texts classify hypotheses by their written form. An interrogative or question-form hypothesis is phrased as a question: \"Is there a difference in anxiety between first-year and final-year students?\" A declarative hypothesis is a positive statement that a relationship or difference exists: \"There is a difference in anxiety between first-year and final-year students.\" A directional hypothesis also states which way the difference goes, for example \"First-year students will report higher anxiety than final-year students\", and is tested with a one-tailed test. A non-directional hypothesis predicts a difference without saying which way (two-tailed test), and the null hypothesis predicts no difference. Clinical example: a researcher who predicts that CBT will reduce depression scores more than supportive counselling has written a directional hypothesis. Option d is right because, in this classification, all three listed forms are recognised types. Exam trap: link directional with one-tailed and non-directional with two-tailed tests; do not reverse them. Key note: strict texts such as Kerlinger treat a question as a research problem rather than a hypothesis, but the paper follows the forms classification, so d stands.", "topic": "Forms and types of hypotheses"}, {"q": "A non-probability sampling method is", "options": {"a": "Simple random sampling", "b": "Cluster sampling", "c": "Systematic sampling", "d": "Quota sampling"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: quota sampling is a non-probability sampling method. In probability sampling, every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of selection decided by a random process, which allows sampling error to be estimated. Probability methods include simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster and multistage sampling. In non-probability sampling, selection depends on the researcher's judgement or convenience, so each member's chance of selection is unknown. The main non-probability methods are convenience (accidental), purposive (judgement), quota and snowball sampling. In quota sampling, the researcher fixes how many people are needed from each subgroup, often in proportion to the population (say 60 women and 40 men, or a set number per age band), and fieldworkers then fill those quotas with whoever is available. It resembles stratified sampling but has no random selection within groups, so interviewers tend to choose approachable people and bias creeps in. A famous failure: quota-based polls in the 1948 United States presidential election predicted that Thomas Dewey would beat Harry Truman. Example: a market researcher in a mall who must interview 50 college students and 50 working adults and stops whoever passes is using quota sampling. Option d is right because quota sampling fills subgroup targets without random selection. Exam trap: quota sampling looks like stratified random sampling, but only stratified sampling selects randomly within each stratum.", "topic": "Quota sampling as non-probability"}, {"q": "For the population of a finite size, which sampling method is generally preferred?", "options": {"a": "Cluster sampling", "b": "Purposive sampling", "c": "Area sampling", "d": "Systematic sampling"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: for a population of finite size, systematic sampling is generally preferred. In systematic sampling, the researcher lists the population, calculates a sampling interval k by dividing the population size (N) by the desired sample size (n), picks a random start between 1 and k, and then takes every kth unit. For example, to draw 100 students from a register of 2,000, k = 20; with a random start of 7, the sample is the 7th, 27th, 47th student and so on. The method needs a known, countable population and a complete sampling frame, which is exactly what a finite population provides; with an infinite or unlisted population, N and k cannot be fixed. It is quick, easy for fieldworkers, and spreads the sample evenly across the list. Its one danger is periodicity: if the list repeats a pattern that matches k, such as a hostel list where every fourth name is a room prefect, the sample can be badly biased. Clinical example: a hospital psychologist auditing 50 of 1,000 case files can take every 20th file from the record register. Option d is right because systematic sampling works best when the population is finite and listed. Exam trap: systematic sampling is a probability method because the start is random, even though some Indian texts call it a mixed method.", "topic": "Systematic sampling for finite populations"}, {"q": "Which of the following is related to the concept of intelligence ______", "options": {"a": "Cognition", "b": "Motivation", "c": "Perception", "d": "Learning"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: cognition is the concept most closely related to intelligence. Cognition covers all the mental processes of knowing: attention, perception, memory, language, reasoning, problem solving and decision making. Intelligence is the general ability to use these processes well. Charles Spearman (1904) found that people who do well on one mental test tend to do well on others and called this common factor g, general intelligence. David Wechsler defined intelligence as the global capacity to act purposefully, think rationally and deal effectively with the environment. Later models, such as Robert J. Sternberg's triarchic theory and the PASS model of J. P. Das and Jack Naglieri, explain intelligence through cognitive processes such as planning, attention and information processing. That is why intelligence tests are classed as measures of cognitive ability. Example: solving a Raven's Progressive Matrices item requires attending to patterns, holding rules in working memory and reasoning about the missing piece, all cognitive operations. Option a is right because intelligence is fundamentally a property of cognition, the umbrella term that contains perception, learning and memory. Exam trap: motivation affects test scores but is a non-cognitive (conative) factor, not intelligence itself. Key note: learning and perception are also linked to intelligence (Walter Dearborn even defined intelligence as the capacity to learn), but cognition is the broader term that includes them, so a is the best answer.", "topic": "Intelligence as a cognitive ability"}, {"q": "______ is a dimension of visual experience.", "options": {"a": "Loudness", "b": "Timbre", "c": "Pitch", "d": "Brightness"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: brightness is a dimension of visual experience. Psychology separates the physical properties of a stimulus from the psychological dimensions we experience. Light is electromagnetic energy, and humans see wavelengths of roughly 380 to 760 nanometres. Its three physical properties map onto three visual dimensions: wavelength is experienced as hue (the colour name, such as red or blue), intensity or amplitude is experienced as brightness, and purity (how far a single wavelength dominates rather than a mixture) is experienced as saturation, the vividness of the colour. Sound has a parallel set: frequency is heard as pitch, amplitude as loudness, and complexity (the mix of overtones) as timbre. Everyday example: dimming a red lamp lowers its brightness while the hue stays red, and adding white light to that red turns it pink, which lowers saturation. A flute and a violin playing the same note at the same volume differ only in timbre. Option d is right because brightness is the visual dimension that corresponds to the intensity of light, while loudness, timbre and pitch all describe hearing. Exam trap: amplitude maps to brightness in vision but to loudness in hearing, and wavelength gives hue in vision while frequency gives pitch in hearing, so identify the sense before matching the property.", "topic": "Dimensions of visual experience: brightness"}, {"q": "The prototype of the modern intelligence test was developed by:", "options": {"a": "Wechsler", "b": "Binet", "c": "Terman", "d": "Stanford"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the prototype of the modern intelligence test was developed by Binet. In 1904 the French Ministry of Public Instruction asked for a way to identify children who could not benefit from ordinary schooling and needed special classes. Alfred Binet, working with the physician Theodore Simon, rejected Francis Galton's approach of measuring reaction time and sensory acuity and instead tested higher mental processes such as judgement, comprehension and reasoning. The Binet-Simon scale of 1905 had 30 items arranged in order of difficulty. The 1908 revision grouped items by the age at which most children passed them, creating the concept of mental age, and a further revision followed in 1911, the year Binet died. Later work built on this prototype: William Stern proposed the intelligence quotient (mental age divided by chronological age), and Lewis M. Terman of Stanford University adapted the test as the Stanford-Binet (1916), multiplying the ratio by 100. Example: on the 1908 scale, a seven-year-old who passed the items most nine-year-olds pass had a mental age of nine. Option b is right because the Binet-Simon scale was the first practical intelligence test and the model for later tests. Exam trap: Terman popularised the IQ in America and Wechsler introduced the deviation IQ, but neither created the first test, and \"Stanford\" is a university, not a person.", "topic": "Binet-Simon scale, first intelligence test"}, {"q": "______ introduced the concept of adaptation.", "options": {"a": "Kohler", "b": "Brown", "c": "Helson", "d": "Wertheimer"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: Harry Helson introduced the concept of adaptation level. Helson (1898 to 1977), an American psychologist, set out adaptation-level theory in the late 1940s and in his book Adaptation-Level Theory (1964). He argued that we never judge a stimulus in absolute terms. Instead, we carry a running neutral point, the adaptation level, formed by pooling three sources: the focal stimulus being judged, the background (contextual) stimuli around it, and residual stimuli from past experience. Anything above this level feels heavy, bright, hot or pleasant; anything below it feels light, dim, cold or unpleasant; stimuli near it feel neutral. Everyday example: after carrying a heavy suitcase, a school bag feels feather-light, and a 20 degree day feels chilly to someone from Chennai but warm to someone from Shimla. The idea later spread to motivation and well-being: Philip Brickman and Donald Campbell (1971) used it to describe the hedonic treadmill, in which a pay rise or a lottery win stops feeling special once the adaptation level catches up. Option c is right because adaptation level is Helson's signature concept. Exam trap: do not confuse Helson's adaptation level with sensory adaptation (receptors fading under an unchanging stimulus) or with Jean Piaget's adaptation (assimilation plus accommodation).", "topic": "Helson's adaptation-level theory"}, {"q": "Which one part of the brain relays the color, smell, and feel of the flowers?", "options": {"a": "Thalamus", "b": "Acetylcholine", "c": "Motor area", "d": "None of the these."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the thalamus relays the colour, smell and feel of the flowers. The thalamus is a paired, egg-shaped mass of grey matter in the diencephalon, sitting on top of the brainstem on either side of the third ventricle. It is called the brain's relay station, or gateway to the cortex, because incoming sensory signals stop at one of its nuclei before being passed to the right area of cerebral cortex. Vision travels through the lateral geniculate nucleus to the occipital lobe, hearing through the medial geniculate nucleus to the temporal lobe, and touch, pain and temperature through the ventral posterior nuclei to the somatosensory cortex of the parietal lobe. So when you look at a rose and hold it, both the colour and the soft feel of the petals pass through the thalamus. Option a is right because it is the classic textbook sensory relay, and the only relay station among the options. Exam trap: smell is the famous exception; olfactory signals go from the olfactory bulb straight to the olfactory (piriform) cortex and amygdala without first synapsing in the thalamus. Key note: because smell largely bypasses the thalamus, the question is loosely worded, but the key stands: a secondary olfactory route does pass through the mediodorsal thalamus to the orbitofrontal cortex, and no other option is a sensory relay.", "topic": "Thalamus as the sensory relay"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a treatment developed from classical conditioning principles?", "options": {"a": "Token economies", "b": "Flooding", "c": "Aversion therapies", "d": "Systematic desensitization."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: token economies are not built on classical conditioning; they come from operant conditioning. Classical conditioning, discovered by Ivan Pavlov, is learning by association: a neutral stimulus paired with one that already triggers a reflex comes to trigger a similar response on its own. Several therapies apply this to emotional responses. Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe (1958), pairs relaxation with a graded fear hierarchy so that relaxation replaces anxiety (counterconditioning, or reciprocal inhibition). Flooding exposes the person to the feared stimulus at full strength, with escape blocked, until the conditioned fear extinguishes. Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour, such as drinking alcohol, with an unpleasant stimulus like a nausea-inducing drug so that the behaviour itself becomes aversive. A token economy works differently: it uses consequences. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin ran one on a psychiatric ward, described in The Token Economy (1968): patients earned tokens (generalised secondary reinforcers) for self-care and ward jobs and exchanged them for privileges, exactly as B. F. Skinner's operant principles predict. A child earning stars for homework that buy screen time is a token economy at home. Option a is right because tokens strengthen behaviour through reinforcement after the response, not through stimulus pairing. Exam trap: ask whether the technique changes what follows a behaviour (operant) or what a stimulus is paired with (classical).", "topic": "Token economy as operant technique"}, {"q": "Parts of the autonomic nervous system are ______", "options": {"a": "Sympathetic and parasympathetic", "b": "Voluntary and involuntary", "c": "Thalamus and hypothalamus", "d": "Cerebrospinal and cortical"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the two parts of the autonomic nervous system are the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions. The nervous system splits into the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system. The peripheral system has a somatic branch, which carries sensation and voluntary movement of skeletal muscles, and an autonomic branch, which runs the heart, glands, blood vessels and the smooth muscle of the gut largely without conscious control. The British physiologist John Newport Langley named the autonomic nervous system in 1898. Its sympathetic division, arising from the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord, prepares the body for action in what Walter B. Cannon called the fight-or-flight response: the heart races, pupils dilate, digestion slows and glucose is released. The parasympathetic division, arising from the brainstem and sacral cord, does the rest-and-digest work: it slows the heart, constricts the pupils and restarts digestion. Picture a near-miss on a scooter: the pounding heart is sympathetic, and the slow calming afterwards is parasympathetic. Option a is right because these two divisions are the standard textbook parts of the autonomic system. Exam trap: voluntary versus involuntary is the split between somatic and autonomic, not a division within the autonomic system; some modern texts add a third, enteric division in the gut wall, but exam keys expect sympathetic and parasympathetic.", "topic": "Divisions of the autonomic nervous system"}, {"q": "The concept of introversion and extroversion was advanced by", "options": {"a": "Spranger", "b": "Eysenc", "c": "Kretshmer", "d": "Jung"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: Carl Gustav Jung advanced the concept of introversion and extraversion. In Psychological Types (1921) Jung, the Swiss psychiatrist who founded analytical psychology, described two attitudes, or directions of psychic energy (libido). In extraversion, energy flows outward to people, objects and events; in introversion, it flows inward to thoughts, feelings and the inner world. Everyone has both, but one usually dominates while the other stays in the unconscious. Jung crossed the two attitudes with four functions (thinking, feeling, sensation, intuition) to get eight psychological types, the basis of the later Myers-Briggs Type Indicator. Example: after a long party, an extravert feels recharged, while an introvert needs a quiet evening alone to recover. Hans Eysenck later turned extraversion-introversion into a measurable dimension tied to cortical arousal, but he borrowed it from Jung. Option d is right because Jung coined and first systematically developed the two attitudes. Exam trap: students pick Eysenck because his tests (EPI, EPQ) measure extraversion; remember that Jung introduced the concept and Eysenck measured it. Jung spelled it \"extraversion\"; \"extroversion\" is the popular spelling. The paper also misspells two names: \"Eysenc\" is Hans Eysenck and \"Kretshmer\" is Ernst Kretschmer.", "topic": "Jung's introversion and extraversion"}, {"q": "Dependence, passivity, and greediness are regarded as traits found in the:", "options": {"a": "Anal character", "b": "Genital stage", "c": "Phallic stage", "d": "Oral character"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: dependence, passivity and greediness are traits of the oral character. Sigmund Freud proposed that personality develops through psychosexual stages, each centred on a body zone, and that too much or too little gratification at a stage leaves a fixation that shows up as adult traits. The first is the oral stage, from birth to about 18 months, when pleasure comes from sucking, swallowing and biting and the central issue is feeding and weaning. Karl Abraham, Freud's colleague, split it into two modes. The early oral-incorporative (oral-receptive) mode produces an adult who is dependent, passive, gullible (\"swallows\" anything) and greedy for food, drink, smoking, attention and reassurance. The later oral-aggressive (oral-sadistic) mode, linked with teething, produces sarcasm, biting remarks and argumentativeness. A clinical example is a client who constantly seeks reassurance, overeats under stress and expects others to take care of everything. Freud also linked alcohol dependence to oral fixation. Option d is right because passive dependence and greed are the textbook oral-receptive traits. Exam trap: the anal character is about control (orderliness, stinginess and stubbornness), not neediness; also note that \"oral character\" names an adult type, while the \"genital stage\" and \"phallic stage\" options name stages rather than character types.", "topic": "Freud's oral character traits"}, {"q": "Psychosomatic disorders are best illustrated by:", "options": {"a": "Asthma caused by anxiety", "b": "Insomnia", "c": "Neurotic anxiety", "d": "Over-eating"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: asthma caused by anxiety best illustrates a psychosomatic disorder. A psychosomatic (psychophysiological) disorder is a real physical illness, with actual tissue damage or measurable bodily change, that is caused or worsened by psychological factors such as stress, conflict or emotion. This differs from conversion and somatic symptom disorders, where there is no matching organic damage. Franz Alexander, a founder of psychosomatic medicine, wrote Psychosomatic Medicine (1950) and named seven classic examples, the \"holy seven\": bronchial asthma, peptic ulcer, essential hypertension, rheumatoid arthritis, ulcerative colitis, neurodermatitis and thyrotoxicosis. He believed a specific unconscious conflict acted on a vulnerable organ. Modern health psychology replaces this with a diathesis-stress view: stress hormones and autonomic arousal act on an organ that is already vulnerable. Example: a student with mild asthma has a severe attack on the morning of an entrance exam; the airway constriction is real and measurable, but anxiety triggered it. DSM-5-TR now codes this as psychological factors affecting other medical conditions. Option a is right because it pairs a real bodily disease (asthma) with a psychological cause (anxiety), the exact definition of a psychosomatic disorder. Exam trap: psychosomatic does not mean imaginary, and it is not conversion disorder; the illness is real, and only its trigger is psychological.", "topic": "Psychosomatic disorders defined"}, {"q": "Psychophysics is the study of the relationship between:", "options": {"a": "Sensory attributes and physical energy", "b": "Psychology and Physics", "c": "Perception and physiological process", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: psychophysics studies the relationship between sensory attributes (what we experience) and physical energy (the stimulus). Gustav Theodor Fechner, a German physicist and philosopher, founded the field in Elements of Psychophysics (1860). He wanted to measure the mind by finding lawful links between physical stimulus intensity, such as decibels of sound or grams of weight, and the strength of the sensation produced, such as loudness or heaviness. Building on Ernst Heinrich Weber's finding that the just noticeable difference is a constant fraction of the original stimulus (Weber's law), Fechner proposed that sensation grows with the logarithm of intensity (S = k log I). He also devised the classic methods for measuring thresholds: limits, constant stimuli and adjustment. Later, Stanley Smith Stevens offered a power law, and signal detection theory separated sensitivity from response bias. Everyday example: one candle lit in a dark room is obvious, but the same candle added to a brightly lit room goes unnoticed, because perceived brightness depends on the ratio of change, not the absolute amount. Option a is right because it states the textbook definition: the quantitative relation between physical stimuli and the sensations they produce. Exam trap: psychophysics is not \"psychology plus physics\", and the link between experience and brain processes belongs to physiological psychology, not psychophysics.", "topic": "Definition of psychophysics (Fechner)"}, {"q": "Convergence of the eyes is controlled primarily by:", "options": {"a": "Extrinsic muscle of the eye", "b": "Ciliary muscle of eye", "c": "Curvature of lens", "d": "Muscles of iris"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: convergence of the eyes is controlled primarily by the extrinsic (extraocular) muscles of the eye. Each eye is moved by six extraocular muscles attached to the outside of the eyeball: four recti (superior, inferior, medial and lateral) and two obliques. When you look at something close, such as a phone held near your face, both eyes rotate inward so that the image falls on corresponding points of both retinas. This inward turning is convergence, produced mainly by contraction of the two medial rectus muscles, which are supplied by the oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve III). Feedback about how far the eyes have turned also serves as a binocular depth cue: the more the eyes converge, the nearer the object. Convergence is one part of the near response (near triad), alongside accommodation of the lens and constriction of the pupil, and the wrong options in this question come from those other two parts. Option a is right because only the extraocular muscles physically rotate the eyeballs; the paper's \"extrinsic muscle\" means exactly these. Exam trap: accommodation and convergence both happen when you look at a near object, so students mix them up; convergence is the eyes turning (extrinsic muscles), while accommodation is the lens changing shape (the intrinsic ciliary muscle).", "topic": "Convergence and the extraocular muscles"}, {"q": "The primary emotion includes:", "options": {"a": "Pain, Fear, hate, love", "b": "Joy, fear, anger, sadness", "c": "Shame, guilt, anger, grief", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: joy, fear, anger and sadness are primary emotions. Primary (basic) emotions are thought to be innate, universal across cultures, present early in infancy, and linked to distinct facial expressions and survival functions. Secondary emotions are blends, or later emotions that depend on self-awareness and social learning. Theorists give slightly different lists, but joy, fear, anger and sadness appear on all of them. Paul Ekman and Wallace V. Friesen found six expressions recognised worldwide, even among the isolated Fore people of Papua New Guinea: happiness, sadness, fear, anger, surprise and disgust. Robert Plutchik's wheel of emotions (1980) has eight primaries in opposing pairs: joy and sadness, trust and disgust, fear and anger, surprise and anticipation. Michael Lewis's developmental research places these primary emotions within the first six months of life, while the self-conscious emotions come later: embarrassment, envy and empathy at about 18 to 24 months, once the child recognises itself, and pride, shame and guilt at about 2 and a half to 3 years, once the child has learned standards and rules. Example: a six-month-old shows clear anger when a toy is taken away but cannot yet feel guilty about breaking it. Option b is right because all four of its emotions are on every major list of primary emotions. Exam trap: shame and guilt sound basic but are self-conscious, secondary emotions (although Carroll Izard did include them among his ten fundamental emotions), and love and hate are complex sentiments, not primaries.", "topic": "Primary (basic) emotions"}, {"q": "An insufficient supply of thyroid hormone during pregnancy can result in:", "options": {"a": "Increased metabolic rate", "b": "Increased emotional arousal", "c": "Cretinism", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: an insufficient supply of thyroid hormone during pregnancy can result in cretinism. The thyroid gland in the neck makes thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), which need iodine and set the body's metabolic rate. Before and just after birth these hormones are also essential for brain growth, myelination and skeletal development. If the mother is severely iodine deficient, or the foetus's own thyroid fails (congenital hypothyroidism), the child develops cretinism: stunted growth, coarse facial features, a large tongue, delayed motor milestones and intellectual disability. Today the condition is called congenital hypothyroidism or congenital iodine deficiency syndrome, and it is one of the most preventable causes of intellectual disability; iodised salt programmes, including India's, and newborn screening with prompt thyroxine treatment largely prevent it. Example: a baby found at birth to have low thyroid hormone who starts daily thyroxine within the first weeks can develop normally, while an untreated child suffers permanent cognitive impairment. In adults, thyroid deficiency produces myxoedema, with sluggishness, weight gain, cold intolerance and depressed mood. Option c is right because cretinism is the classic developmental result of thyroid hormone deficiency in foetal and early life. Exam trap: excess thyroid hormone (hyperthyroidism, as in Graves' disease) raises metabolism and arousal, so options a and b describe the opposite condition, not deficiency.", "topic": "Thyroid deficiency and cretinism"}, {"q": "The so-called “master gland,” which helps control the activities of various ductless glands is the:", "options": {"a": "Adrenal medulla", "b": "Anterior pituitary", "c": "Thyroid", "d": "Endocrine glands"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the anterior pituitary is called the master gland. The pituitary is a pea-sized gland hanging by a stalk from the base of the brain, just below the hypothalamus. Its front part, the anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis), releases tropic hormones that switch on other endocrine (ductless) glands: thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) drives the thyroid, adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) drives the adrenal cortex, and follicle-stimulating and luteinising hormones (FSH and LH) drive the ovaries and testes. It also makes growth hormone and prolactin. Because so many glands wait for its signals, it earned the nickname master gland. The posterior pituitary, by contrast, only stores and releases oxytocin and vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone) made in the hypothalamus. Modern texts add a twist: the pituitary itself takes orders from the hypothalamus through releasing hormones, so the hypothalamus is sometimes called the master of the master gland. In stress, for example, the hypothalamus releases CRH, the anterior pituitary releases ACTH, and the adrenal cortex releases cortisol: the HPA axis. Option b is right because the anterior pituitary is the part whose tropic hormones control the other ductless glands. Exam trap: the adrenal medulla is famous for fight-or-flight hormones and the thyroid for metabolism, but neither controls other glands, and \"endocrine glands\" is just the name of the whole category.", "topic": "Anterior pituitary: the master gland"}, {"q": "One of the following is not a method of measuring retention:", "options": {"a": "Recognition", "b": "Recall", "c": "Relearning", "d": "Reminiscence"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: reminiscence is not a method of measuring retention. Retention is how much of what was learned is still stored, and psychologists measure it in three classic ways, each more sensitive than the last. Recall asks the person to reproduce the material with few or no cues, as in an essay answer or a fill-in-the-blank. Recognition asks the person to pick out the learned item from alternatives, as in a multiple-choice test or an identity parade, and usually shows more retention than recall. Relearning, the savings method, was devised by Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885): he learned lists of nonsense syllables, relearned them later and expressed the time saved as a percentage. Savings appear even when nothing can be recalled, so relearning is the most sensitive measure. Some texts add reconstruction (putting items back in their original order). Reminiscence, by contrast, is a phenomenon, not a method: it is the improvement in recall after a delay without any extra practice, first reported by Philip B. Ballard (1913) with schoolchildren who recalled more of a poem two days later than immediately after learning it. Option d is right because reminiscence is something retention measures can reveal, not a way of measuring retention. Exam trap: do not confuse it with reminiscence therapy for older adults or the reminiscence bump in autobiographical memory; none of these is a retention method.", "topic": "Methods of measuring retention"}, {"q": "When new acquisitions improve retention of the old, that is:", "options": {"a": "Positive transfer", "b": "Negative transfer", "c": "Reproductive facilitation", "d": "Reproductive interference"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: when new learning improves retention of the old, that is reproductive facilitation. The stem is taken almost word for word from David Krech and Richard S. Crutchfield's Elements of Psychology (1958): when new acquisitions improve retention of the old, the term reproductive facilitation is applied, and when the effect is negative, reproductive interference. Transfer of training is the effect that learning one task has on learning or remembering another. It is positive when one task helps the other, negative when it hinders, and zero when there is no effect. Edward L. Thorndike and Robert S. Woodworth (1901) explained transfer by identical elements: the more two tasks share, the more transfer occurs. Transfer can run forward or backward in time. Forward effects are proactive: old learning helping new learning (proactive facilitation) or hindering it (proactive interference). Backward effects are retroactive: new learning helping retention of the old (retroactive facilitation, which Krech and Crutchfield call reproductive facilitation) or hindering it (retroactive interference, their reproductive interference). Example: a student who takes up Spanish may find her school French improving, because the new learning rehearses shared vocabulary and grammar. Option c is right because it is the textbook name for exactly this effect: new learning helping the retention of old learning. Exam trap: positive transfer sounds right too, since the effect is helpful, but it is the broad name for any learning that helps other learning, usually earlier learning making new learning easier; when new learning acts back on the retention of old learning, the answer is reproductive (retroactive) facilitation. Key note: the coaching book keys a, positive transfer; c is used here because the stem is Krech and Crutchfield's own definition of reproductive facilitation.", "topic": "Reproductive (retroactive) facilitation"}, {"q": "Which of the following illnesses caused by long-term abuse of alcohol often involves profound retrograde amnesia?", "options": {"a": "Alzheimer's disease", "b": "Huntington's disease", "c": "Parkinson's disease", "d": "Korsakoff's disease"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: Korsakoff's disease (Korsakoff's syndrome) is the alcohol-related illness with profound retrograde amnesia. The Russian neuropsychiatrist Sergei Korsakoff described it in the 1880s in long-term heavy drinkers. Chronic alcohol abuse, with poor diet and impaired absorption, causes thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency. The acute phase is Wernicke's encephalopathy, described by Carl Wernicke, with confusion, ataxia and eye-movement problems (ophthalmoplegia, nystagmus). If thiamine is not given quickly it can progress to Korsakoff's syndrome, so the two are often named together as Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. Damage centres on the mammillary bodies and the mediodorsal nucleus of the thalamus. The memory picture is severe anterograde amnesia (inability to form new memories) plus extensive retrograde amnesia with a temporal gradient: recent decades are lost more than early life. Patients often confabulate, filling gaps with invented but sincere stories, and lack insight. Example: a man in his fifties insists he still works at a job he left years ago and cheerfully describes a meeting there \"this morning\". Immediate memory and general intelligence are relatively spared. Option d is right because Korsakoff's is the only option caused by alcohol abuse, and its retrograde amnesia is classically profound. Exam trap: Korsakoff's is not a dementia like Alzheimer's; it is an amnestic disorder caused by a vitamin deficiency, and early thiamine can prevent it.", "topic": "Korsakoff's syndrome and retrograde amnesia"}, {"q": "What did Chomsky not argue about language development?", "options": {"a": "There is a universal grammar", "b": "It is based on reinforced learning", "c": "It is genetically wired", "d": "There is an innate modular language."}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: Noam Chomsky did not argue that language development is based on reinforced learning. That was B. F. Skinner's position in Verbal Behavior (1957), which explained speech as operant behaviour shaped by reinforcement and imitation. Chomsky's 1959 review of that book attacked it and helped launch the cognitive revolution. Chomsky, an American linguist, argued that children are born with a language acquisition device (LAD), an innate, biologically wired capacity specialised for language. Within it lies universal grammar: principles shared by all human languages, such as nouns, verbs and rules for combining them, with experience simply setting the parameters of the local language. His key evidence is the poverty of the stimulus: children hear incomplete, error-filled speech, yet rapidly master complex grammar they were never taught or reinforced for. Children's errors point the same way: a three-year-old who says \"I goed\" or \"two mouses\" has never heard those forms, so the child must be applying an internal rule (overregularisation) rather than copying reinforced examples. Chomsky also treated language as a distinct mental faculty, separate from general intelligence, which fits the idea of an innate language module. Option b is right because reinforcement-based learning is the behaviourist view Chomsky rejected. Exam trap: in \"not\" questions students pick the least familiar wording; here the three Chomskyan options are just different phrasings of nativism, and only reinforcement belongs to Skinner.", "topic": "Chomsky's nativist view of language"}, {"q": "What are the two types of declarative memory given by Edel Tulving?", "options": {"a": "Episodic and semantic", "b": "Reconstructive and semantic", "c": "Semantic and procedural", "d": "Procedural and reconstructive"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Endel Tulving divided declarative memory into episodic and semantic memory. The paper spells his name \"Edel\"; the correct spelling is Endel Tulving, an Estonian-born Canadian psychologist who proposed the distinction in 1972. Episodic memory stores personal experiences tied to a specific time and place: what you ate at your birthday dinner, or where you were when exam results came out. Tulving linked it to autonoetic consciousness, the sense of mentally travelling back in time. Semantic memory stores general facts and knowledge, independent of when you learned them: that Delhi is India's capital, what a neuron is, the meanings of words. Both are declarative (explicit) memory, because their contents can be consciously recalled and put into words. Larry Squire's taxonomy places declarative memory opposite non-declarative (implicit) memory, which includes procedural skills like riding a bicycle, priming and classical conditioning. The distinction matters clinically: after hippocampal damage and in early Alzheimer's disease, episodic memory fails first while much semantic knowledge survives, and the patient H.M. could not form new episodic memories yet still learned new motor skills such as mirror drawing. Option a is right because episodic and semantic are exactly the two kinds Tulving named. Exam trap: procedural memory is long-term but not declarative; it is the classic implicit memory, so any option pairing it with semantic memory is wrong.", "topic": "Tulving's episodic and semantic memory"}, {"q": "Which is not the component of Kluver-Bucy syndrome following the removal of anterior temporal lobes?", "options": {"a": "Rejection of anything that is edible", "b": "Lack of fear", "c": "A tendency to investigate objects with the mouth.", "d": "Increased sexual activity often directed at inappropriate objects"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: rejection of anything edible is not a component of Kluver-Bucy syndrome; in fact the opposite happens. Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy (the paper writes \"Kluver\" without the umlaut) removed both anterior temporal lobes, including the amygdala, in rhesus monkeys in the late 1930s. The monkeys showed a striking cluster of changes. They became tame and lost their fear, approaching snakes and humans they would normally avoid, because the amygdala is central to fear. They showed hyperorality, putting every object in the mouth to explore it. They had psychic blindness (visual agnosia): they could see objects but not recognise what they meant. They showed hypermetamorphosis, compulsively attending to every new stimulus, and hypersexuality, sometimes directed at inappropriate objects or other species. Their diet changed: they ate indiscriminately, including meat and non-food objects that normal monkeys reject. In humans, a partial form appears after herpes simplex encephalitis, head injury or frontotemporal dementia, with placid emotion, mouthing of objects, overeating and disinhibited sexual behaviour. Option a is right because the syndrome brings increased, indiscriminate eating and mouthing of objects, not refusal of food. Exam trap: in this \"not\" question three options are genuine features, and the false one simply reverses the dietary change: the animals eat anything rather than reject food.", "topic": "Klüver-Bucy syndrome features"}, {"q": "Arrange the following tests in increasing order of their saturation on projective criteria.<br>1. Thematic Apperception test<br>2. Differential Aptitude Test<br>3. Rorschach Inkblot Test<br>4. Incomplete Sentence Blank", "options": {"a": "3-1-2-4", "b": "3-1-4-2", "c": "2-4-3-1", "d": "2-4-1-3"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: 2-4-1-3, that is, Differential Aptitude Test, Incomplete Sentence Blank, Thematic Apperception Test, Rorschach. \"Saturation on projective criteria\" means how strongly a test has the features of a projective technique: an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus, minimal instructions, freedom of response and a disguised purpose. The projective hypothesis holds that the less structured the stimulus, the more people reveal their own needs, conflicts and ways of seeing. The Differential Aptitude Tests (George K. Bennett, Harold G. Seashore and Alexander G. Wesman, 1947) are objective, structured ability tests with right and wrong answers, so they have essentially no projective saturation. The Rotter Incomplete Sentences Blank (Julian Rotter and Janet Rafferty, 1950; the paper calls it the Incomplete Sentence Blank) gives the start of a sentence such as \"My father...\", so the response is partly guided and the purpose fairly transparent: a semi-structured projective. The Thematic Apperception Test (Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, 1935) shows ambiguous pictures of people and asks for a story, leaving much more room. The Rorschach (Hermann Rorschach, 1921) uses ten inkblots that depict nothing at all, making it the least structured and most projective. Option d is right because it runs from no projection to maximal projection. Exam trap: \"increasing order\" means least to most; option b is the correct sequence reversed.", "topic": "Projective tests ordered by structure"}, {"q": "Biofeedback therapy is based on which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Classical conditioning", "b": "Operant conditioning", "c": "Observational learning", "d": "Cognitive learning"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: biofeedback therapy is based on operant conditioning. In biofeedback, a device measures a bodily process we normally cannot sense, such as muscle tension (EMG), skin temperature, heart rate, blood pressure or brain waves (EEG), and turns it into an immediate signal: a tone, a light or a moving line on a screen. When the person shifts the process in the desired direction, the signal changes, and that change acts as a reinforcer. Over sessions the person learns to produce the change voluntarily, and eventually without the machine. The approach grew from the work of Neal E. Miller in the 1960s, who reported that rats could learn to change heart rate and other autonomic responses when those changes were rewarded, challenging the belief that only voluntary skeletal responses could be operantly conditioned (later replications were weaker, but clinical biofeedback continued). Clinical uses include EMG biofeedback for tension headache, thermal (hand-warming) biofeedback for migraine and Raynaud's disease, and EEG neurofeedback for ADHD. Example: a stressed student watches the tension reading from her forehead fall as she relaxes, and the falling tone rewards her success. Option b is right because the response (the physiological change) is shaped by its consequence, the feedback signal. Exam trap: because biofeedback works on involuntary, autonomic responses, students assume classical conditioning; but nothing is being paired, and the response is reinforced after it occurs.", "topic": "Biofeedback and operant conditioning"}, {"q": "According to Sigmund Freud, different types of anxiety are:<br>1. Neurotic Anxiety<br>2. State Anxiety<br>3. Moral Anxiety<br>4. Reality Anxiety", "options": {"a": "1, 2 and 3 only", "b": "1, 3 and 4 only", "c": "1, 2 and 4 only", "d": "2, 3 and 4 only"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: 1, 3 and 4 only; Sigmund Freud described neurotic, moral and reality anxiety. In his structural model the ego must serve three masters: the outside world, the id and the superego. Anxiety, which Freud in Inhibitions, Symptoms and Anxiety (1926) treated as the ego's danger signal, arises when any one of them threatens the ego, so there are three types. Reality (objective) anxiety is fear of a real danger in the outside world, such as a snarling dog or a speeding truck; it is useful and fades when the threat passes. Neurotic anxiety arises from conflict between the id and the ego: the unconscious fear that one's sexual or aggressive impulses will break through and bring punishment. Moral anxiety arises from conflict between the ego and the superego: guilt or shame at doing, or even thinking of, something that violates one's conscience. Example: a student who has cheated feels moral anxiety, while one who becomes tense without knowing why whenever angry at a parent may be showing neurotic anxiety. Anxiety then sets defence mechanisms such as repression in motion. Option b is right because neurotic, moral and reality anxiety are the three Freud named. Exam trap: state anxiety comes from Charles Spielberger's state-trait theory and his State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (STAI); it is not Freudian, so any option containing 2 is wrong.", "topic": "Freud's three types of anxiety"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a cause of forgetting?", "options": {"a": "Interference", "b": "Selective attention", "c": "Decay", "d": "Retrieval inhibition"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: selective attention is not a cause of forgetting. Forgetting is the loss of, or inability to retrieve, information that was once stored, and the main explanations are these. Decay theory, traced to Edward L. Thorndike's law of disuse, holds that memory traces fade with time if unused. Interference theory holds that other learning gets in the way: proactive interference when old learning disrupts new, and retroactive interference when new learning disrupts old. Retrieval failure, studied by Endel Tulving, holds that the memory is still there but the right cue is missing. Retrieval inhibition, or retrieval-induced forgetting, shown by Michael C. Anderson, Robert A. Bjork and Elizabeth Ligon Bjork (1994), holds that practising some items suppresses related ones. Freud's motivated forgetting (repression) and consolidation failure complete the usual list. Selective attention, studied by Donald Broadbent with his filter model (1958), is an encoding process: choosing which input to process and ignoring the rest. Information you never attended to was never stored, so it cannot be forgotten. Option b is right because selective attention operates before storage, not on stored memories. Exam trap: some texts list \"encoding failure\" as a reason we cannot remember, as when you cannot draw a coin you have handled thousands of times; that is a failure to store, and selective attention itself is not a theory of forgetting.", "topic": "Theories of forgetting"}, {"q": "Awareness of one's own thought processes enabling effective learning through correction is called:", "options": {"a": "Algorithm", "b": "Metacognition", "c": "Analogies", "d": "Constructive process"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: awareness of one's own thought processes, used to learn more effectively by correcting oneself, is metacognition. The American developmental psychologist John H. Flavell introduced the term in the 1970s (key paper 1979), describing it as \"thinking about thinking\". It has two parts. Metacognitive knowledge is what you know about your own mind, tasks and strategies: for example, that you remember diagrams better than lists. Metacognitive regulation is the active use of that knowledge: planning how to study, monitoring whether you understand, and correcting your approach when you do not. Its memory part, metamemory, includes the feeling of knowing and judging how well you have learned something. Metacognition improves through childhood and adolescence and predicts academic success. Example: while preparing for an entrance exam, you notice you keep getting psychophysics questions wrong, realise that rereading notes is not working, and switch to self-testing with past papers; noticing and fixing the problem is metacognition in action. In clinical work, Adrian Wells's metacognitive therapy targets beliefs about worrying itself, such as \"worry keeps me safe\". Option b is right because the question describes metacognition exactly: monitoring and correcting one's own thinking. Exam trap: metacognition is not ordinary cognition or general intelligence; \"meta\" means one level up, so it is cognition about cognition.", "topic": "Flavell's concept of metacognition"}, {"q": "How many subjects would be required in a 3 × 3×2 factorial design with ten subjects per cell?", "options": {"a": "10", "b": "90", "c": "18", "d": "180"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: 180 subjects. A factorial design studies two or more independent variables (factors) at once, crossing every level of each factor with every level of the others. The notation tells you the structure: each number is one factor, and its value is that factor's number of levels. So 3 × 3 × 2 means three factors, two with three levels each and one with two levels. The number of cells (conditions) is the product of the levels: 3 × 3 × 2 = 18. With ten subjects in each cell, the total is 18 × 10 = 180. Example: a researcher studies anxiety reduction by therapy type (CBT, mindfulness, waiting list), session frequency (weekly, twice weekly, daily) and gender (male, female); that gives 18 combinations, and with ten participants in each, 180 people are needed. Factorial designs, analysed with factorial analysis of variance (ANOVA), are efficient because one study tests every main effect and every interaction. Option d is right because you multiply the levels to get the cells and then multiply by the subjects per cell. Exam trap: students add the levels (3 + 3 + 2 = 8) or stop at the number of cells (18, option c). The calculation assumes a between-subjects design, the standard reading, in which each participant serves in only one cell.", "topic": "Factorial design sample size"}, {"q": "In an experiment, if a light is repeatedly presented just before the electric shock is given the animal learns:", "options": {"a": "Operant escape and then operant avoidance", "b": "Only operant escape", "c": "Operant avoidance and then operant escape", "d": "Only operant avoidance"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the animal learns operant escape first and then operant avoidance. This is the classic signalled (discriminated) avoidance experiment, usually run in a shuttle box with two compartments. A warning light comes on, and a few seconds later the floor delivers a shock. On early trials the animal ignores the light; when the shock arrives, it scrambles over the barrier, and that response ends the shock. This is escape learning: the response terminates an aversive stimulus already present and is strengthened by negative reinforcement. After repeated pairings the animal starts crossing as soon as the light comes on, before the shock begins. This is avoidance learning: the response prevents the aversive stimulus from arriving at all. Richard L. Solomon and Lyman C. Wynne (1953) showed that dogs trained this way kept avoiding for hundreds of trials after the shock was switched off. Orval Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory explains why: through classical conditioning the light comes to evoke fear, and through operant conditioning the jump is reinforced by the reduction of that fear. Clinically, this is why phobic avoidance persists: the person never stays long enough to learn that the feared outcome does not happen. Option a is right because escape always develops before avoidance. Exam trap: you cannot avoid a shock you have not first learned to escape, so reversed orders and avoidance-only options are wrong.", "topic": "Escape then avoidance learning"}, {"q": "The contention that our facial expressions influence our emotional experience attests to which of the following hypotheses?", "options": {"a": "Universality hypothesis of facial expressions", "b": "Emotional specificity hypothesis of ANS activity", "c": "Emotional generality hypothesis of ANS activity", "d": "Facial feedback hypothesis"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the facial feedback hypothesis. This idea says the face does not just display an emotion; feedback from the facial muscles to the brain also helps create or strengthen the feeling. Charles Darwin and William James hinted at it in the nineteenth century, and Silvan Tomkins and Carroll Izard developed it in the 1960s and 1970s. In a well-known 1988 study, Fritz Strack, Leonard Martin and Sabine Stepper had people hold a pen between their teeth, which forces a smile, or between their lips, which blocks one; the 'smilers' rated cartoons as funnier. Paul Ekman's lab similarly found that people told to move particular facial muscles, without being told which emotion they were posing, showed autonomic changes and reported a trace of that emotion. Everyday example: forcing a smile at a tiring party can make you feel slightly better. A 17-lab replication (Wagenmakers and colleagues, 2016) found no reliable pen effect, but a 2019 meta-analysis found small facial feedback effects overall and the Many Smiles Collaboration (2022) found that posed smiles do raise happiness a little, so the effect is real but small. Option d is right because the stem says our expressions influence our emotional experience, which is exactly the direction this hypothesis proposes. Exam trap: students pick universality because it also concerns faces, but universality is about expressions being produced and recognised the same way across cultures, not about faces changing feelings.", "topic": "Facial feedback hypothesis of emotion"}, {"q": "We sometimes attempt to solve problems by applying techniques that worked in similar situations in the past. What type of method are we using?", "options": {"a": "Trial and error", "b": "Algorithms", "c": "Analogy", "d": "Heuristics"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: analogy, using a solution that worked for a similar problem in the past. Textbooks such as Baron and Ciccarelli group problem-solving strategies into trial and error, algorithms, heuristics and analogies. An analogy maps the structure of an old, solved problem onto a new one. The classic research is by Mary Gick and Keith Holyoak (1980), who used Karl Duncker's radiation problem: a tumour must be destroyed by rays that also harm healthy tissue at full strength. People who had first read a story about a general who split his army into small groups converging on a fortress were more likely to find the solution of many weak rays converging on the tumour, especially when hinted to use the story. Everyday example: having fixed a jammed printer by reseating its cartridge, you try reseating the toner when the photocopier jams. Clinically, a therapist may help a client apply the coping plan that worked for exam anxiety to a coming job interview. Option c is right because the stem describes transferring a past technique to a similar situation, which is the definition of reasoning by analogy. Exam trap: many students choose heuristics because analogy is sometimes listed as one kind of heuristic; when analogy is offered as a separate option, it is the precise answer.", "topic": "Analogy in problem solving"}, {"q": "The fact that a nearer object may obscure a more distant object from the view is due to:", "options": {"a": "Figure and ground", "b": "Interposition", "c": "Proximity", "d": "Closure"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: interposition, also called overlap or occlusion. Interposition is a monocular depth cue: when one object partly covers another, we see the covering object as nearer. It is one of the pictorial cues that painters have used for centuries, along with linear perspective, relative size, texture gradient, aerial perspective, shading and height in the visual field. Because it needs only one eye, it works in photographs and paintings. It tells us only relative order (which object is in front), not absolute distance. Everyday example: at a cricket match, a spectator's head blocks part of the scoreboard, so you instantly know the head is closer without judging any distance in metres. In a class photo, the students whose bodies cut off others look nearer. Hermann von Helmholtz treated such cues as the basis of unconscious inference about depth, whereas James J. Gibson argued that the information lies directly in the light reaching the eye. Binocular cues, by contrast, need two eyes: retinal disparity and convergence. Option b is right because the stem describes a nearer object hiding part of a farther one, which is the exact definition of interposition. Exam trap: students confuse it with figure and ground or closure because these are also perceptual terms, but those are Gestalt principles of organising shapes, not cues for depth.", "topic": "Interposition as a depth cue"}, {"q": "In a 3 × 4 contingency table, using the chi-square test, the degrees of freedom associated with the chi-square will be:", "options": {"a": "12", "b": "7", "c": "9", "d": "6"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: 6 degrees of freedom. The chi-square test was introduced by Karl Pearson in 1900 to compare observed frequencies with the frequencies expected if the null hypothesis is true. In a contingency table, the test of independence asks whether two categorical variables are related, for example diagnosis (three categories) and treatment setting (four categories). Degrees of freedom (df) are the number of cell values that are free to vary once the row and column totals are fixed. For a contingency table the formula is df = (r - 1)(c - 1), where r is the number of rows and c the number of columns. Here, (3 - 1)(4 - 1) = 2 × 3 = 6. Intuition: if all row and column totals are known, you can fill only 2 cells in each of the first 3 columns freely; every remaining cell is then fixed by the totals. The df matter because the critical value depends on them: at the .05 level, chi-square needs to exceed 12.59 when df is 6. Option d is right because applying (r - 1)(c - 1) to a 3 × 4 table gives 6. Exam trap: students multiply rows by columns (12, the number of cells), add them (7), or subtract one from only one dimension (9), instead of subtracting one from each dimension before multiplying.", "topic": "Chi-square degrees of freedom"}, {"q": "Why do individuals with a high need for achievement quit early when faced by a very difficult task?", "options": {"a": "Get tired easily", "b": "Have high anxiety", "c": "Have poor perseverance", "d": "Have high need for efficiency"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: they have a high need for efficiency, meaning they want their effort to pay off. David C. McClelland defined the need for achievement (nAch) as a concern with doing things well and better than before. John William Atkinson's 1957 risk-taking model explains task choice: the tendency to approach success equals motive to succeed × probability of success (Ps) × incentive value (1 - Ps). The product peaks when Ps is about 0.5, so achievers prefer moderately difficult tasks where success is realistic and clearly due to their own skill. On a very difficult task Ps is tiny, so the expected return on effort is low, and Norman Feather's 1961 persistence study found that achievement-oriented people gave up sooner on a task described as very hard than on one described as easy. Example: an ambitious student drops an impossible extra-credit puzzle and puts the time into a challenging but winnable project. Option d is right because, of the four choices, only efficiency (spending effort where it can realistically succeed) matches McClelland's and Atkinson's account. Exam trap: high anxiety and poor perseverance describe people high in fear of failure, who oddly pick very easy or very hard tasks. Key note: 'need for efficiency' is not textbook wording; d is keyed as the best fit, and the real reason is the very low probability of success.", "topic": "Achievement motivation and task difficulty"}, {"q": "A man reflects his anger from his boss to his child, is an example of", "options": {"a": "Projection", "b": "Displacement", "c": "Reaction formation", "d": "Regression"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: displacement. Displacement is an ego defence mechanism, first described by Sigmund Freud and systematised by his daughter Anna Freud in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936). The ego redirects an impulse, usually anger or sexual desire, from the person who aroused it to a safer, less threatening substitute. The original target is dangerous to attack (a boss who could sack you, a parent you depend on), so the energy is discharged on someone weaker or on an object. The man in the stem cannot shout at his boss, so he snaps at his child at home: the classic 'kick the dog' chain, in which the child may in turn kick the dog. Other examples are slamming a door after an argument, or a student scolded by a teacher who then bullies a younger sibling. Freud also explained phobias this way: in the case of Little Hans, fear of the father was displaced onto horses. Scapegoating of minority groups is displacement at the social level. Option b is right because the feeling stays the same (anger) while only its target changes, from the boss to the child. Exam trap: students pick projection, but projection means attributing your own unacceptable feeling to someone else ('my boss hates me'), whereas displacement means expressing your own feeling towards a different target.", "topic": "Displacement as a defence mechanism"}, {"q": "The visual signals from each retina reach striate cortex via which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Lateral geniculate nucleus", "b": "Medical geniculate nucleus", "c": "Infero-temporal cortex", "d": "Posterior parietal cortex"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) of the thalamus. The visual pathway runs from the photoreceptors to bipolar cells to retinal ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve. At the optic chiasm, fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross, so each side of the brain receives information about the opposite visual field. These fibres continue as the optic tract, and most of them synapse in the LGN, which has six layers: two magnocellular layers (motion and coarse form) and four parvocellular layers (colour and fine detail). From the LGN, the optic radiations carry signals to the primary visual cortex in the occipital lobe, called striate cortex (V1, Brodmann area 17) because of a visible stripe of myelinated fibres. John P. J. Pinel calls this the retina-geniculate-striate pathway. Clinically, a stroke damaging the LGN or the optic radiations causes loss of the opposite half of the visual field in both eyes (a homonymous defect). Option a is right because the LGN is the thalamic relay through which signals from each retina reach striate cortex. Exam trap: the medial geniculate nucleus is the thalamic relay for hearing, not vision; remember 'lateral for light, medial for music'. The paper prints 'Medical geniculate', a misspelling of medial geniculate.", "topic": "Lateral geniculate nucleus and vision"}, {"q": "When stress is prolonged, the most likely result is", "options": {"a": "generalized weakening of the immune system", "b": "damage to brain areas with within the pons and medulla", "c": "an increase in the level of naturally produced endorphins", "d": "intensification of the effects of natural killer cells"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: a generalised weakening of the immune system. Hans Selye's general adaptation syndrome (1936) describes three stages: alarm, resistance and exhaustion. Selye's chronically stressed rats showed enlarged adrenal glands, ulcers and shrunken immune tissue. In prolonged stress the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis keeps releasing cortisol, and sustained cortisol suppresses immune activity: fewer and less active lymphocytes, lower natural killer cell activity and slower wound healing. The field that studies these links is psychoneuroimmunology, founded when Robert Ader and Nicholas Cohen (1975) conditioned immune suppression in rats. A meta-analysis by Suzanne Segerstrom and Gregory Miller (2004) found that brief stressors can briefly boost some natural immune responses, but chronic stressors such as caring for a relative with dementia, unemployment or long-term marital conflict suppress immunity broadly. Janice Kiecolt-Glaser showed that dementia caregivers' wounds healed more slowly and that medical students had weaker immune measures during examinations. Everyday example: many students catch a cold just after a long, stressful exam season. Option a is right because the best-supported effect of prolonged stress is broad immune suppression, which makes infections and slower recovery more likely. Exam trap: students pick d because acute stress can briefly mobilise natural killer cells, but prolonged stress reduces their activity rather than intensifying it; and endorphin release (option c) is a short-term stress response that numbs pain, not the typical outcome of chronic stress.", "topic": "Chronic stress and immune suppression"}, {"q": "A 40-item Vocabulary test was administered to a group of students. A second similar test of vocabulary terms was administered to this same group of students approximately one week later. The researcher reported that the correlation between these two tests was r = 0.90. What type of reliability is represented in this example?", "options": {"a": "Test retest", "b": "Internal consistency", "c": "Alternate forms", "d": "Split half"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: alternate (parallel) forms reliability. Reliability is the consistency of test scores. Classical test theory, built on the work of Charles Spearman, treats each observed score as a true score plus error, and a reliability coefficient estimates how much of the score variation is true rather than error. Alternate-forms reliability uses two equivalent versions of a test, with different items sampling the same content at the same difficulty, given to the same people; the correlation between the forms is called the coefficient of equivalence. When the second form is given after a gap, as here (one week), Anne Anastasi calls it delayed alternate-form reliability, which reflects both content-sampling error and changes over time. In the stem, a 40-item vocabulary test is followed a week later by a 'second similar test' of vocabulary, and the two correlate 0.90, showing that both forms rank the students consistently. Clinically, parallel forms are used when a patient must be retested soon, as the original Wechsler Memory Scale offered two forms, so that remembering the items does not inflate the second score. Option c is right because the key clue is a different but similar test, not the same test repeated. Exam trap: the one-week gap tempts students to choose test-retest, but test-retest requires giving the identical test twice; a new equivalent form makes it alternate forms.", "topic": "Alternate forms reliability"}, {"q": "The idea that people cope with stress by moving towards people, away from people or against people is most consistent with the views of", "options": {"a": "Aron Beck", "b": "Carl Rogers", "c": "Karen Horney", "d": "Erik Erikson"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Karen Horney. Karen Horney, a German-born psychoanalyst who later worked in the United States, broke with Sigmund Freud's emphasis on instincts and stressed social and cultural forces. She proposed that basic anxiety, a child's feeling of being isolated and helpless in a potentially hostile world, grows out of parental indifference, inconsistency or domination. To cope with it, people develop neurotic needs, which she grouped into three trends in Our Inner Conflicts (1945). Moving toward people is the compliant type, who seeks love and approval ('if you love me, you will not hurt me'). Moving against people is the aggressive type, who seeks power and control ('if I have power, no one can hurt me'). Moving away from people is the detached type, who seeks independence and privacy ('if I withdraw, nothing can hurt me'). Healthy people use all three flexibly; the neurotic person relies rigidly on one. Example: a student who agrees with everyone to avoid any conflict is moving toward people. Option c is right because moving toward, against and away from people are Horney's three neurotic trends. Exam trap: the paper prints 'Aron Beck', a misspelling of Aaron T. Beck, the founder of cognitive therapy; his cognitive triad also has three parts (self, world and future), which tempts students, but it describes depressive thinking, not interpersonal coping styles.", "topic": "Horney's three neurotic trends"}, {"q": "Alexander Thomas and Stella Chase described three categories of infants: easy, difficult and slow to warm up. These are categories of", "options": {"a": "Play", "b": "Toilet Training", "c": "Emotion", "d": "Temperament"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: temperament. Temperament is the biologically based, early-appearing style of responding to the world: activity level, mood, adaptability and reactivity. The psychiatrists Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess (the paper misspells her name as 'Chase') began the New York Longitudinal Study in 1956, following a group of children from infancy into adulthood. They rated nine dimensions: activity level, rhythmicity, approach or withdrawal, adaptability, intensity of reaction, threshold of responsiveness, quality of mood, distractibility, and attention span with persistence. From these they described three types. Easy children (about 40 percent) are regular, cheerful and adapt quickly. Difficult children (about 10 percent) are irregular, react intensely and negatively, and adapt slowly. Slow-to-warm-up children (about 15 percent) are mildly negative and inactive at first but adapt gradually. Their key idea was goodness of fit: outcomes depend on how well parenting matches the child's temperament, so a patient, consistent parent can help a difficult child adjust well. Example: a slow-to-warm-up toddler clings to her parent at a new playgroup but joins in after a few visits. Option d is right because easy, difficult and slow-to-warm-up are the temperament categories of the New York Longitudinal Study. Exam trap: students pick emotion because the labels sound like moods, but they describe stable behavioural styles, not single emotions; do not confuse them with Mary Ainsworth's attachment types either.", "topic": "Thomas and Chess temperament types"}, {"q": "Which of the following types of cognitive abilities is LEAST likely to show a decline in late life?", "options": {"a": "Working memory", "b": "Processing speed", "c": "Fluid intelligence", "d": "Semantic memory"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: semantic memory. Endel Tulving (1972) divided long-term declarative memory into episodic memory, for personal events tied to a time and place (what you ate at a wedding last month), and semantic memory, for general facts, word meanings and knowledge (that Delhi is India's capital). Research on ageing, summarised in lifespan texts such as Santrock's, shows a clear pattern. Working memory, the ability to hold and manipulate information briefly, declines with age. Processing speed slows steadily from early adulthood, and Timothy Salthouse's processing-speed theory argues that this slowing explains much of the other age-related loss. Fluid intelligence, the ability to solve novel problems, also declines. Semantic memory, by contrast, is largely preserved: vocabulary and general knowledge stay stable or even grow into the seventies. Its main weakness in old age is retrieval, such as more frequent tip-of-the-tongue states for names, rather than loss of the knowledge itself. Procedural memory, such as cycling or typing, is also well preserved. Example: a 75-year-old grandfather may be slow to learn a new phone app but can still explain rare words and recall history accurately. Option d is right because semantic memory is the most stable of the four abilities listed. Exam trap: students treat all memory as declining with age, but the decline falls mainly on episodic and working memory, while semantic and procedural memory hold up.", "topic": "Cognitive ageing and semantic memory"}, {"q": "Which of the following is an atypical developmental pattern characterised by stereotyped motor response and poor communication skills?", "options": {"a": "Tardive Dyskinesia", "b": "Down Syndrome", "c": "Autism Spectrum Disorder", "d": "Attention Deficit/ Hyperactivity Disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: autism spectrum disorder (ASD). Leo Kanner described 'early infantile autism' in 1943, and Hans Asperger described a related group of children in 1944. DSM-5 (2013) merged autistic disorder, Asperger's disorder, childhood disintegrative disorder and pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified into one neurodevelopmental diagnosis, ASD, retained in DSM-5-TR. It has two core domains. The first is persistent deficits in social communication and interaction: poor social-emotional reciprocity, limited eye contact and gesture, and difficulty forming relationships; many children also have delayed or absent speech, echolalia or an unusual tone of voice. The second is restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests or activities: stereotyped motor movements such as hand flapping, rocking or spinning objects, insistence on sameness, intense narrow interests and unusual sensory responses. Symptoms appear in the early developmental period. Example: a three-year-old who does not respond to his name, lines up toy cars for hours and flaps his hands when excited would be referred for ASD assessment, which in India often uses the ISAA or CARS. Option c is right because stereotyped motor responses plus poor communication are exactly ASD's two diagnostic domains. Exam trap: tardive dyskinesia also involves repetitive movements, but it is a drug side effect in adults, not a developmental pattern; and ADHD involves restlessness and impulsivity rather than stereotypies and social-communication deficits.", "topic": "Autism spectrum disorder core features"}, {"q": "Which theorist revised Sigmund Freud's stages of psychosexual development, replacing it with psychosocial stages?", "options": {"a": "Karen Horneys", "b": "Erik Erikson", "c": "Alfred Adler", "d": "Viktor Frankl"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: Erik Erikson. Erik H. Erikson, a German-born psychoanalyst who was analysed by Anna Freud in Vienna, accepted much of Sigmund Freud's theory but shifted the focus from sexual drives to the ego and social relationships. In Childhood and Society (1950) he replaced Freud's five psychosexual stages (oral, anal, phallic, latency and genital) with eight psychosocial stages that span the whole lifespan. Each stage is a crisis between two poles: trust versus mistrust (infancy), autonomy versus shame and doubt, initiative versus guilt, industry versus inferiority, identity versus role confusion (adolescence), intimacy versus isolation, generativity versus stagnation, and integrity versus despair (old age). Resolving each crisis yields a virtue, such as hope from trust. The early stages map onto Freud's: trust versus mistrust parallels the oral stage, and autonomy parallels the anal stage. Unlike Freud, Erikson argued that personality keeps developing after adolescence. Example: a teenager trying out different career plans and friend groups is working through identity versus role confusion. Option b is right because Erikson is the theorist who recast the psychosexual stages as psychosocial ones. Exam trap: other neo-Freudians, such as Karen Horney (misspelt 'Karen Horneys' in the paper) and Alfred Adler, also stressed social factors, but neither built a stage theory; only Erikson's name goes with 'psychosocial stages'.", "topic": "Erikson's psychosocial stages"}, {"q": "Rapid Eye Movement is most closely associated with which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Alpha Waves", "b": "Delta Waves", "c": "Dreaming", "d": "Apnea"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: dreaming. In 1953 Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman at the University of Chicago noticed periods of rapid, darting eye movements during sleep; people woken during these periods usually reported vivid dreams, far more often than when woken from other stages. William Dement extended the work. REM sleep is called paradoxical sleep because the EEG shows fast, low-amplitude, desynchronised activity resembling wakefulness, with characteristic sawtooth waves, yet the body's muscles are almost paralysed (atonia) apart from the eyes and breathing muscles. Heart rate and breathing become irregular. REM periods recur in cycles of about 90 minutes and get longer towards morning, so most REM sleep falls late in the night; adults spend about 20 to 25 percent of sleep in REM. REM dreams are story-like, emotional and bizarre, while non-REM reports tend to be brief and thought-like. Example: when your alarm rings near dawn, you often wake in the middle of a vivid dream because REM periods are longest then. Clinically, in REM sleep behaviour disorder the atonia fails and people act out their dreams. Option c is right because REM is the stage most closely associated with vivid dreaming. Exam trap: students choose alpha waves because the REM EEG looks wakeful, but alpha belongs to relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed; REM shows fast, beta-like activity and sawtooth waves.", "topic": "REM sleep and dreaming"}, {"q": "If a psychological disorder has a genetic basis, one would expect to find the highest concordance rate for the illness between which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Mother and child", "b": "Father and child", "c": "Identical twins", "d": "Fraternal twins"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: identical twins. A concordance rate is the percentage of pairs of relatives in which, if one member has a disorder, the other also has it. Identical (monozygotic, MZ) twins come from one fertilised egg that splits, so they share essentially all their genes. Fraternal (dizygotic, DZ) twins come from two eggs and share about 50 percent of their genes on average, the same as ordinary siblings and the same as a parent and child. If genes contribute to a disorder, concordance should rise with genetic similarity, so MZ concordance should exceed DZ concordance. The twin method goes back to Francis Galton in the 1870s. Irving Gottesman's summary of schizophrenia studies is the classic example: lifetime risk is about 1 percent in the general population, about 13 percent for the child of one affected parent, about 17 percent for a DZ co-twin and about 48 percent for an MZ co-twin. Bipolar disorder and autism show similar MZ advantages. Because MZ concordance is never 100 percent, the environment must also matter, which supports the diathesis-stress model. Option c is right because identical twins share the most genes and so show the highest concordance for a genetically influenced disorder. Exam trap: fraternal twins share a womb and an upbringing, but genetically they are no closer than ordinary siblings, so they rank below identical twins.", "topic": "Twin concordance and heritability"}, {"q": "According to Sigmund Freud, a child who grabs food from another child because of hunger is driven by", "options": {"a": "The id", "b": "The ego", "c": "Environmental reinforcers", "d": "The superego"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: the id. In The Ego and the Id (1923), Sigmund Freud described personality as three interacting systems. The id is present at birth, wholly unconscious and the reservoir of instinctual energy (sexual and aggressive drives). It runs on the pleasure principle, demanding immediate gratification and the release of tension without regard for logic, morality or reality. The ego develops from the id to deal with the outside world; it follows the reality principle, finding realistic ways to meet needs. The superego, formed around age five or six as the child internalises parental standards while resolving the Oedipus complex, contains the conscience and the ego ideal. A child who snatches food from another because he is hungry is acting on raw biological need with no thought of consequences or fairness, which is pure id. If the ego were in charge, he might ask for some food or wait for snack time; if the superego dominated, he would feel guilty at the very thought of taking another child's food. Option a is right because grabbing food to satisfy hunger at once is driven by the id's pleasure principle. Exam trap: some choose the ego because grabbing is an action in the real world, but the ego would delay or find a realistic, acceptable way; impulsive, immediate gratification is always the id.", "topic": "Freud's id and the pleasure principle"}, {"q": "Which memory store is believed to have the largest capacity?", "options": {"a": "The sensory store", "b": "The short-term store", "c": "The long-term store", "d": "The phonological loop"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the long-term store. Richard C. Atkinson and Richard M. Shiffrin's multi-store (modal) model (1968) describes three stores. The sensory store holds a brief, raw copy of input: iconic memory for vision lasts under a second, as George Sperling's partial-report experiments (1960) showed, and echoic memory for sound lasts a few seconds. It fades almost at once. Attended information moves to the short-term store, which holds about seven plus or minus two chunks (George A. Miller, 1956) for roughly 15 to 30 seconds without rehearsal. Rehearsed and meaningfully encoded information passes into the long-term store, which is thought to have essentially unlimited capacity and can hold information for a lifetime, such as your mother tongue, your school address or how to ride a bicycle. No one has found a point at which long-term memory is 'full'; forgetting there comes mainly from interference and retrieval failure, not lack of space. Example: you can keep learning new words and faces all your life without old ones being pushed out. Option c is right because, of the stores listed, long-term memory has the largest, practically limitless capacity. Exam trap: some questions say 'largest capacity but shortest duration', which points to sensory memory, but 'largest capacity' alone points to long-term memory. The phonological loop is only a small part of Alan Baddeley's working memory.", "topic": "Long-term memory capacity"}, {"q": "The inability to recognize a once-familiar face, while still being able to accurately describe elements of the face, is a characteristic symptom of", "options": {"a": "amnesia", "b": "blindsight", "c": "prosopagnosia", "d": "sensory neglect"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: prosopagnosia, often called face blindness. Prosopagnosia is a visual agnosia, a failure to recognise familiar stimuli despite intact basic vision and intellect. People with it can see a face clearly, describe its parts (a long nose, grey eyes, a moustache) and even match two photographs, yet cannot tell whose face it is, sometimes not even a spouse's or their own in a mirror. They usually recognise people by voice, gait, hairstyle or clothing instead. The German neurologist Joachim Bodamer coined the term in 1947 from the Greek prosopon (face) and agnosia (not knowing). Acquired prosopagnosia usually follows damage to the fusiform gyrus on the underside of the occipital and temporal lobes, the region Nancy Kanwisher named the fusiform face area, typically on both sides or on the right. A developmental form, present from birth without known injury, affects roughly 2 percent of people; the neurologist Oliver Sacks had it. Clinically, a stroke patient may greet her own daughter politely as a stranger until the daughter speaks. Option c is right because the stem describes intact perception of facial features with lost recognition of identity, the defining feature of prosopagnosia. Exam trap: amnesia also involves failing to recognise people, but the amnesic patient has lost the memory itself, whereas the prosopagnosic still knows the person and recognises them at once by voice.", "topic": "Prosopagnosia (face blindness)"}, {"q": "Ability derived from previous experience is known as", "options": {"a": "crystallized intelligence", "b": "formal operations", "c": "fluid intelligence", "d": "concrete operations"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: crystallised intelligence. Raymond B. Cattell proposed in the 1940s, and later developed with his student John L. Horn, that general intelligence has two broad parts. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason, see relationships and solve new problems without relying on prior learning; Raven's Progressive Matrices and novel puzzles tap it. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the knowledge, vocabulary, skills and judgement acquired through schooling, culture and experience; vocabulary, general information and word-problem tests measure it. In Cattell's investment theory, Gc is fluid ability invested in learning, so it is built from previous experience. The two follow different age curves: Gf peaks in early adulthood and then declines, while Gc holds steady or keeps growing into late adulthood. Their model later merged with John B. Carroll's three-stratum theory into the Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) model, which underlies modern tests such as the Woodcock-Johnson and the Stanford-Binet Fifth Edition. Example: a retired judge who instantly spots the legal flaw in a contract is drawing on crystallised intelligence built over decades. Option a is right because ability derived from previous experience is the definition of crystallised intelligence. Exam trap: students mix up the two; remember that fluid intelligence handles new problems on the spot, while crystallised intelligence is the stored product of experience. Formal and concrete operations are Jean Piaget's stages, not kinds of intelligence.", "topic": "Crystallised versus fluid intelligence"}, {"q": "Personality disorders are characterised by which specific symptoms", "options": {"a": "Permanent severe deficits of cognitive functioning", "b": "loss of contact with reality", "c": "long-term maladaptive traits and behaviour", "d": "unexplained physical symptoms"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: long-term maladaptive traits and behaviour. DSM-5-TR defines a personality disorder as an enduring pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from the expectations of the person's culture, is pervasive and inflexible, begins in adolescence or early adulthood, is stable over time, and leads to distress or impairment. The pattern shows in at least two of four areas: cognition (ways of seeing self and others), affectivity, interpersonal functioning and impulse control. The ten specific disorders fall into three clusters: Cluster A, odd or eccentric (paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal); Cluster B, dramatic, emotional or erratic (antisocial, borderline, histrionic, narcissistic); and Cluster C, anxious or fearful (avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive). Unlike an episode of depression or psychosis, these patterns are usually ego-syntonic: the person sees the traits as simply 'how I am' and often blames others for the problems they cause. Example: a woman with borderline personality disorder has had unstable relationships, intense fear of abandonment and impulsive self-harm since her teens, across many jobs and partners. ICD-11 has replaced the separate types with a single personality disorder diagnosis graded by severity, plus trait domains. Option c is right because enduring, inflexible and maladaptive traits are the defining feature of every personality disorder. Exam trap: loss of contact with reality tempts students who think of schizotypal or paranoid types, but personality disorders do not involve sustained psychosis.", "topic": "Defining features of personality disorders"}, {"q": "Somatic symptoms of severe anxiety include all of the following except:", "options": {"a": "Diarrhoea", "b": "Sighing", "c": "Constipation", "d": "Hypoventilation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: hypoventilation. Severe anxiety activates the sympathetic nervous system and the fight-or-flight response that Walter B. Cannon described. This produces a recognisable set of bodily (somatic) symptoms, which Kaplan and Sadock's textbook and the Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale (Max Hamilton, 1959) list by body system. Cardiovascular: palpitations and chest discomfort. Respiratory: tightness or choking feelings, frequent sighing and over-breathing (hyperventilation). Gastrointestinal: dry mouth, nausea, abdominal discomfort, loose motions or diarrhoea, and sometimes constipation. Others: tremor, sweating, frequent urination, muscle tension and dizziness. Hyperventilation matters most here: rapid breathing blows off carbon dioxide, causing tingling around the mouth and fingers, light-headedness and still more panic, which is why slow-breathing training is part of panic treatment. Hypoventilation, breathing too slowly or shallowly so that carbon dioxide builds up, is the opposite; it occurs with opioid or sedative overdose, severe obesity or neuromuscular disease, not with anxiety. Example: before a viva, an anxious student sighs repeatedly, feels her heart pounding and keeps needing the toilet. Option d is right because anxiety drives breathing up, not down, so hypoventilation is not one of its somatic symptoms. Exam trap: the two words look alike; remember that anxious people over-breathe (hyper), while a drug overdose causes under-breathing (hypo).", "topic": "Somatic symptoms of anxiety"}, {"q": "All of the following are normal experiences except:", "options": {"a": "Déjà vu", "b": "Derealization", "c": "Jamais vu", "d": "Hallucinations"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: hallucinations. The other three are common, brief experiences in healthy people. Déjà vu (French for 'already seen') is the feeling that a new situation has been lived before; surveys suggest about two thirds of people have had it. Jamais vu ('never seen') is the reverse: something familiar suddenly feels strange, as when a common word written many times starts to look wrong. Derealisation is a sense that the surroundings are unreal, flat or dreamlike; it occurs briefly in healthy people under exhaustion, sleep loss or acute stress, and becomes a disorder only when persistent and distressing (depersonalisation-derealisation disorder). A hallucination, as Jean-Etienne Esquirol defined it, is a perception without an external object; it has the full vividness of a real perception and is located in outer space. Hallucinations are a hallmark of psychosis, as in schizophrenia, or of organic states such as delirium. Option d is right because hallucinations are the only one of the four that is a recognised psychopathological symptom rather than a normal variant. Exam trap: students pick derealisation because it sounds abnormal, but brief derealisation is common under fatigue. Key note: brief hallucinations can also occur in healthy people (hypnagogic images while falling asleep, or hearing a dead spouse's voice in grief), but the key stands because hallucinations are the clearest abnormal experience listed.", "topic": "Normal experiences versus hallucinations"}, {"q": "Psychotic depression is characterised by:", "options": {"a": "Circumstantiality", "b": "Nihilistic delusions", "c": "Flight of ideas", "d": "Hypnagogic hallucinations"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: nihilistic delusions. Psychotic depression, called major depressive disorder with psychotic features in DSM-5-TR and severe depressive episode with psychotic symptoms in ICD-10, is a severe depression accompanied by delusions or hallucinations. The psychotic content is usually mood-congruent, matching depressive themes of guilt, worthlessness, disease, poverty, deserved punishment and death. Nihilistic delusions are the extreme case: the patient believes that she, part of her body or the world does not exist, is dead or is rotting, for example that her intestines have stopped working and rotted away, or that her family is already dead. The French neurologist Jules Cotard described this picture in 1880, and its fullest form is called Cotard's syndrome, seen especially in severe depression in older adults. Hallucinations in psychotic depression are similarly mood-congruent, such as voices calling the patient a sinner who deserves to die. Psychotic depression carries a high suicide risk and usually needs an antidepressant combined with an antipsychotic, or electroconvulsive therapy, which works well for it. Option b is right because nihilistic delusions are the classic mood-congruent delusions of psychotic depression. Exam trap: flight of ideas sounds like a psychotic sign, but it belongs to mania, the opposite pole; circumstantiality is a long-winded way of speaking seen in obsessional personalities and epilepsy, not a marker of depression.", "topic": "Nihilistic delusions in psychotic depression"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a Schneider first-rank symptom?", "options": {"a": "Thought withdrawal", "b": "Third-person auditory hallucinations", "c": "Somatic passivity", "d": "Thought block"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: thought block is not a first-rank symptom. The German psychiatrist Kurt Schneider listed first-rank symptoms (FRS) that, in the absence of brain disease, strongly suggest schizophrenia; his Clinical Psychopathology appeared in English in 1959. They are usually grouped as: three kinds of auditory hallucination (thought echo or audible thoughts, voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, and voices giving a running commentary on his actions); thought alienation (thought insertion, thought withdrawal and thought broadcasting); passivity experiences, meaning made feelings, made impulses, made actions and somatic passivity (bodily sensations imposed by an outside agency); and delusional perception (a normal perception suddenly given a private delusional meaning). Thought block is a sudden interruption of the train of thought, so the patient stops mid-sentence and cannot continue; it is an observed disorder of the stream of thought, also common in schizophrenia, but it is not on Schneider's list. What matters is the patient's explanation: if he says someone has pulled the thoughts out of his head, that is thought withdrawal, which is first rank. Option d is right because thought block describes only the interruption, without the delusional belief that an outside force removed the thoughts. Exam trap: thought block and thought withdrawal look identical from outside; withdrawal is first rank only because of the delusional explanation attached to it.", "topic": "Schneider's first-rank symptoms"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about obsessional thoughts?", "options": {"a": "They are best treated by thought-stopping", "b": "They always give rise to compulsions", "c": "They are ego-dystonic", "d": "They are rarely of a sexual nature"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: obsessional thoughts are ego-dystonic. An obsession is a recurrent, intrusive thought, image or impulse that the person experiences as unwanted, senseless and out of character, yet recognises as a product of his or her own mind. Kurt Schneider's classic definition describes contents of consciousness that carry a feeling of subjective compulsion and cannot be got rid of, although on quiet reflection they are seen as senseless. \"Ego-dystonic\" means the experience clashes with the person's values and self-image, so it causes distress and is resisted. Think of a devoted young mother who keeps getting the thought \"I might stab my baby\" while cutting vegetables: she is horrified, hides the knives and fights the thought, which is exactly why it is an obsession and not a wish. ICD-10 adds that at least one thought or act is still resisted, even if unsuccessfully, and that the thought of carrying out the act is not in itself pleasurable. Obsessive-compulsive personality traits such as perfectionism are different: they feel ego-syntonic, natural and right to the person. Option c is right because distress, resistance and the sense that the thought is alien to the self are the core of an obsession. Exam trap: \"recognised as one's own\" (obsession) is not \"inserted from outside\" (thought insertion in schizophrenia); the obsessional patient owns the thought but rejects its content.", "topic": "Obsessions are ego-dystonic"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true regarding delusions?", "options": {"a": "They are usually of a bizarre nature", "b": "They are a reality for the patient", "c": "They are held with a conviction that is shakable", "d": "They are usually self-referent"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: for the patient, a delusion is reality. A delusion is a false, firmly held belief, out of keeping with the person's cultural and educational background, that is not changed by evidence or argument. Karl Jaspers, in his General Psychopathology (1913), gave three external marks: extraordinary conviction (subjective certainty), imperviousness to counter-argument (incorrigibility) and impossible or false content. The clinical core is that the patient does not hold the belief as an idea that might be wrong; he lives it as a plain fact about the world, as real as the chair he sits on. A man convinced that his neighbours are poisoning his water will stop drinking it and may move house; a clean laboratory report does not help, because it is absorbed into the delusion (\"they bribed the lab\"). This separates a delusion from an overvalued idea, held strongly but with some room for doubt, and from an obsession, which the person recognises as senseless. Option b is right because lived certainty, the belief being reality for the patient, is the defining quality of a delusion. Exam trap: conviction in a delusion is unshakable, never shakable, and most delusions are not bizarre. Key note: most delusions do concern the self, so d is partly true and might be marked true in a true/false format; as a single best answer, b is the key.", "topic": "Nature of delusions"}, {"q": "Obsessions of obsessive-compulsive disorder may present as", "options": {"a": "Hallucinations", "b": "Delusions", "c": "Mental images", "d": "Panic attacks"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: obsessions may present as mental images. Obsessions are not only verbal thoughts. ICD-10 describes obsessional thoughts as ideas, images or impulses that enter the mind again and again in a stereotyped form, and Frank Fish's Clinical Psychopathology lists several forms: obsessional thoughts, images, ruminations, doubts, impulses and fears. An obsessional image is a vivid picture that intrudes unbidden, such as a mother repeatedly \"seeing\" her child lying dead after an accident, or a religious man seeing a blasphemous scene during prayer. Like other obsessions, the image is recognised as coming from the person's own mind, is felt as distressing and senseless, and is resisted; the patient may try to cancel it with a neutralising thought, a prayer or a ritual. DSM-5-TR likewise defines obsessions as recurrent and persistent thoughts, urges or images experienced as intrusive and unwanted. Option c is right because the image is one of the standard forms an obsession takes. Exam trap: a vivid image is not a hallucination. A hallucination is perceived as coming through the senses from outside and as real; an obsessional image is experienced in the mind's eye and known to be one's own mental product.", "topic": "Forms of obsessions"}, {"q": "The most commonly seen symptom of schizophrenia is", "options": {"a": "Delusion", "b": "Visual hallucinations", "c": "Auditory hallucinations", "d": "Thought withdrawal"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: auditory hallucinations are the most commonly seen symptom among these options. The best-known data come from the World Health Organization's International Pilot Study of Schizophrenia (IPSS), published in 1973, which examined patients in nine countries, including India. Ranked by frequency, lack of insight came first (about 97 percent of patients), then auditory hallucinations (about 74 percent), verbal hallucinations and ideas of reference (about 70 percent each), and delusions of persecution (about 64 percent). Thought alienation (insertion, withdrawal or broadcasting) was found in roughly half. In schizophrenia these hallucinations are usually voices that talk to the patient, comment on what he is doing or argue about him in the third person; Kurt Schneider counted voices commenting and voices arguing among his first-rank symptoms. A typical patient hears a voice saying \"now he's getting up, now he's going out\" through the day. Visual hallucinations are much less common in schizophrenia and should make you think of delirium, drugs or epilepsy. Option c is right because, after lack of insight (not offered here), auditory hallucinations top the IPSS list. Exam trap: the commonest hallucination is auditory in schizophrenia but visual in organic states. Key note: some texts argue that delusions of one kind or another are near universal, making a the main rival, but the IPSS ranking supports c.", "topic": "Commonest symptom of schizophrenia"}, {"q": "Which of the following is one of the four A's identified by Eugene Bleuler as the primary symptoms of schizophrenia?", "options": {"a": "Abnormal association", "b": "Autistic behaviour", "c": "Ambivalence", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: all of the above, since abnormal association, autism and ambivalence are each one of Bleuler's four A's. The Swiss psychiatrist Eugen Bleuler (the paper spells it \"Eugene\"; the correct spelling is Eugen) coined the word schizophrenia in a 1908 lecture and in his 1911 monograph Dementia Praecox or the Group of Schizophrenias. He replaced Emil Kraepelin's \"dementia praecox\" because not every patient deteriorated or fell ill young. The name means a splitting of mental functions, thought, emotion and behaviour losing their connection, not a split personality. Bleuler held that four fundamental symptoms were present in every case: loosening of Associations (thoughts lose their logical links, so speech drifts from topic to topic); blunted or inappropriate Affect (smiling while describing a parent's death); Ambivalence (opposite feelings or wishes towards the same thing at once, such as loving and hating a brother, a term Bleuler coined); and Autism (withdrawal into a private inner world, quite different from today's autism spectrum disorder). Hallucinations and delusions he called accessory symptoms, because they also occur in other illnesses. Option d is right because three of the four A's are listed, and only Affect is missing. Exam trap: hallucinations are not one of the four A's, and alogia, avolition and anhedonia are later negative-symptom A's, not Bleuler's.", "topic": "Bleuler's four A's"}, {"q": "Which of the following forms of schizophrenia has the best prognosis?", "options": {"a": "Paranoid Schizophrenia", "b": "Catatonic Schizophrenia", "c": "Hebephrenic Schizophrenia", "d": "Undifferentiated Schizophrenia"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: paranoid schizophrenia has the best prognosis among these subtypes. The classical subtypes go back to the nineteenth century: Ewald Hecker described hebephrenia in 1871, Karl Ludwig Kahlbaum described catatonia in 1874, and Emil Kraepelin grouped these with a paranoid form under dementia praecox. ICD-10 kept paranoid, hebephrenic, catatonic, undifferentiated, residual and simple types. Paranoid schizophrenia, the commonest type, is dominated by fairly stable persecutory or grandiose delusions and voices, while speech, affect and behaviour stay relatively intact. Onset tends to be later, after the person has finished education, worked and formed relationships, so there is more premorbid functioning to return to. A 32-year-old clerk who believes his colleagues are plotting against him may still dress well, talk coherently and, with treatment, keep his job. Hebephrenic (disorganised) schizophrenia, by contrast, starts early with incoherence and silly, shallow affect, and has the worst outlook. Option a is right because later onset, preserved personality and predominantly positive symptoms all predict a better course. Exam trap: hebephrenic has the worst prognosis, not the best. Key note: some Indian review books give catatonic as the best prognosis, because acute catatonia often responds dramatically to lorazepam or ECT; and DSM-5 (2013) and ICD-11 have dropped the subtypes altogether.", "topic": "Prognosis of schizophrenia subtypes"}, {"q": "A patient who presents to a clinic describes seeing ghosts when he hears classical music. What is the phenomenon described by the patient?", "options": {"a": "Haptic hallucination", "b": "Hypnagogic hallucination", "c": "Autoscopic hallucination", "d": "Reflex hallucination"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: seeing ghosts whenever classical music is heard is a reflex hallucination. In descriptive psychopathology, as set out in Frank Fish's Clinical Psychopathology and Andrew Sims's Symptoms in the Mind, a reflex hallucination occurs when a real stimulus in one sensory modality produces a hallucination in a different modality. Here a genuine auditory stimulus (the music) triggers a false visual perception (the ghosts). It is a morbid form of synaesthesia, the normal experience some people have of \"seeing\" colours when they hear sounds. Reflex hallucinations are reported in schizophrenia and with hallucinogens such as LSD. Its close cousin is the functional hallucination, in which a real stimulus triggers a hallucination in the same modality and both are perceived together: a patient hears voices only while the tap is running, and hears both the water and the voices. A simple way to remember: functional means the same sense, reflex means a jump to another sense. Option d is right because the trigger (music, heard) and the hallucination (ghosts, seen) are in different senses. Exam trap: students choose functional hallucination whenever a real stimulus is involved; check whether the hallucination stays in the same sense (functional) or crosses to another (reflex).", "topic": "Reflex hallucination across senses"}, {"q": "Which of the following did Vaughn and Leff consider to be a component of expressed emotion”?", "options": {"a": "Critical comments", "b": "Overinvolvement", "c": "Hostility", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: all of the above, since critical comments, hostility and emotional overinvolvement are all components of expressed emotion (EE). The idea grew from George Brown's work in London, which showed that patients with schizophrenia discharged to certain families relapsed more often. Christine Vaughn and Julian Leff refined it in their 1976 study, using the Camberwell Family Interview, a taped semi-structured interview with a relative about the patient. Raters score critical comments, hostility and emotional overinvolvement (the three that define high EE), plus warmth and positive remarks. A mother who says \"he just lies in bed all day to annoy me\" is making critical comments; one who rejects him as a person shows hostility; one who has given up her job, will not let him go out alone and weeps through the interview shows overinvolvement. Vaughn and Leff found that patients returning to high-EE homes relapsed far more often within nine months, especially with more than 35 hours a week of face-to-face contact and no medication. Option d is right because all three listed elements are EE components. Exam trap: EE predicts relapse, not the onset of schizophrenia; it is not Frieda Fromm-Reichmann's \"schizophrenogenic mother\" or Gregory Bateson's double bind, and family work that lowers EE reduces relapse.", "topic": "Components of expressed emotion"}, {"q": "The term “dementia praecox” was coined by", "options": {"a": "Emil Kraepelin", "b": "Eugene Bleuler", "c": "Karl Jaspers", "d": "Adolf Meyer"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: Emil Kraepelin. The German psychiatrist Emil Kraepelin (1856 to 1926) is the name attached to \"dementia praecox\", Latin for early (precocious) dementia. He used the term in the fourth edition of his textbook in 1893, and in the sixth edition of 1899 he drew his famous dividing line: dementia praecox, which begins in youth and runs a deteriorating course, versus manic-depressive insanity, which comes in episodes with good recovery between them. He united pictures described by others, hebephrenia (Ewald Hecker), catatonia (Karl Ludwig Kahlbaum) and paranoid states, as forms of one illness defined above all by its course. Think of a 19-year-old student who withdraws, hears voices and over the years loses his drive and social skills: to Kraepelin, the downhill course itself was diagnostic. In 1908 Eugen Bleuler renamed the group schizophrenia (his monograph followed in 1911) because not every patient deteriorated or fell ill young. Option a is right because Kraepelin made the Latin term famous and built the concept around it. Exam trap: the French physician Benedict Morel used the French phrase \"démence précoce\" earlier, in the 1850s, for young patients who deteriorated, and Arnold Pick used the Latin term in 1891; neither is offered here. Key note: strictly, Kraepelin popularised rather than coined the term, but among these options he is the standard answer.", "topic": "Origin of dementia praecox"}, {"q": "All of the following are features of endogenous depression except", "options": {"a": "Lack of appetite", "b": "Weight loss", "c": "Irritability", "d": "Anhedonia"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: irritability is not a classic feature of endogenous depression. \"Endogenous\" means arising from within, and the term was used for depression that seemed biologically driven rather than a reaction to events. In mid-twentieth-century British psychiatry, Aubrey Lewis argued that depression was a single continuum, while Martin Roth's Newcastle group argued that endogenous and reactive (neurotic) depression were separate types. Today the endogenous picture survives as the DSM-5-TR specifier \"with melancholic features\" and the ICD-10 \"somatic syndrome\". Its hallmarks are profound loss of pleasure (anhedonia) with lack of mood reactivity, a distinct quality of low mood, depression worse in the morning, early morning waking, marked psychomotor retardation or agitation, significant loss of appetite and weight, and excessive guilt. Picture a 55-year-old teacher who wakes at 3 a.m., cannot enjoy even her grandchildren's visit, has lost 6 kg and blames herself for imagined failures, with no life event to explain it. Irritability, by contrast, is more typical of reactive or neurotic depression, of depression in children and adolescents (where DSM-5-TR allows irritable mood in place of sad mood) and of mixed states. Option c is right because irritability is not part of the melancholic or somatic syndrome. Exam trap: in this \"except\" question the three biological features (appetite, weight, anhedonia) belong together; the odd one out is the reactive, emotional feature.", "topic": "Features of endogenous depression"}, {"q": "Who proposed the theory of “Learned helplessness” to explain depression?", "options": {"a": "Wolpe", "b": "Seligman", "c": "Freud", "d": "Beck"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: Martin Seligman. Learned helplessness was discovered in the late 1960s, when Martin Seligman and Steven Maier gave dogs electric shocks they could not escape or control. Later, in a shuttle box where jumping over a low barrier would end the shock, many of these dogs did not even try; they lay down and whimpered, while dogs without the earlier experience quickly learned to escape. The animals had learned that their actions made no difference. Seligman saw a parallel with human depression: passivity, low motivation, slowed learning and sad mood after repeated uncontrollable bad events, and he set this out in his 1975 book Helplessness. A student who fails maths several times despite real effort may stop studying and conclude \"nothing I do matters\". In 1978 Lyn Abramson, Seligman and John Teasdale reformulated the theory in attributional terms: people at risk explain bad events as internal (\"my fault\"), stable (\"it will last\") and global (\"it ruins everything\"). This later grew into the hopelessness theory and Seligman's work on explanatory style and learned optimism. Option b is right because Seligman proposed learned helplessness as a model of depression. Exam trap: Beck's cognitive triad is also a theory of depression, so read the term carefully: learned helplessness is Seligman, the negative triad is Beck.", "topic": "Learned helplessness theory of depression"}, {"q": "The “Negative Cognitive Triad” was proposed by Beck to explain", "options": {"a": "Anxiety", "b": "Bereavement reaction", "c": "Depression", "d": "Adjustment disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: depression. The American psychiatrist Aaron Beck, originally trained in psychoanalysis, noticed in the 1960s that his depressed patients' thinking was dominated by consistently negative, distorted thoughts. He proposed the negative cognitive triad: a negative view of the self (\"I am worthless\"), of the world or present experience (\"nothing ever goes right for me\") and of the future (\"it will always be like this\"). These views are driven by dysfunctional schemas formed early in life, activated by stress, and kept alive by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, selective abstraction, arbitrary inference, personalisation and all-or-nothing thinking. A young employee who receives one piece of criticism may think \"I'm useless\" (self), \"my boss hates me\" (world) and \"I'll never succeed in any job\" (future). Beck built cognitive therapy on this model, teaching patients to catch their automatic thoughts, test them against evidence and replace them with balanced ones; his 1979 book Cognitive Therapy of Depression described the method. Option c is right because the triad was proposed specifically to explain depression. Exam trap: Beck also wrote a cognitive model of anxiety, but it centres on overestimating danger and underestimating one's coping, not on the triad; and the triad is self, world and future, with no corner for the past.", "topic": "Beck's negative cognitive triad"}, {"q": "According to ICD-10, for a diagnosis of a depressive episode, the symptoms should be present for a minimum of", "options": {"a": "1 week", "b": "2 weeks", "c": "1 month", "d": "2 months"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: 2 weeks. ICD-10, the World Health Organization's classification published in 1992, requires that a depressive episode last at least two weeks, although a shorter period may be accepted if the symptoms are unusually severe and of rapid onset. DSM-5-TR uses the same two-week rule for a major depressive episode. The two weeks separate a clinical episode from ordinary bad days: everyone has a sad weekend, but a fortnight of almost continuous symptoms is a different matter. ICD-10 lists three typical symptoms (depressed mood, loss of interest and enjoyment, and reduced energy with increased fatigability) and seven others, including poor concentration, low self-esteem, guilt, pessimism, ideas of self-harm, disturbed sleep and reduced appetite. Severity is graded by count: a mild episode needs two typical plus two other symptoms, a moderate episode two typical plus three or four others, and a severe episode all three typical plus at least four others. A woman who has felt low, joyless and exhausted for three weeks, with poor sleep and guilt, meets the time rule. Option b is right because two weeks is the ICD-10 minimum. Exam trap: one week is the minimum for a manic episode and one month is the ICD-10 duration for schizophrenia, so match each number to its disorder.", "topic": "ICD-10 duration for depressive episode"}, {"q": "Who first described autism?", "options": {"a": "Kanner", "b": "Asperger", "c": "Chess", "d": "Benham"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: Leo Kanner. The Austrian-American child psychiatrist Leo Kanner, working at Johns Hopkins in Baltimore, published \"Autistic Disturbances of Affective Contact\" in 1943, describing 11 children who from early life showed extreme aloneness, an inability to relate normally to people, odd or delayed language (echolalia, pronoun reversal, very literal speech) and an \"anxiously obsessive desire for the maintenance of sameness\". Such a child might become distressed if a single piece of furniture was moved. He gave it the name early infantile autism in a 1944 paper, and for decades it was known as Kanner's syndrome. In 1944, a year after Kanner's first paper, the Austrian paediatrician Hans Asperger independently described boys with \"autistic psychopathy\": fluent speech and normal intelligence but poor social understanding, narrow interests and clumsiness. His German-language paper became widely known only after Lorna Wing's 1981 paper on \"Asperger's syndrome\". DSM-5-TR now merges both pictures into autism spectrum disorder. Option a is right because Kanner's 1943 paper is the first standard description of autism as a childhood syndrome. Exam trap: the word \"autism\" was coined earlier by Eugen Bleuler (1910, then in his 1911 book on schizophrenia), but for withdrawal in schizophrenia, not for the developmental disorder; and Asperger came after Kanner, not before.", "topic": "First description of autism"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the person with oppositional defiant disorder?", "options": {"a": "They often lose their temper", "b": "They blame others for their mistakes", "c": "They deliberately annoy others", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: all of the above. Oppositional defiant disorder (ODD) entered the DSM in 1980 (DSM-III, as oppositional disorder) and took its present name in DSM-III-R (1987). DSM-5-TR defines it as a pattern of angry or irritable mood, argumentative or defiant behaviour, or vindictiveness lasting at least six months, shown by at least four of eight symptoms with at least one person who is not a sibling. Angry or irritable mood covers often losing temper, being touchy or easily annoyed, and being angry and resentful. Argumentative or defiant behaviour covers arguing with authority figures, refusing rules and requests, deliberately annoying others, and blaming others for one's own mistakes or misbehaviour. Vindictiveness means being spiteful at least twice in six months. A nine-year-old who explodes when told to switch off the TV, hides his sister's books to provoke her and says \"the teacher made me fail\" fits the picture. Conduct disorder is different: it violates others' rights through aggression, destruction, theft and serious rule-breaking. Option d is right because temper loss, blaming others and deliberately annoying others are all DSM-5-TR symptoms. Exam trap: ODD is defiance without serious aggression; once a child is cruel, destructive or stealing, think conduct disorder. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "topic": "Oppositional defiant disorder features"}, {"q": "Raven's Standard Progressive Matrices provide a direct measure of", "options": {"a": "Verbal intelligence", "b": "Concrete intelligence", "c": "Abstract intelligence", "d": "Emotional intelligence"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: abstract intelligence. Raven's Standard Progressive Matrices (SPM) was published in 1938 by the British psychologist John C. Raven, a student of Charles Spearman. Each item shows a matrix of designs with one piece missing, and the person picks the piece that completes it from six or eight alternatives. The SPM has 60 items in five sets (A to E) of 12, growing harder as it goes, hence \"progressive\". No words, reading or general knowledge are needed: to solve an item you must spot the rule running across rows and columns and apply it. Raven designed it to measure Spearman's general factor g, specifically eductive ability, the capacity to draw out relations and make sense of new material; in modern terms it is a classic test of fluid, abstract, non-verbal reasoning. Being largely free of language and schooling, it is used as a culture-reduced test, including in India. Related forms are the Coloured Progressive Matrices for young children and older adults and the Advanced Progressive Matrices for able adults. Option c is right because the SPM directly taps abstract reasoning with non-verbal symbols. Exam trap: \"non-verbal\" does not mean \"concrete\"; the matrices are abstract patterns, not real objects to handle. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "topic": "Raven's Progressive Matrices"}, {"q": "Problem-solving comprises four stages. Choose the correct sequence of stages:", "options": {"a": "Incubation, preparation, verification, illumination", "b": "Preparation, incubation, illumination, verification", "c": "Incubation, preparation, illumination, verification", "d": "Preparation, illumination, incubation, verification"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: preparation, incubation, illumination, verification. This sequence comes from the English social psychologist Graham Wallas in his 1926 book The Art of Thought, drawing on Henri Poincare's and Hermann von Helmholtz's accounts of discovery. Wallas described it as the creative process, and textbooks often teach it as the stages of creative problem-solving. In preparation you define the problem and gather information, working on it consciously. In incubation you set the problem aside, but work seems to continue outside awareness. In illumination the solution appears suddenly, the \"aha\" moment, often during something unrelated: Poincare reported that a mathematical solution came to him just as he stepped onto a bus. In verification you test and refine the idea to check that it really works. A student stuck on a statistics problem who studies it for an hour (preparation), goes for a walk (incubation), suddenly sees the answer in the shower (illumination) and then checks it on paper (verification) has gone through all four. Option b is right because it is the only option that gives Wallas's stages in his order. Exam trap: incubation always comes after preparation, since you cannot incubate a problem you have not worked on, and before illumination. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "topic": "Wallas's four stages of creativity"}, {"q": "The child who says “Milk gone” engages in ______. This type of utterance demonstrates that children are actively experimenting with the rules of ______.", "options": {"a": "Babbling, syntax", "b": "Telegraphic speech, syntax", "c": "Babbling, semantics", "d": "Telegraphic speech, semantics"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: telegraphic speech, syntax. Between about 18 and 24 months, children move from single words to two-word utterances such as \"Milk gone\", \"Daddy car\". Roger Brown, the Harvard psychologist who studied early language in detail, called this telegraphic speech because, like an old telegram where every word cost money, it keeps the content words and drops function words and endings such as \"the\", \"is\" and \"-ed\". These short utterances are not random. The words come in a consistent order that follows the grammar of the child's language: the child says \"Milk gone\", not \"Gone milk\". Ordering words to express who did what to whom is syntax, the set of rules for combining words into sentences. Since adults rarely say \"Milk gone\", the child is not just copying but actively experimenting with syntactic rules. A later sign of the same rule learning is overregularisation, as in \"I goed\" or \"two mouses\". Option b is right because \"Milk gone\" is a two-word telegraphic utterance that shows the child using word-order rules. Exam trap: babbling (repeated syllables like \"ba-ba-ba\" from about six months) comes before real words, and semantics is about meaning, while the point of telegraphic speech is rule-governed word order. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "topic": "Telegraphic speech and syntax"}, {"q": "This area contains “Somatosensory Cortex”", "options": {"a": "Parietal lobe", "b": "Frontal lobe", "c": "Temporal lobe", "d": "Occipital lobe"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the parietal lobe. The primary somatosensory cortex lies on the postcentral gyrus, the strip of cortex just behind the central sulcus, which divides the frontal from the parietal lobe. It corresponds to Brodmann areas 3, 1 and 2 and receives touch, pressure, temperature, pain and body-position information from the opposite side of the body. The Canadian neurosurgeon Wilder Penfield mapped it during epilepsy surgery by stimulating the exposed cortex of awake patients, who reported tingling in particular body parts. The result is the sensory homunculus, a distorted body map in which the lips, tongue and fingers get huge areas because they are densely supplied with receptors, while the back and legs get little. Just in front, on the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe, lies the primary motor cortex. Parietal damage causes loss of fine touch and position sense, astereognosis (failing to recognise an object such as a key by touch) and, with right-sided lesions, neglect of the left side of space. Option a is right because the postcentral gyrus is part of the parietal lobe. Exam trap: motor is in front of the central sulcus (frontal), sensory is behind it (parietal); students often swap them. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "topic": "Location of somatosensory cortex"}, {"q": "When a child gets two chocolates once every week for its performance in class, it is an instance of", "options": {"a": "Fixed ratio, variable interval", "b": "Fixed ratio, fixed interval", "c": "Variable ratio, fixed interval", "d": "Variable ratio, variable interval"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: fixed ratio, fixed interval. Schedules of reinforcement were studied by Burrhus Frederic (B. F.) Skinner with Charles Ferster in their 1957 book Schedules of Reinforcement. A ratio schedule rewards after a number of responses: fixed ratio (FR) after a set number, as in piece-rate pay, and variable ratio (VR) after an unpredictable number, as in a slot machine. An interval schedule rewards the first response after a period of time: fixed interval (FI) after a set time, as in a weekly allowance or monthly salary, and variable interval (VI) after unpredictable times, as in surprise quizzes. FI produces a \"scallop\": little effort just after a reward and a burst as the next one approaches, like students who study mostly before exams. Here the child is rewarded once every week, a fixed time, so the core schedule is fixed interval. The amount is also fixed, two chocolates every time, which is what the setter calls \"fixed ratio\". Option b is right because it is the only option that pairs fixed interval with a fixed reward. Exam trap: ratio means number of responses and interval means time; a fixed amount of reward is not, strictly, a ratio schedule. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts; b is the only defensible choice.", "topic": "Schedules of reinforcement"}, {"q": "Feeling touchy or hypersensitive following an upsetting experience is a form of", "options": {"a": "Imprinting", "b": "Habituation", "c": "Sensitization", "d": "Reflexive Behaviour"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: sensitization. Sensitization is, with habituation, the simplest kind of learning, and is called non-associative because it involves a single stimulus rather than a link between two. After a strong, upsetting or painful stimulus, the organism becomes more responsive to a whole range of other stimuli, even mild ones. Eric Kandel, who shared the 2000 Nobel Prize, traced its cellular basis in the sea slug Aplysia: after a shock to the tail, a light touch to the siphon produced a much bigger gill-withdrawal reflex than before. Human examples are everyday. After a near-miss on the road at night, you jump at every horn and flash of headlights; after a frightening film, every creak in the house makes your heart race. That touchy, hypersensitive state is sensitization. Its near opposite is habituation, in which the response to a repeated, harmless stimulus decreases, as when you stop noticing the trains outside a new hostel room after a few nights. Option c is right because the heightened reactivity follows an upsetting experience, which is exactly how sensitization arises. Exam trap: habituation means reacting less to a repeated harmless stimulus, sensitization means reacting more after an upsetting one; textbooks pair them, so students swap them. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "topic": "Sensitization as non-associative learning"}, {"q": "Which of the following is the final destination for much of the brain's information about emotion before action is taken?", "options": {"a": "Amygdala", "b": "Prefrontal Cortex", "c": "Anterior cingulate cortex", "d": "Hypothalamus"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the prefrontal cortex. Emotional information passes through several stations. Joseph LeDoux showed that sensory signals reach the amygdala by a fast, crude \"low road\" straight from the thalamus and by a slower, detailed \"high road\" through the cortex, so the amygdala can raise the alarm before we know what we have seen. Deciding what to do about the feeling happens at the front of the brain. The prefrontal cortex, especially its ventromedial and orbitofrontal parts, receives input from the amygdala, the insula and the senses, weighs it against goals, rules and likely consequences, and then guides action. Antonio Damasio's somatic marker hypothesis (1994) proposes that these regions use bodily emotional signals to steer decisions; patients with such damage, like the railway foreman Phineas Gage in 1848, keep their intelligence but make reckless, socially inappropriate choices. If a stranger shouts at you on a bus, your amygdala fires and your heart races, but the prefrontal cortex decides whether to shout back, walk away or laugh. Option b is right because the prefrontal cortex is the final integration point for emotional information before action is chosen. Exam trap: the amygdala is the emotional alarm, not the decision-maker. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "topic": "Prefrontal cortex and emotion"}, {"q": "Amit met with an accident and later turned impulsive, irresponsible and less concerned with the consequences of his actions. He must have suffered damage in:", "options": {"a": "Parietal lobe", "b": "Frontal lobe", "c": "Occipital lobe", "d": "Temporal lobe"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the frontal lobe. The frontal lobes, especially the prefrontal cortex in front of the motor areas, handle planning, judgement, inhibition of impulses and foreseeing consequences, together called executive functions. The classic case is Phineas Gage, a railway foreman whose left frontal lobe was pierced by an iron tamping rod in an explosion in 1848. He survived and could walk, talk and remember, but his physician, John Harlow, reported that he became fitful, impulsive, profane, obstinate and unable to carry out plans; his friends said he was \"no longer Gage\". Modern neuropsychology describes several frontal syndromes. Orbitofrontal damage produces disinhibition: impulsivity, tactlessness, poor social judgement and indifference to consequences. Dorsolateral damage produces a dysexecutive syndrome with poor planning, rigidity and perseveration, and medial damage produces apathy. Amit's new impulsiveness, irresponsibility and lack of concern for consequences after an accident fit the disinhibited picture, a common result of head injury because the undersides of the frontal lobes bruise easily against the bony skull base. Option b is right because personality change with impulsivity and poor judgement is the hallmark of frontal damage. Exam trap: memory loss points to the temporal lobes; personality change and poor judgement point to the frontal lobes. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "topic": "Frontal lobe damage and personality"}, {"q": "Learning disabilities in Mathematics can be assessed most appropriately by which of the following tests?", "options": {"a": "Aptitude tests", "b": "Diagnostic tests", "c": "Screening tests", "d": "Achievement tests"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: diagnostic tests. Anne Anastasi, in Psychological Testing, separates the survey achievement test, which gives an overall level of attainment, from the diagnostic test, which analyses a person's specific strengths and weaknesses within a subject and points to the source of difficulty. A learning disability in mathematics (dyscalculia) is not just low marks; it is a particular pattern of breakdown, for example in number sense, memory for arithmetic facts, place value, carrying and borrowing, or word problems. A diagnostic test breaks arithmetic into these sub-skills and analyses the errors. Suppose a Class 4 child scores 40 percent in maths. An achievement test only confirms she is behind. A diagnostic test, such as KeyMath or the arithmetic section of the NIMHANS Index for Specific Learning Disabilities, can show that she adds well but always takes the smaller digit from the larger (52 minus 17 gives 45), which tells the teacher exactly what to remedy. Option b is right because diagnostic tests are built to pinpoint the nature of a specific learning difficulty. Exam trap: achievement tests show how much has been learned and confirm low attainment, but do not show why the child is failing; diagnostic tests do. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "topic": "Diagnostic tests for learning disability"}, {"q": "Following a head injury, a victim's performance dropped in a language test but not a spatial one. This may be due to a possible injury in the following brain area", "options": {"a": "The right hemisphere", "b": "Broca's area", "c": "The reticular system", "d": "Medulla"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: Broca's area. In about 95 percent of right-handed people, and most left-handers, language depends mainly on the left hemisphere, while the right hemisphere is relatively better at spatial tasks, face recognition and the emotional tone of speech. The first clear evidence came from the French surgeon Paul Broca, who in 1861 examined a patient named Leborgne, nicknamed \"Tan\" because it was almost the only syllable he could say. At autopsy Broca found a lesion in the left inferior frontal gyrus, now called Broca's area (Brodmann areas 44 and 45). Damage here causes Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia: slow, effortful, telegraphic speech with comprehension relatively preserved. In the question, a head-injured person does worse on a language test but not on a spatial one. That pattern points to the left hemisphere, and among the options only Broca's area is a left-hemisphere language area. Option b is right because a Broca's area injury impairs language while sparing spatial abilities, which rely more on the right hemisphere. Exam trap: right hemisphere damage would do the reverse, hitting spatial skills more than language. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts; strictly the pattern points to the left hemisphere generally (Wernicke's area would also fit), and Broca's area is the best option offered.", "topic": "Language lateralisation and Broca's area"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"cip-2020": [{"q": "______ is the process which involves detecting, translating, and transmitting from the external environment to the brain.", "concept": "The answer is b: sensation is the process of detecting, translating and transmitting energy from the external environment to the brain. Sensation has three steps. First, specialised receptor cells detect a physical stimulus: light in the eye, sound waves in the ear, chemicals on the tongue and in the nose, pressure and temperature on the skin. Second, the receptors translate that energy into nerve impulses, a step called transduction. Third, sensory nerves transmit the impulses to the brain, where most senses are relayed through the thalamus to the cortex. The scientific study of this link between physical energy and experience is psychophysics, founded by Gustav Theodor Fechner in Elements of Psychophysics (1860), building on Ernst Heinrich Weber's work on the just noticeable difference. Example: when you step into sunlight, light strikes the rods and cones of your retina, which pass signals through the bipolar cells to the ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve; recognising that the shape ahead is a friend's face is the next stage, perception. Option b is right because detecting, transducing and transmitting raw stimulus energy is the textbook definition of sensation. Exam trap: sensation is the raw input from receptors to brain, while perception is the organising and interpreting of that input; the words \"to the brain\" mark sensation. Key note: the coaching book prints a here, but its own explanation describes sensation, so b is used.", "traps": {"a": "Adaptation (sensory adaptation) is the drop in sensitivity to an unchanging stimulus, such as no longer smelling your own perfume after a few minutes; it is one feature of sensation, not the whole process.", "c": "Selective attention is focusing on some stimuli while filtering out others, as in the cocktail party effect studied by Colin Cherry; it acts on information already received rather than carrying it to the brain.", "d": "Perception is the brain's organisation and interpretation of sensory input into meaningful objects and events; it comes after sensation has delivered the signals."}}, {"q": "Which option is not commonly diagnosed in criminals?", "concept": "The answer is a: depersonalization disorder is not commonly diagnosed in criminals. Depersonalization/derealization disorder (DSM-5-TR) involves persistent or recurrent experiences of feeling detached from one's own thoughts, feelings, body or actions, as if watching oneself from outside (depersonalization), or of the surroundings seeming dreamlike or unreal (derealization). Reality testing stays intact: the person says \"it is as if I am in a dream\", knows it is not so, and is usually frightened by the feeling. It is a dissociative disorder with no special link to offending. By contrast, forensic research shows antisocial personality disorder is the most common diagnosis among offenders; a large review of prison surveys by Seena Fazel and John Danesh (2002) found that nearly half of male prisoners met its criteria. Narcissistic traits are over-represented among violent offenders, and psychotic disorders such as schizophrenia, though present in only a minority of prisoners, occur at several times the general-population rate. Clinical example: a college student who, under exam stress, feels her hands belong to someone else and her room looks like a film set has depersonalization and derealization, not a criminal tendency. Option a is right because depersonalization is an inward, distressing dissociative experience with no established link to crime. Exam trap: do not pick schizophrenia just because most people with schizophrenia are not violent; the question asks about rates among criminals, where it is elevated.", "traps": {"b": "Schizophrenia is found in a minority of offenders, yet psychotic illness is several times more common in prisons than in the general population, so it is a recognised forensic diagnosis.", "c": "Narcissistic personality disorder, marked by grandiosity, entitlement and low empathy, is over-represented among offenders, and narcissists are prone to aggression when their self-image is threatened.", "d": "Antisocial personality disorder, a pervasive disregard for and violation of others' rights, is the single most common diagnosis among prisoners."}}, {"q": "People lose touch with reality when they are...", "concept": "The answer is a: people lose touch with reality when they are psychotic. Psychosis is a loss of contact with reality, shown by delusions (fixed false beliefs), hallucinations (perceptions without an external stimulus) and grossly disorganised speech or behaviour. Reality testing, the ability to tell inner experience from the outside world, breaks down, and insight is usually poor: the person does not recognise that anything is wrong. The classic contrast is with neurosis, a word coined by the Scottish physician William Cullen in 1769 and later narrowed by Sigmund Freud to non-psychotic disorders of unconscious conflict in which insight is preserved. Older classifications divided most mental illness into these two groups. Schizophrenia, delusional disorder, brief psychotic disorder and mood disorders with psychotic features are the main psychotic conditions today. Clinical example: a man who insists that his neighbours have planted a chip in his brain and hears them commenting on his actions is psychotic; a woman who fears contamination and washes her hands for hours while knowing the fear is excessive is not, because her reality testing is intact. Option a is right because loss of reality contact is the defining feature of psychosis. Exam trap: \"psychopathic\" sounds like \"psychotic\" but means a callous, remorseless personality style with reality testing intact, and \"neurotic\" means distress with insight retained.", "traps": {"b": "Mania is a period of abnormally elevated or irritable mood with high energy, little need for sleep and racing thoughts; it counts as psychotic only when delusions or hallucinations appear, so it is not the defining term.", "c": "Neurosis is the old term for anxiety-based disorders in which the person is distressed but keeps insight and contact with reality.", "d": "Psychopathy, described by Hervey Cleckley and measured by Robert Hare's PCL-R, is a callous, remorseless, manipulative personality pattern; psychopaths know what is real and usually know right from wrong."}}, {"q": "The MMPI is used to measure:", "concept": "The answer is b: the MMPI measures personality and psychological disorders. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was developed at the University of Minnesota by the psychologist Starke R. Hathaway and the psychiatrist J. Charnley McKinley and published in 1943. They used empirical criterion keying: an item was kept if a psychiatric patient group (for example, people with depression) answered it differently from ordinary people, whatever the item seemed to be about. The result was ten clinical scales, including Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Paranoia, Schizophrenia and Social Introversion (added by Drake in 1946), plus validity scales (L for lying, F for infrequent responding, and K for defensiveness, added by Meehl and Hathaway in 1946) that show whether the person answered honestly. The MMPI-2 (1989) has 567 true/false items; the MMPI-2-RF and MMPI-3 are shorter revisions, and the MMPI-A is for adolescents. Clinical example: a psychologist assessing a patient with vague physical complaints may find Hypochondriasis and Hysteria raised well above Depression, the classic \"conversion V\" profile, which guides diagnosis and treatment planning. Option b is right because the MMPI is the most widely used objective inventory for assessing personality and psychopathology. Exam trap: the MMPI is an objective self-report inventory, not a projective test, and it was built empirically, not from the Big Five or any theory of traits.", "traps": {"a": "Leadership potential is judged with tools such as assessment centres, situational judgement tests or normal-range inventories like the California Psychological Inventory; the MMPI was not designed for this.", "c": "The Big Five (openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, neuroticism) are measured by the NEO-PI-R of Paul T. Costa Jr. and Robert R. McCrae, a factor-analytic inventory of normal personality.", "d": "Unconscious drives are the target of projective techniques such as the Rorschach inkblots and the TAT, which assume people project hidden needs onto ambiguous stimuli."}}, {"q": "Insight learning is basically learning by", "concept": "The answer is a: insight learning is basically learning by cognition. Insight learning was described by the Gestalt psychologist Wolfgang Kohler, who studied chimpanzees at the anthropoid station on Tenerife between 1913 and 1920 and reported the work in The Mentality of Apes (1917 in German, 1925 in English). A banana was hung out of reach or placed outside the cage. After failed attempts, a chimpanzee named Sultan would suddenly join two short sticks into a long one (the keeper saw him hit on it while idly playing with the sticks, then run straight to the bars), and in the box tests the apes paced, stopped short and dragged or stacked boxes to get the fruit. Kohler argued that the animal had mentally restructured the perceptual field, seeing the relationship between the parts and the goal. Insight has typical marks: the solution comes suddenly (the \"aha\" experience), it is performed smoothly once found, it is remembered, and it transfers to similar problems. Everyday example: you struggle with a riddle, stop, and the answer suddenly clicks, after which you can solve others like it. Option a is right because insight depends on thinking and understanding relationships, a cognitive process, rather than on reinforced associations. Exam trap: insight is the opposite of Edward L. Thorndike's gradual trial-and-error learning in the puzzle box, and Kohler's work was a Gestalt challenge to behaviourism.", "traps": {"b": "\"Conditions\" points to conditioning, classical or operant, where learning comes from associations between stimuli, responses and consequences rather than sudden understanding.", "c": "Trial and error is Edward L. Thorndike's account of cats in puzzle boxes, where correct responses are stamped in gradually by their consequences; insight is sudden, not gradual.", "d": "Imitation is observational learning, studied by Albert Bandura in the Bobo doll experiments, where behaviour is copied from a model."}}, {"q": "“Bell” in Pavlov's classical conditioning is", "concept": "The answer is b: in Pavlov's classical conditioning, the bell is the neutral stimulus (NS). Ivan Petrovich Pavlov, a Russian physiologist who won the 1904 Nobel Prize for his work on digestion, noticed that his dogs began salivating before food reached their mouths, and he set out to study this systematically. Food (meat powder) is the unconditioned stimulus (UCS) because it produces salivation without any learning; that salivation is the unconditioned response (UCR). A sound such as a bell (Pavlov's laboratory also used metronomes, buzzers and tones) at first produces no salivation, only an orienting response, so it is a neutral stimulus. After the sound is repeatedly presented just before the food, it alone triggers salivation. At that point the bell has become the conditioned stimulus (CS), and salivation to it is the conditioned response (CR). Everyday example: a child who is regularly given an injection after smelling a hospital's antiseptic may start crying at that smell alone. Option b is right because, before conditioning, the bell is a stimulus that naturally elicits no salivation, which is the definition of a neutral stimulus. Exam trap: after conditioning the bell becomes the CS, which is not offered here, so choose NS; and never label a stimulus as a response (UCR or CR).", "traps": {"a": "The unconditioned stimulus (UCS) is the food, which produces salivation automatically without any learning.", "c": "The unconditioned response (UCR) is the natural salivation to food; it is a response, not a stimulus.", "d": "The conditioned response (CR) is the learned salivation to the bell after pairing; the bell is the stimulus that triggers it, not the response itself."}}, {"q": "When a behaviour occurs consistently in the presence of a discriminative stimulus, it is said to be under", "concept": "The answer is a: a behaviour that occurs consistently in the presence of a discriminative stimulus is under stimulus control. The idea comes from B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning. A discriminative stimulus (SD) is a cue signalling that a particular response will now be reinforced; a cue signalling no reinforcement is called an S-delta. When an organism learns to respond in the presence of the SD and not in its absence, the behaviour is under stimulus control. Together with the behaviour and its consequence, the SD forms the three-term contingency, often written A-B-C: antecedent, behaviour, consequence. In the laboratory, a pigeon learns to peck a key only when a green light is on, because only then are pecks rewarded with grain. In daily life, a chai stall's \"Open\" board signals that asking for tea will be rewarded, children ask a lenient grandparent rather than a strict parent for sweets, and drivers stop at red lights. Clinically, Richard Bootzin's stimulus control therapy for insomnia keeps the bed for sleep only, so that the bed becomes a cue for sleeping rather than worrying. Option a is right because consistent responding tied to a discriminative stimulus is exactly what stimulus control means. Exam trap: a discriminative stimulus does not elicit the response the way a conditioned stimulus does in classical conditioning; it only sets the occasion for an operant response.", "traps": {"b": "\"Delayed process\" is not a standard term; it may echo delayed conditioning (the CS starts before and overlaps the UCS) or delayed reinforcement, neither of which describes responding to a discriminative stimulus.", "c": "Extinction is the weakening and eventual disappearance of a response when reinforcement stops, the opposite of consistent responding.", "d": "Classical conditioning concerns reflexive responses elicited by a conditioned stimulus; discriminative stimuli and stimulus control belong to operant conditioning."}}, {"q": "Role learning is part of", "concept": "The answer is b: role learning is part of socialization. Socialization is the lifelong process by which a person learns the norms, values, skills and roles of a society until they become part of the self. A role is the set of expectations, rights and duties attached to a social position (status), such as daughter, student, teacher or doctor, and learning these roles is one of socialization's main tasks. Charles Horton Cooley described the looking-glass self, in which we come to see ourselves through others' reactions. George Herbert Mead explained how children take roles: in the play stage a child acts out particular others (playing \"mother\" or \"doctor\"), and in the game stage the child grasps the generalised other, the expectations of the whole group. Primary socialization happens in the family; secondary socialization continues through school, peers, work and the media. Robert K. Merton added anticipatory socialization, rehearsing the role of a group one hopes to join. Example: a medical aspirant who already talks and dresses like a doctor, or a new bride learning what her in-laws expect of her, is engaged in role learning. Option b is right because acquiring socially defined roles is a central component of socialization. Exam trap: role learning does involve learning, but the question asks which wider social process it belongs to, and that process is socialization.", "traps": {"a": "Learning is any relatively permanent change in behaviour through experience; it is true but too general, since role learning is specifically the social learning that makes up socialization.", "c": "\"Rebuke condition\" is not a recognised concept in psychology or sociology; it is a filler distractor.", "d": "\"Reduction change\" is not a recognised concept; it may be meant to sound like Clark L. Hull's drive reduction, a theory of motivation, not of social roles."}}, {"q": "The impact of child-rearing practices on personality development was discovered by", "concept": "The answer is d: Watson. John B. Watson, who launched behaviourism with his 1913 paper \"Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It\", held that personality is the sum of learned habits and that the environment parents create shapes the child. With Rosalie Rayner he conditioned fear in the infant Little Albert (1920) by pairing a white rat with a loud noise, which he took as proof that emotional reactions are built by conditioning. His 1925 boast, \"Give me a dozen healthy infants\", claimed he could train any child into any type of adult, whatever its heredity. In Psychological Care of Infant and Child (1928), written with Rayner, he advised strict feeding schedules and little hugging or kissing, arguing that rearing practices directly mould a child's character. Example: on Watson's view, a child whose tantrums are rewarded with attention develops a demanding personality because of what the parents reinforced, not because of inborn temperament. Option d is right because the paper credits Watson's behaviourist claim that parents, by controlling the child's stimulus-response associations, determine personality. Exam trap: \"Gestalt\" is a school, not a person, and Berkowitz is an aggression researcher, so the real contest is between Watson and Freud. Key note: Sigmund Freud's theory that feeding, weaning and toilet training shape adult character is a strong rival answer, so this key is arguable; it follows the coaching book's behaviourist reading.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud argued that how parents handle feeding, weaning and toilet training during the psychosexual stages can leave lasting fixations in adult personality; this makes a a strong rival, but the paper's key credits Watson.", "b": "Gestalt psychology is a school founded by Max Wertheimer, Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka that studied perception and insight; it is not a person and did not study child rearing.", "c": "Leonard Berkowitz studied aggression, known for the weapons effect and his reformulation of the frustration-aggression hypothesis, not for child rearing and personality."}}, {"q": "An organism tends to repeat those behaviours that bring about satisfaction, and it tends to discard those that bring about dissatisfaction", "concept": "The answer is c: Thorndike's Law of Effect. Edward L. Thorndike, an American psychologist, studied hungry cats in puzzle boxes for his 1898 doctoral work, Animal Intelligence. A cat could escape and reach food only by pulling a loop or pressing a lever. At first it scratched and clawed at random until it hit the latch by chance. Over trials the useless responses dropped out and the correct one came sooner, a gradual curve showing trial-and-error learning rather than sudden understanding. Thorndike explained this with the Law of Effect, stated formally in 1911: responses followed by a satisfying state of affairs are \"stamped in\" (the stimulus-response connection is strengthened), and responses followed by an annoying state are \"stamped out\". He also proposed the laws of exercise and readiness, and his theory is called connectionism. In the 1930s he revised the law, concluding that punishment weakens responses far less than reward strengthens them. Everyday example: a child praised for tidying the room tidies more often, while one whose joke is met with silence stops telling it. Option c is right because the statement is almost word for word Thorndike's definition of the Law of Effect. Exam trap: this law is the forerunner of B. F. Skinner's principle of reinforcement, while \"proximity\" and \"similarity\" are Gestalt laws of perceptual grouping, not laws of learning.", "traps": {"a": "The law of similarity is a Gestalt principle of perceptual grouping (items that look alike are seen as belonging together) and also one of Aristotle's laws of association; it says nothing about consequences.", "b": "Clark L. Hull is known for drive-reduction theory and a hypothetico-deductive system of learning, not a \"law of proximity\"; proximity is a Gestalt grouping law in which items close together are seen as a group.", "d": "\"None of these\" is wrong because option c states the exact law described."}}, {"q": "With the help of ______, the behaviour of children can easily be modified", "concept": "The answer is b: with the help of operant conditioning, the behaviour of children can easily be modified. Operant conditioning, developed by B. F. Skinner from Edward L. Thorndike's Law of Effect, holds that behaviour is controlled by its consequences. Reinforcement, either positive (adding something pleasant) or negative (removing something unpleasant), makes a behaviour more likely; punishment and extinction make it less likely. Because parents and teachers control many of the consequences in a child's life, these principles form the basis of behaviour modification and applied behaviour analysis. Common techniques include shaping (reinforcing successive approximations, as when a child with autism is rewarded first for eye contact, then for any sound, then for a word), token economies (stars or points exchanged for rewards, developed by Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin), the Premack principle (\"finish your homework, then you can play\"), time-out and response cost. Clinical example: a seven-year-old who refuses homework earns a sticker for each completed page and a weekend outing for ten stickers, and homework completion rises within weeks. Option b is right because changing voluntary behaviour through planned consequences is the core application of operant conditioning. Exam trap: classical conditioning also has uses with children, such as the bell-and-pad alarm for bedwetting, but it changes involuntary reflexive responses, not everyday voluntary behaviour.", "traps": {"a": "Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov) pairs stimuli to change reflexive, involuntary responses such as fear or bladder control; it has uses like the bell-and-pad for enuresis but is not the main tool for modifying children's behaviour.", "c": "\"Simple conditioning\" is not a recognised type of conditioning; it is a distractor.", "d": "\"None of these\" is wrong because operant conditioning is the standard answer."}}, {"q": "What technique is used by TAT?", "concept": "The answer is d: the TAT uses the projective technique. The Thematic Apperception Test was developed by Christiana D. Morgan and Henry A. Murray at the Harvard Psychological Clinic and first described in 1935. It has 31 cards: 30 pictures of ambiguous scenes and one blank card, on which the person imagines a picture. The examiner shows a selection of cards and asks for a story about each: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters think and feel, and how it ends. The projective hypothesis, named by Lawrence K. Frank in 1939, holds that when a stimulus is ambiguous, people fill it with their own needs, conflicts and attitudes. Murray read the stories through his need-press theory: the hero's needs (such as achievement or affiliation) and the environmental press acting on the hero. David McClelland later used TAT-type pictures to measure need for achievement, and Uma Chowdhury adapted the test for India. Clinical example: a client whose heroes are repeatedly abandoned and helpless may be revealing themes of loss and dependency. Option d is right because the TAT presents ambiguous material and infers hidden motives from what the person projects onto it. Exam trap: the TAT relies on apperception (interpreting through past experience), not mere perception, and the Children's Apperception Test of Leopold and Sonya Bellak uses animal figures.", "traps": {"a": "An inventory is a structured self-report questionnaire with fixed answers, such as the MMPI or the 16PF; the TAT has no fixed answers.", "b": "Interviewing is direct face-to-face questioning, structured or unstructured; the TAT is a story-telling test, though an inquiry may follow it.", "c": "\"Analytical\" is not a category of test technique; it may echo Carl Jung's analytical psychology or psychoanalysis, which inspired projective ideas but is not the TAT's method."}}, {"q": "Learned helplessness underlines psychological cases of ________.", "concept": "The answer is d: learned helplessness underlies psychological cases of depression. Martin E. P. Seligman and Steven F. Maier reported learned helplessness in 1967. Dogs first exposed to electric shocks they could not escape were later placed in a shuttle box where simply jumping a low barrier would end the shock. Instead of escaping, most lay down and whimpered; they had learned that their actions made no difference. Dogs that had not received uncontrollable shocks learned to escape quickly. In Helplessness (1975), Seligman proposed this as a model of depression: repeated uncontrollable bad events produce passivity, low motivation, sadness and difficulty seeing that one's actions can work. In 1978 Lyn Abramson, Seligman and John Teasdale reformulated the theory, arguing that depression follows when people explain bad events with internal, stable and global causes (\"It is my fault, it will always be like this, and it ruins everything\"). This later grew into hopelessness theory. Clinical example: a student who has failed several times despite studying hard stops attending classes, saying nothing she does will ever matter. Option d is right because learned helplessness is one of the classic psychological theories of depression. Exam trap: learned helplessness is about lack of control producing passivity; do not confuse it with frustration, which is goal-blocking that tends to produce aggression.", "traps": {"a": "Conflict is the clash of incompatible motives, classified by Kurt Lewin as approach-approach, approach-avoidance and avoidance-avoidance; it is not what learned helplessness explains.", "b": "Frustration is the blocking of a goal, which John Dollard and colleagues (1939) linked to aggression; helplessness concerns perceived lack of control, not a blocked goal.", "c": "Learned helplessness has also been linked to anxiety and PTSD, but its central, best-researched application is depression; the classic learning model of anxiety is conditioned fear."}}, {"q": "The concept of “Programmed learning” was introduced by", "concept": "The answer is a: Skinner introduced the concept of programmed learning. B. F. Skinner, after watching his daughter's arithmetic class in 1953, felt that a teacher could not reinforce each pupil's correct answers quickly enough. In his 1954 paper \"The Science of Learning and the Art of Teaching\" he applied operant conditioning to education through programmed instruction, delivered by teaching machines or programmed textbooks. The material is broken into small steps called frames. The learner makes an active response to each frame, receives immediate feedback that acts as reinforcement, and moves on at their own pace, with steps small enough that errors are rare. This is linear programming, since every learner follows the same sequence; Norman Crowder later developed branching programmes, which send a learner who errs to a remedial frame. Sidney Pressey had built an early teaching machine in the 1920s, but it served mainly for testing; Skinner supplied the reinforcement theory that made programmed learning a method. Everyday example: language apps that teach in short exercises, mark each answer instantly and unlock the next level are descendants of programmed instruction. Option a is right because programmed learning is Skinner's direct application of operant principles to teaching. Exam trap: Pressey built the first teaching machine and Thorndike imagined self-paced books, but the concept of programmed learning is credited to Skinner.", "traps": {"b": "Clark L. Hull built a hypothetico-deductive theory of learning around drive reduction and habit strength; he did not develop programmed instruction.", "c": "John B. Watson founded behaviourism in 1913 and ran the Little Albert study, but he did not create programmed learning.", "d": "Edward L. Thorndike gave the Law of Effect and shaped educational psychology, and he even foresaw self-paced instruction, but the programmed learning concept is credited to Skinner."}}, {"q": "Psychoanalytical theory about human personality was proposed by", "concept": "The answer is b: psychoanalytic theory of personality was proposed by Sigmund Freud. Freud (1856 to 1939), a Viennese neurologist, developed psychoanalysis from his work with hysterical patients, first with Josef Breuer in Studies on Hysteria (1895) and then in The Interpretation of Dreams (1900). He held that much of mental life is unconscious and that behaviour is driven by instincts, chiefly the sexual drive (libido) and later aggression. His topographic model divides the mind into conscious, preconscious and unconscious levels; his structural model (The Ego and the Id, 1923) divides personality into the pleasure-seeking id, the superego or internalised conscience, and the ego, which mediates between them and reality. Anxiety signals danger, and the ego protects itself with defence mechanisms such as repression and projection. Personality develops through the oral, anal, phallic, latency and genital psychosexual stages, and unresolved conflict at a stage can leave a fixation. His methods were free association, dream analysis and the study of slips of the tongue. Example: a man who calls his wife by his mother's name may, in Freudian terms, be revealing an unconscious wish. Option b is right because Freud founded and named psychoanalysis. Exam trap: Carl Jung's theory is analytical psychology and Alfred Adler's is individual psychology; both are psychodynamic, but only Freud's is psychoanalysis.", "traps": {"a": "Carl Gustav Jung broke with Freud in 1913 and founded analytical psychology, known for the collective unconscious, archetypes and introversion-extraversion.", "c": "B. F. Skinner was a radical behaviourist who explained personality as learned patterns of operant behaviour and rejected unconscious forces.", "d": "Alfred Adler left Freud's circle in 1911 and founded individual psychology, centred on inferiority feelings, striving for superiority, style of life and social interest."}}, {"q": "A Null Hypothesis is", "concept": "The answer is a: a null hypothesis is a hypothesis of no difference. The term was coined by Ronald A. Fisher in The Design of Experiments (1935), and Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson later paired it with the alternative hypothesis and defined Type I and Type II errors. The null hypothesis, written H0, states that there is no difference between groups, or no relationship between variables, in the population, so any difference seen in the sample is due to chance. For example, H0 may say that mean anxiety in a yoga group equals mean anxiety in a control group, or that the correlation between sleep and marks is zero. The alternative hypothesis (H1) states that a difference or relationship exists. A statistical test calculates how likely the observed data would be if H0 were true; if that probability (the p value) falls below the chosen level, usually .05, the researcher rejects H0 and accepts H1. Otherwise the researcher \"fails to reject\" H0, which is not the same as proving it true. Clinical example: in a trial of a new antidepressant, H0 says the drug and placebo produce the same improvement. Option a is right because \"no difference\" (or no relationship) is the standard definition of the null hypothesis. Exam trap: H0 sets the difference or relationship at zero, not the variable itself.", "traps": {"b": "\"Hypothesis of zero significance\" is not a real term; significance refers to the probability level at which H0 is rejected, not to the content of the hypothesis.", "c": "The null hypothesis sets the difference between groups or the correlation at zero, not the variable itself; anxiety scores do not become zero under H0.", "d": "\"None of the above\" is wrong because option a gives the standard definition."}}, {"q": "The advantages of Sampling is", "concept": "The answer is d: both a and b, time-saving and increased accuracy. Sampling means studying a part of a population to draw conclusions about the whole. In India, Prasanta Chandra Mahalanobis of the Indian Statistical Institute championed large-scale sample surveys and designed the National Sample Survey, showing that a well-drawn sample gives reliable estimates faster and more cheaply than a census. Standard texts list the advantages. Sampling saves time, because fewer units are studied and results come sooner. It saves money. It can be more accurate, because a few trained investigators collect data more carefully than an army of hasty ones, which reduces non-sampling errors such as careless recording. It allows more detailed study of each case, and it is the only option when the population is infinite or testing destroys what is tested, as in checking the life of light bulbs. Example: a psychologist estimating exam anxiety among a city's college students can survey a random sample of 1,000 instead of every student and finish in weeks, not years. Option d is right because it combines two genuine advantages of sampling. Exam trap: the accuracy gain comes from fewer non-sampling errors; sampling error itself exists only in samples. Key note: the coaching book itself says c (capital or cost saving) is also correct, but with no \"all of the above\" option, d is kept as the intended answer.", "traps": {"a": "Time-saving is a true advantage, but choosing it alone leaves out increased accuracy, which d includes.", "b": "Increased accuracy is a true advantage (trained staff make fewer non-sampling errors), but on its own it is incomplete compared with d.", "c": "Saving money (cost) is also a genuine advantage of sampling, as the coaching book admits; it is not keyed only because the combined option d covers a and b."}}, {"q": "The method of Randomization involves", "concept": "The answer is d: all of the above, since the lottery, Tippett's table of random digits and the coin method are all ways of randomizing. Randomization means letting chance alone decide which units enter a sample, or which participants go to which group, so that every unit has an equal and independent chance. Ronald A. Fisher made random assignment a cornerstone of experimental design because it spreads unknown extraneous variables evenly across groups. In the lottery or fishbowl method, names or numbers are written on identical slips, mixed and drawn blind. A table of random numbers is a long list of digits with no pattern; the first published table was by the British statistician L. H. C. Tippett in 1927, with 41,600 digits taken from census figures, later followed by tables from Fisher and Frank Yates and the RAND Corporation's million random digits. The paper spells the name \"Tippit\"; the correct spelling is Tippett. The coin method uses tosses (heads to the treatment group, tails to the control group) when there are two groups. Today computers generate random numbers. Clinical example: 40 patients with panic disorder can be assigned to CBT or a waitlist by tossing a coin for each. Option d is right because all three listed methods are recognised randomization techniques. Exam trap: random does not mean haphazard or convenient; picking whoever walks in first is not randomization.", "traps": {"a": "The lottery (fishbowl) method is a valid randomization technique, but it is only one of the three listed, so a alone is incomplete.", "b": "Tippett's table of random numbers (1927) is a valid randomization tool, but choosing it alone ignores the other two valid methods.", "c": "The coin method is valid for splitting participants into two groups, but it is only one of the correct options."}}, {"q": "Scientific methods are used in", "concept": "The answer is c: scientific methods are used both in research in the pure sciences and in social science research. The scientific method is a way of gaining knowledge that is systematic, empirical (based on observation), controlled, objective, public and self-correcting. Fred N. Kerlinger, in Foundations of Behavioral Research, described scientific research as systematic, controlled, empirical and critical investigation of hypothetical propositions about the relations among natural phenomena. He drew on Charles Sanders Peirce's four methods of knowing (tenacity, authority, a priori reasoning and science) to show that only science has built-in checks against error. The same steps apply in any field: identify a problem, review the literature, form a testable hypothesis, design the study, collect and analyse data, and draw conclusions that others can replicate. Psychology, sociology and economics use experiments, surveys, observation and statistics just as physics and chemistry use laboratory measurement. Social sciences face extra difficulties, such as human variability and ethical limits on control, but the logic of inquiry is the same. Example: a psychologist who tests whether mindfulness lowers exam anxiety by randomly assigning students to training or a waitlist follows the same reasoning as a chemist testing a catalyst. Option c is right because the scientific method is not tied to any subject matter. Exam trap: the word \"only\" in option a makes it false.", "traps": {"a": "This is false because of the word \"only\"; the scientific method is also used in psychology and the other social sciences.", "b": "Social science research does use the scientific method, but this option leaves out the pure sciences, so it is incomplete.", "d": "\"Neither\" is false because both kinds of research use the scientific method."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a type of Hypothesis?", "concept": "The answer is d: all of the above, since interrogative, declarative and directional are all listed as types (forms) of hypothesis. A hypothesis is a tentative, testable statement about the relationship between two or more variables; Fred N. Kerlinger called it a conjectural statement of the relation between variables. Many texts classify hypotheses by their written form. An interrogative or question-form hypothesis is phrased as a question: \"Is there a difference in anxiety between first-year and final-year students?\" A declarative hypothesis is a positive statement that a relationship or difference exists: \"There is a difference in anxiety between first-year and final-year students.\" A directional hypothesis also states which way the difference goes, for example \"First-year students will report higher anxiety than final-year students\", and is tested with a one-tailed test. A non-directional hypothesis predicts a difference without saying which way (two-tailed test), and the null hypothesis predicts no difference. Clinical example: a researcher who predicts that CBT will reduce depression scores more than supportive counselling has written a directional hypothesis. Option d is right because, in this classification, all three listed forms are recognised types. Exam trap: link directional with one-tailed and non-directional with two-tailed tests; do not reverse them. Key note: strict texts such as Kerlinger treat a question as a research problem rather than a hypothesis, but the paper follows the forms classification, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "The interrogative (question-form) hypothesis is a recognised form in many texts, but choosing it alone ignores the other two.", "b": "The declarative hypothesis, a statement that a relationship exists, is a valid type, but it is only one of the three listed.", "c": "The directional hypothesis, which states the direction of the expected difference and uses a one-tailed test, is valid but only one of the correct options."}}, {"q": "A non-probability sampling method is", "concept": "The answer is d: quota sampling is a non-probability sampling method. In probability sampling, every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of selection decided by a random process, which allows sampling error to be estimated. Probability methods include simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster and multistage sampling. In non-probability sampling, selection depends on the researcher's judgement or convenience, so each member's chance of selection is unknown. The main non-probability methods are convenience (accidental), purposive (judgement), quota and snowball sampling. In quota sampling, the researcher fixes how many people are needed from each subgroup, often in proportion to the population (say 60 women and 40 men, or a set number per age band), and fieldworkers then fill those quotas with whoever is available. It resembles stratified sampling but has no random selection within groups, so interviewers tend to choose approachable people and bias creeps in. A famous failure: quota-based polls in the 1948 United States presidential election predicted that Thomas Dewey would beat Harry Truman. Example: a market researcher in a mall who must interview 50 college students and 50 working adults and stops whoever passes is using quota sampling. Option d is right because quota sampling fills subgroup targets without random selection. Exam trap: quota sampling looks like stratified random sampling, but only stratified sampling selects randomly within each stratum.", "traps": {"a": "Simple random sampling is the basic probability method, in which every member has an equal and independent chance of selection, as with the lottery method.", "b": "Cluster sampling is a probability method in which natural groups such as schools or villages are randomly selected and then studied.", "c": "Systematic sampling is a probability method: every kth unit is taken from a list after a random start, so chance decides the starting point."}}, {"q": "For the population of a finite size, which sampling method is generally preferred?", "concept": "The answer is d: for a population of finite size, systematic sampling is generally preferred. In systematic sampling, the researcher lists the population, calculates a sampling interval k by dividing the population size (N) by the desired sample size (n), picks a random start between 1 and k, and then takes every kth unit. For example, to draw 100 students from a register of 2,000, k = 20; with a random start of 7, the sample is the 7th, 27th, 47th student and so on. The method needs a known, countable population and a complete sampling frame, which is exactly what a finite population provides; with an infinite or unlisted population, N and k cannot be fixed. It is quick, easy for fieldworkers, and spreads the sample evenly across the list. Its one danger is periodicity: if the list repeats a pattern that matches k, such as a hostel list where every fourth name is a room prefect, the sample can be badly biased. Clinical example: a hospital psychologist auditing 50 of 1,000 case files can take every 20th file from the record register. Option d is right because systematic sampling works best when the population is finite and listed. Exam trap: systematic sampling is a probability method because the start is random, even though some Indian texts call it a mixed method.", "traps": {"a": "Cluster sampling randomly selects whole groups (schools, villages) and suits large, scattered populations that have no complete list of individuals.", "b": "Purposive (judgement) sampling is a non-probability method in which the researcher hand-picks typical or informative cases; it is not the general choice for a finite population.", "c": "Area sampling is a form of cluster sampling in which geographic areas are the clusters, used for large territories where no list of individuals exists."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is related to the concept of intelligence ______", "concept": "The answer is a: cognition is the concept most closely related to intelligence. Cognition covers all the mental processes of knowing: attention, perception, memory, language, reasoning, problem solving and decision making. Intelligence is the general ability to use these processes well. Charles Spearman (1904) found that people who do well on one mental test tend to do well on others and called this common factor g, general intelligence. David Wechsler defined intelligence as the global capacity to act purposefully, think rationally and deal effectively with the environment. Later models, such as Robert J. Sternberg's triarchic theory and the PASS model of J. P. Das and Jack Naglieri, explain intelligence through cognitive processes such as planning, attention and information processing. That is why intelligence tests are classed as measures of cognitive ability. Example: solving a Raven's Progressive Matrices item requires attending to patterns, holding rules in working memory and reasoning about the missing piece, all cognitive operations. Option a is right because intelligence is fundamentally a property of cognition, the umbrella term that contains perception, learning and memory. Exam trap: motivation affects test scores but is a non-cognitive (conative) factor, not intelligence itself. Key note: learning and perception are also linked to intelligence (Walter Dearborn even defined intelligence as the capacity to learn), but cognition is the broader term that includes them, so a is the best answer.", "traps": {"b": "Motivation is the force that energises and directs behaviour; it influences how hard someone tries on a test, but it is a non-cognitive factor, not part of intelligence itself.", "c": "Perception is one cognitive process that intelligence draws on, but it is narrower than cognition as a whole.", "d": "Learning is closely tied to intelligence, and Walter Dearborn defined intelligence as the capacity to learn, but learning is one cognitive process within the broader category of cognition."}}, {"q": "______ is a dimension of visual experience.", "concept": "The answer is d: brightness is a dimension of visual experience. Psychology separates the physical properties of a stimulus from the psychological dimensions we experience. Light is electromagnetic energy, and humans see wavelengths of roughly 380 to 760 nanometres. Its three physical properties map onto three visual dimensions: wavelength is experienced as hue (the colour name, such as red or blue), intensity or amplitude is experienced as brightness, and purity (how far a single wavelength dominates rather than a mixture) is experienced as saturation, the vividness of the colour. Sound has a parallel set: frequency is heard as pitch, amplitude as loudness, and complexity (the mix of overtones) as timbre. Everyday example: dimming a red lamp lowers its brightness while the hue stays red, and adding white light to that red turns it pink, which lowers saturation. A flute and a violin playing the same note at the same volume differ only in timbre. Option d is right because brightness is the visual dimension that corresponds to the intensity of light, while loudness, timbre and pitch all describe hearing. Exam trap: amplitude maps to brightness in vision but to loudness in hearing, and wavelength gives hue in vision while frequency gives pitch in hearing, so identify the sense before matching the property.", "traps": {"a": "Loudness is the auditory dimension that corresponds to the amplitude (intensity) of a sound wave, measured in decibels.", "b": "Timbre is the auditory quality that corresponds to a sound wave's complexity, which lets us tell a flute from a violin playing the same note.", "c": "Pitch is the auditory dimension that corresponds to the frequency of a sound wave, measured in hertz."}}, {"q": "The prototype of the modern intelligence test was developed by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the prototype of the modern intelligence test was developed by Binet. In 1904 the French Ministry of Public Instruction asked for a way to identify children who could not benefit from ordinary schooling and needed special classes. Alfred Binet, working with the physician Theodore Simon, rejected Francis Galton's approach of measuring reaction time and sensory acuity and instead tested higher mental processes such as judgement, comprehension and reasoning. The Binet-Simon scale of 1905 had 30 items arranged in order of difficulty. The 1908 revision grouped items by the age at which most children passed them, creating the concept of mental age, and a further revision followed in 1911, the year Binet died. Later work built on this prototype: William Stern proposed the intelligence quotient (mental age divided by chronological age), and Lewis M. Terman of Stanford University adapted the test as the Stanford-Binet (1916), multiplying the ratio by 100. Example: on the 1908 scale, a seven-year-old who passed the items most nine-year-olds pass had a mental age of nine. Option b is right because the Binet-Simon scale was the first practical intelligence test and the model for later tests. Exam trap: Terman popularised the IQ in America and Wechsler introduced the deviation IQ, but neither created the first test, and \"Stanford\" is a university, not a person.", "traps": {"a": "David Wechsler created the Wechsler-Bellevue scale (1939), later the WAIS and WISC, and introduced the deviation IQ, decades after Binet.", "c": "Lewis M. Terman revised Binet's scale as the Stanford-Binet (1916) and popularised the IQ, but he adapted an existing test rather than creating the prototype.", "d": "Stanford is the university where Terman worked; it lent its name to the Stanford-Binet but is not a person who developed a test."}}, {"q": "______ introduced the concept of adaptation.", "concept": "The answer is c: Harry Helson introduced the concept of adaptation level. Helson (1898 to 1977), an American psychologist, set out adaptation-level theory in the late 1940s and in his book Adaptation-Level Theory (1964). He argued that we never judge a stimulus in absolute terms. Instead, we carry a running neutral point, the adaptation level, formed by pooling three sources: the focal stimulus being judged, the background (contextual) stimuli around it, and residual stimuli from past experience. Anything above this level feels heavy, bright, hot or pleasant; anything below it feels light, dim, cold or unpleasant; stimuli near it feel neutral. Everyday example: after carrying a heavy suitcase, a school bag feels feather-light, and a 20 degree day feels chilly to someone from Chennai but warm to someone from Shimla. The idea later spread to motivation and well-being: Philip Brickman and Donald Campbell (1971) used it to describe the hedonic treadmill, in which a pay rise or a lottery win stops feeling special once the adaptation level catches up. Option c is right because adaptation level is Helson's signature concept. Exam trap: do not confuse Helson's adaptation level with sensory adaptation (receptors fading under an unchanging stimulus) or with Jean Piaget's adaptation (assimilation plus accommodation).", "traps": {"a": "Wolfgang Kohler (spelled Kohler in the paper) was a Gestalt psychologist known for insight learning, shown in his chimpanzee studies on Tenerife (The Mentality of Apes); he did not propose adaptation-level theory.", "b": "No psychologist named Brown is linked to the concept of adaptation; the Browns in exam syllabi are Roger Brown (language development, tip-of-the-tongue and flashbulb memory research) and John Brown of the Brown-Peterson short-term memory task.", "d": "Max Wertheimer founded Gestalt psychology with his 1912 paper on the phi phenomenon (apparent motion) and described the laws of perceptual grouping, not adaptation level."}}, {"q": "Which one part of the brain relays the color, smell, and feel of the flowers?", "concept": "The answer is a: the thalamus relays the colour, smell and feel of the flowers. The thalamus is a paired, egg-shaped mass of grey matter in the diencephalon, sitting on top of the brainstem on either side of the third ventricle. It is called the brain's relay station, or gateway to the cortex, because incoming sensory signals stop at one of its nuclei before being passed to the right area of cerebral cortex. Vision travels through the lateral geniculate nucleus to the occipital lobe, hearing through the medial geniculate nucleus to the temporal lobe, and touch, pain and temperature through the ventral posterior nuclei to the somatosensory cortex of the parietal lobe. So when you look at a rose and hold it, both the colour and the soft feel of the petals pass through the thalamus. Option a is right because it is the classic textbook sensory relay, and the only relay station among the options. Exam trap: smell is the famous exception; olfactory signals go from the olfactory bulb straight to the olfactory (piriform) cortex and amygdala without first synapsing in the thalamus. Key note: because smell largely bypasses the thalamus, the question is loosely worded, but the key stands: a secondary olfactory route does pass through the mediodorsal thalamus to the orbitofrontal cortex, and no other option is a sensory relay.", "traps": {"b": "Acetylcholine is a neurotransmitter, not a part of the brain; it acts at the neuromuscular junction, in the parasympathetic system and in memory circuits that degenerate in Alzheimer's disease.", "c": "The motor area (primary motor cortex in the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe) sends commands out to the muscles; it handles output, not incoming sensations like colour, smell or touch.", "d": "None of these is wrong because the thalamus is the textbook sensory relay; it only tempts a student who over-applies the fact that smell bypasses the thalamus."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a treatment developed from classical conditioning principles?", "concept": "The answer is a: token economies are not built on classical conditioning; they come from operant conditioning. Classical conditioning, discovered by Ivan Pavlov, is learning by association: a neutral stimulus paired with one that already triggers a reflex comes to trigger a similar response on its own. Several therapies apply this to emotional responses. Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe (1958), pairs relaxation with a graded fear hierarchy so that relaxation replaces anxiety (counterconditioning, or reciprocal inhibition). Flooding exposes the person to the feared stimulus at full strength, with escape blocked, until the conditioned fear extinguishes. Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour, such as drinking alcohol, with an unpleasant stimulus like a nausea-inducing drug so that the behaviour itself becomes aversive. A token economy works differently: it uses consequences. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin ran one on a psychiatric ward, described in The Token Economy (1968): patients earned tokens (generalised secondary reinforcers) for self-care and ward jobs and exchanged them for privileges, exactly as B. F. Skinner's operant principles predict. A child earning stars for homework that buy screen time is a token economy at home. Option a is right because tokens strengthen behaviour through reinforcement after the response, not through stimulus pairing. Exam trap: ask whether the technique changes what follows a behaviour (operant) or what a stimulus is paired with (classical).", "traps": {"b": "Flooding is a classical conditioning (extinction) technique: prolonged, full-intensity exposure to the conditioned fear stimulus without the feared outcome lets the fear response die out; Thomas Stampfl's implosive therapy is its imaginal version.", "c": "Aversion therapy is classical conditioning: an unwanted behaviour or stimulus (alcohol, a fetish object) is paired with an aversive unconditioned stimulus such as a nausea-producing drug or mild shock until it evokes discomfort.", "d": "Systematic desensitisation is Joseph Wolpe's counterconditioning technique, pairing relaxation with a graded fear hierarchy, so it rests squarely on classical conditioning."}}, {"q": "Parts of the autonomic nervous system are ______", "concept": "The answer is a: the two parts of the autonomic nervous system are the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions. The nervous system splits into the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system. The peripheral system has a somatic branch, which carries sensation and voluntary movement of skeletal muscles, and an autonomic branch, which runs the heart, glands, blood vessels and the smooth muscle of the gut largely without conscious control. The British physiologist John Newport Langley named the autonomic nervous system in 1898. Its sympathetic division, arising from the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord, prepares the body for action in what Walter B. Cannon called the fight-or-flight response: the heart races, pupils dilate, digestion slows and glucose is released. The parasympathetic division, arising from the brainstem and sacral cord, does the rest-and-digest work: it slows the heart, constricts the pupils and restarts digestion. Picture a near-miss on a scooter: the pounding heart is sympathetic, and the slow calming afterwards is parasympathetic. Option a is right because these two divisions are the standard textbook parts of the autonomic system. Exam trap: voluntary versus involuntary is the split between somatic and autonomic, not a division within the autonomic system; some modern texts add a third, enteric division in the gut wall, but exam keys expect sympathetic and parasympathetic.", "traps": {"b": "Voluntary versus involuntary describes the difference between the somatic nervous system (voluntary skeletal muscle) and the autonomic nervous system as a whole, not the parts within the autonomic system.", "c": "The thalamus and hypothalamus are the two main parts of the diencephalon; the hypothalamus controls the autonomic system, but both belong to the central nervous system.", "d": "Cerebrospinal and cortical are not autonomic divisions at all: cerebrospinal refers to the brain and spinal cord (the central nervous system) and cortical to the cerebral cortex."}}, {"q": "The concept of introversion and extroversion was advanced by", "concept": "The answer is d: Carl Gustav Jung advanced the concept of introversion and extraversion. In Psychological Types (1921) Jung, the Swiss psychiatrist who founded analytical psychology, described two attitudes, or directions of psychic energy (libido). In extraversion, energy flows outward to people, objects and events; in introversion, it flows inward to thoughts, feelings and the inner world. Everyone has both, but one usually dominates while the other stays in the unconscious. Jung crossed the two attitudes with four functions (thinking, feeling, sensation, intuition) to get eight psychological types, the basis of the later Myers-Briggs Type Indicator. Example: after a long party, an extravert feels recharged, while an introvert needs a quiet evening alone to recover. Hans Eysenck later turned extraversion-introversion into a measurable dimension tied to cortical arousal, but he borrowed it from Jung. Option d is right because Jung coined and first systematically developed the two attitudes. Exam trap: students pick Eysenck because his tests (EPI, EPQ) measure extraversion; remember that Jung introduced the concept and Eysenck measured it. Jung spelled it \"extraversion\"; \"extroversion\" is the popular spelling. The paper also misspells two names: \"Eysenc\" is Hans Eysenck and \"Kretshmer\" is Ernst Kretschmer.", "traps": {"a": "Eduard Spranger, a German philosopher, classified people into six value types (theoretical, economic, aesthetic, social, political, religious), the basis of the Allport-Vernon-Lindzey Study of Values, not introversion and extraversion.", "b": "Hans Eysenck made extraversion-introversion one of his superfactors and linked it to cortical arousal, but he adopted the concept from Jung rather than advancing it first.", "c": "Ernst Kretschmer linked body build to temperament in Physique and Character (1921): pyknic with cyclothymic mood, asthenic (leptosome) with schizothymic withdrawal, plus athletic and dysplastic types."}}, {"q": "Dependence, passivity, and greediness are regarded as traits found in the:", "concept": "The answer is d: dependence, passivity and greediness are traits of the oral character. Sigmund Freud proposed that personality develops through psychosexual stages, each centred on a body zone, and that too much or too little gratification at a stage leaves a fixation that shows up as adult traits. The first is the oral stage, from birth to about 18 months, when pleasure comes from sucking, swallowing and biting and the central issue is feeding and weaning. Karl Abraham, Freud's colleague, split it into two modes. The early oral-incorporative (oral-receptive) mode produces an adult who is dependent, passive, gullible (\"swallows\" anything) and greedy for food, drink, smoking, attention and reassurance. The later oral-aggressive (oral-sadistic) mode, linked with teething, produces sarcasm, biting remarks and argumentativeness. A clinical example is a client who constantly seeks reassurance, overeats under stress and expects others to take care of everything. Freud also linked alcohol dependence to oral fixation. Option d is right because passive dependence and greed are the textbook oral-receptive traits. Exam trap: the anal character is about control (orderliness, stinginess and stubbornness), not neediness; also note that \"oral character\" names an adult type, while the \"genital stage\" and \"phallic stage\" options name stages rather than character types.", "traps": {"a": "The anal character, from fixation at about 18 months to 3 years around toilet training, shows orderliness, parsimony and obstinacy if anal-retentive, or messiness and defiance if anal-expulsive, not dependence and greed.", "b": "The genital stage begins at puberty and marks mature, other-directed sexuality; it is the healthy endpoint, not a fixation that produces dependent traits.", "c": "The phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) centres on the Oedipus complex; phallic fixation is linked with vanity, pride, recklessness and exhibitionism, not passivity."}}, {"q": "Psychosomatic disorders are best illustrated by:", "concept": "The answer is a: asthma caused by anxiety best illustrates a psychosomatic disorder. A psychosomatic (psychophysiological) disorder is a real physical illness, with actual tissue damage or measurable bodily change, that is caused or worsened by psychological factors such as stress, conflict or emotion. This differs from conversion and somatic symptom disorders, where there is no matching organic damage. Franz Alexander, a founder of psychosomatic medicine, wrote Psychosomatic Medicine (1950) and named seven classic examples, the \"holy seven\": bronchial asthma, peptic ulcer, essential hypertension, rheumatoid arthritis, ulcerative colitis, neurodermatitis and thyrotoxicosis. He believed a specific unconscious conflict acted on a vulnerable organ. Modern health psychology replaces this with a diathesis-stress view: stress hormones and autonomic arousal act on an organ that is already vulnerable. Example: a student with mild asthma has a severe attack on the morning of an entrance exam; the airway constriction is real and measurable, but anxiety triggered it. DSM-5-TR now codes this as psychological factors affecting other medical conditions. Option a is right because it pairs a real bodily disease (asthma) with a psychological cause (anxiety), the exact definition of a psychosomatic disorder. Exam trap: psychosomatic does not mean imaginary, and it is not conversion disorder; the illness is real, and only its trigger is psychological.", "traps": {"b": "Insomnia is a sleep-wake disorder; it can be stress-related, but it is a disturbance of sleep itself rather than a physical disease of an organ produced by emotion.", "c": "Neurotic anxiety is a psychological state (in Freud's terms, fear that id impulses will break through); it is a psychological symptom, not a physical illness produced by psychological factors.", "d": "Over-eating is a behaviour (and, if recurrent with loss of control, binge-eating disorder), not an organic disease; it can lead to obesity, but it does not illustrate the classic psychosomatic idea."}}, {"q": "Psychophysics is the study of the relationship between:", "concept": "The answer is a: psychophysics studies the relationship between sensory attributes (what we experience) and physical energy (the stimulus). Gustav Theodor Fechner, a German physicist and philosopher, founded the field in Elements of Psychophysics (1860). He wanted to measure the mind by finding lawful links between physical stimulus intensity, such as decibels of sound or grams of weight, and the strength of the sensation produced, such as loudness or heaviness. Building on Ernst Heinrich Weber's finding that the just noticeable difference is a constant fraction of the original stimulus (Weber's law), Fechner proposed that sensation grows with the logarithm of intensity (S = k log I). He also devised the classic methods for measuring thresholds: limits, constant stimuli and adjustment. Later, Stanley Smith Stevens offered a power law, and signal detection theory separated sensitivity from response bias. Everyday example: one candle lit in a dark room is obvious, but the same candle added to a brightly lit room goes unnoticed, because perceived brightness depends on the ratio of change, not the absolute amount. Option a is right because it states the textbook definition: the quantitative relation between physical stimuli and the sensations they produce. Exam trap: psychophysics is not \"psychology plus physics\", and the link between experience and brain processes belongs to physiological psychology, not psychophysics.", "traps": {"b": "Psychology and physics is a literal reading of the word; psychophysics is not a blend of two sciences but the measurement of how sensation relates to stimulus energy.", "c": "The relation between perception and physiological processes is the domain of physiological psychology and neuroscience (for example, which cortical area codes colour), not psychophysics.", "d": "None of these is wrong because option a gives the standard definition of psychophysics."}}, {"q": "Convergence of the eyes is controlled primarily by:", "concept": "The answer is a: convergence of the eyes is controlled primarily by the extrinsic (extraocular) muscles of the eye. Each eye is moved by six extraocular muscles attached to the outside of the eyeball: four recti (superior, inferior, medial and lateral) and two obliques. When you look at something close, such as a phone held near your face, both eyes rotate inward so that the image falls on corresponding points of both retinas. This inward turning is convergence, produced mainly by contraction of the two medial rectus muscles, which are supplied by the oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve III). Feedback about how far the eyes have turned also serves as a binocular depth cue: the more the eyes converge, the nearer the object. Convergence is one part of the near response (near triad), alongside accommodation of the lens and constriction of the pupil, and the wrong options in this question come from those other two parts. Option a is right because only the extraocular muscles physically rotate the eyeballs; the paper's \"extrinsic muscle\" means exactly these. Exam trap: accommodation and convergence both happen when you look at a near object, so students mix them up; convergence is the eyes turning (extrinsic muscles), while accommodation is the lens changing shape (the intrinsic ciliary muscle).", "traps": {"b": "The ciliary muscle is an intrinsic eye muscle that changes the shape of the lens for focusing (accommodation); it does not rotate the eyeball.", "c": "Curvature of the lens is the outcome of accommodation, which focuses near images on the retina; it is a monocular cue and plays no part in turning the eyes inward.", "d": "The muscles of the iris (sphincter and dilator pupillae) control pupil size in response to light and near viewing; they do not move the eyes."}}, {"q": "The primary emotion includes:", "concept": "The answer is b: joy, fear, anger and sadness are primary emotions. Primary (basic) emotions are thought to be innate, universal across cultures, present early in infancy, and linked to distinct facial expressions and survival functions. Secondary emotions are blends, or later emotions that depend on self-awareness and social learning. Theorists give slightly different lists, but joy, fear, anger and sadness appear on all of them. Paul Ekman and Wallace V. Friesen found six expressions recognised worldwide, even among the isolated Fore people of Papua New Guinea: happiness, sadness, fear, anger, surprise and disgust. Robert Plutchik's wheel of emotions (1980) has eight primaries in opposing pairs: joy and sadness, trust and disgust, fear and anger, surprise and anticipation. Michael Lewis's developmental research places these primary emotions within the first six months of life, while the self-conscious emotions come later: embarrassment, envy and empathy at about 18 to 24 months, once the child recognises itself, and pride, shame and guilt at about 2 and a half to 3 years, once the child has learned standards and rules. Example: a six-month-old shows clear anger when a toy is taken away but cannot yet feel guilty about breaking it. Option b is right because all four of its emotions are on every major list of primary emotions. Exam trap: shame and guilt sound basic but are self-conscious, secondary emotions (although Carroll Izard did include them among his ten fundamental emotions), and love and hate are complex sentiments, not primaries.", "traps": {"a": "Pain is a bodily sensation rather than an emotion, and love and hate are complex, enduring sentiments built from several emotions; only fear here is a primary emotion.", "c": "Shame and guilt are self-conscious (secondary) emotions that need self-awareness and learned standards, and grief is a complex response to loss; only anger here is primary.", "d": "All of the above fails because options a and c each contain items that are not primary emotions."}}, {"q": "An insufficient supply of thyroid hormone during pregnancy can result in:", "concept": "The answer is c: an insufficient supply of thyroid hormone during pregnancy can result in cretinism. The thyroid gland in the neck makes thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), which need iodine and set the body's metabolic rate. Before and just after birth these hormones are also essential for brain growth, myelination and skeletal development. If the mother is severely iodine deficient, or the foetus's own thyroid fails (congenital hypothyroidism), the child develops cretinism: stunted growth, coarse facial features, a large tongue, delayed motor milestones and intellectual disability. Today the condition is called congenital hypothyroidism or congenital iodine deficiency syndrome, and it is one of the most preventable causes of intellectual disability; iodised salt programmes, including India's, and newborn screening with prompt thyroxine treatment largely prevent it. Example: a baby found at birth to have low thyroid hormone who starts daily thyroxine within the first weeks can develop normally, while an untreated child suffers permanent cognitive impairment. In adults, thyroid deficiency produces myxoedema, with sluggishness, weight gain, cold intolerance and depressed mood. Option c is right because cretinism is the classic developmental result of thyroid hormone deficiency in foetal and early life. Exam trap: excess thyroid hormone (hyperthyroidism, as in Graves' disease) raises metabolism and arousal, so options a and b describe the opposite condition, not deficiency.", "traps": {"a": "Increased metabolic rate results from too much thyroid hormone (hyperthyroidism), not too little; deficiency lowers metabolism.", "b": "Increased emotional arousal, nervousness and irritability are signs of hyperthyroidism; hypothyroidism tends to produce sluggishness and low mood.", "d": "None of these is wrong because cretinism is the standard consequence of thyroid hormone deficiency in foetal life."}}, {"q": "The so-called “master gland,” which helps control the activities of various ductless glands is the:", "concept": "The answer is b: the anterior pituitary is called the master gland. The pituitary is a pea-sized gland hanging by a stalk from the base of the brain, just below the hypothalamus. Its front part, the anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis), releases tropic hormones that switch on other endocrine (ductless) glands: thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) drives the thyroid, adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) drives the adrenal cortex, and follicle-stimulating and luteinising hormones (FSH and LH) drive the ovaries and testes. It also makes growth hormone and prolactin. Because so many glands wait for its signals, it earned the nickname master gland. The posterior pituitary, by contrast, only stores and releases oxytocin and vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone) made in the hypothalamus. Modern texts add a twist: the pituitary itself takes orders from the hypothalamus through releasing hormones, so the hypothalamus is sometimes called the master of the master gland. In stress, for example, the hypothalamus releases CRH, the anterior pituitary releases ACTH, and the adrenal cortex releases cortisol: the HPA axis. Option b is right because the anterior pituitary is the part whose tropic hormones control the other ductless glands. Exam trap: the adrenal medulla is famous for fight-or-flight hormones and the thyroid for metabolism, but neither controls other glands, and \"endocrine glands\" is just the name of the whole category.", "traps": {"a": "The adrenal medulla secretes adrenaline (epinephrine) and noradrenaline for the fight-or-flight response under sympathetic control; it does not regulate other glands.", "c": "The thyroid secretes thyroxine to set metabolic rate; it is itself controlled by the anterior pituitary's TSH rather than controlling other glands.", "d": "Endocrine glands is simply the name for all ductless glands, so it cannot be the single gland that controls the others."}}, {"q": "One of the following is not a method of measuring retention:", "concept": "The answer is d: reminiscence is not a method of measuring retention. Retention is how much of what was learned is still stored, and psychologists measure it in three classic ways, each more sensitive than the last. Recall asks the person to reproduce the material with few or no cues, as in an essay answer or a fill-in-the-blank. Recognition asks the person to pick out the learned item from alternatives, as in a multiple-choice test or an identity parade, and usually shows more retention than recall. Relearning, the savings method, was devised by Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885): he learned lists of nonsense syllables, relearned them later and expressed the time saved as a percentage. Savings appear even when nothing can be recalled, so relearning is the most sensitive measure. Some texts add reconstruction (putting items back in their original order). Reminiscence, by contrast, is a phenomenon, not a method: it is the improvement in recall after a delay without any extra practice, first reported by Philip B. Ballard (1913) with schoolchildren who recalled more of a poem two days later than immediately after learning it. Option d is right because reminiscence is something retention measures can reveal, not a way of measuring retention. Exam trap: do not confuse it with reminiscence therapy for older adults or the reminiscence bump in autobiographical memory; none of these is a retention method.", "traps": {"a": "Recognition is a standard retention measure: the learner identifies previously learned items among distractors, as in multiple-choice questions.", "b": "Recall is a standard retention measure: the learner reproduces the material from memory with minimal cues, whether free, serial or cued.", "c": "Relearning (Hermann Ebbinghaus's savings method) measures retention by how much less time or how many fewer trials it takes to learn the material a second time."}}, {"q": "When new acquisitions improve retention of the old, that is:", "concept": "The answer is c: when new learning improves retention of the old, that is reproductive facilitation. The stem is taken almost word for word from David Krech and Richard S. Crutchfield's Elements of Psychology (1958): when new acquisitions improve retention of the old, the term reproductive facilitation is applied, and when the effect is negative, reproductive interference. Transfer of training is the effect that learning one task has on learning or remembering another. It is positive when one task helps the other, negative when it hinders, and zero when there is no effect. Edward L. Thorndike and Robert S. Woodworth (1901) explained transfer by identical elements: the more two tasks share, the more transfer occurs. Transfer can run forward or backward in time. Forward effects are proactive: old learning helping new learning (proactive facilitation) or hindering it (proactive interference). Backward effects are retroactive: new learning helping retention of the old (retroactive facilitation, which Krech and Crutchfield call reproductive facilitation) or hindering it (retroactive interference, their reproductive interference). Example: a student who takes up Spanish may find her school French improving, because the new learning rehearses shared vocabulary and grammar. Option c is right because it is the textbook name for exactly this effect: new learning helping the retention of old learning. Exam trap: positive transfer sounds right too, since the effect is helpful, but it is the broad name for any learning that helps other learning, usually earlier learning making new learning easier; when new learning acts back on the retention of old learning, the answer is reproductive (retroactive) facilitation. Key note: the coaching book keys a, positive transfer; c is used here because the stem is Krech and Crutchfield's own definition of reproductive facilitation.", "traps": {"a": "Positive transfer is the broad name for any learning that helps other learning, usually earlier learning making new learning easier; the specific textbook term for new learning improving retention of the old is reproductive (retroactive) facilitation.", "b": "Negative transfer is when one learning interferes with another (for example, an old driving habit on a car with controls in different places); it impairs rather than improves retention.", "d": "Reproductive interference means interference at the reproduction (recall) stage, such as competing responses blocking the old memory; it is a harmful effect, the opposite of what the question describes."}}, {"q": "Which of the following illnesses caused by long-term abuse of alcohol often involves profound retrograde amnesia?", "concept": "The answer is d: Korsakoff's disease (Korsakoff's syndrome) is the alcohol-related illness with profound retrograde amnesia. The Russian neuropsychiatrist Sergei Korsakoff described it in the 1880s in long-term heavy drinkers. Chronic alcohol abuse, with poor diet and impaired absorption, causes thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency. The acute phase is Wernicke's encephalopathy, described by Carl Wernicke, with confusion, ataxia and eye-movement problems (ophthalmoplegia, nystagmus). If thiamine is not given quickly it can progress to Korsakoff's syndrome, so the two are often named together as Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. Damage centres on the mammillary bodies and the mediodorsal nucleus of the thalamus. The memory picture is severe anterograde amnesia (inability to form new memories) plus extensive retrograde amnesia with a temporal gradient: recent decades are lost more than early life. Patients often confabulate, filling gaps with invented but sincere stories, and lack insight. Example: a man in his fifties insists he still works at a job he left years ago and cheerfully describes a meeting there \"this morning\". Immediate memory and general intelligence are relatively spared. Option d is right because Korsakoff's is the only option caused by alcohol abuse, and its retrograde amnesia is classically profound. Exam trap: Korsakoff's is not a dementia like Alzheimer's; it is an amnestic disorder caused by a vitamin deficiency, and early thiamine can prevent it.", "traps": {"a": "Alzheimer's disease, described by Alois Alzheimer in 1906, is a progressive dementia with amyloid plaques and neurofibrillary tangles; it is not caused by alcohol abuse.", "b": "Huntington's disease is an autosomal dominant genetic disorder (an expanded CAG repeat on chromosome 4) causing chorea, psychiatric symptoms and dementia; it has nothing to do with alcohol.", "c": "Parkinson's disease, described by James Parkinson in 1817, results from loss of dopamine neurons in the substantia nigra and causes tremor, rigidity and slowed movement; it is not alcohol-induced."}}, {"q": "What did Chomsky not argue about language development?", "concept": "The answer is b: Noam Chomsky did not argue that language development is based on reinforced learning. That was B. F. Skinner's position in Verbal Behavior (1957), which explained speech as operant behaviour shaped by reinforcement and imitation. Chomsky's 1959 review of that book attacked it and helped launch the cognitive revolution. Chomsky, an American linguist, argued that children are born with a language acquisition device (LAD), an innate, biologically wired capacity specialised for language. Within it lies universal grammar: principles shared by all human languages, such as nouns, verbs and rules for combining them, with experience simply setting the parameters of the local language. His key evidence is the poverty of the stimulus: children hear incomplete, error-filled speech, yet rapidly master complex grammar they were never taught or reinforced for. Children's errors point the same way: a three-year-old who says \"I goed\" or \"two mouses\" has never heard those forms, so the child must be applying an internal rule (overregularisation) rather than copying reinforced examples. Chomsky also treated language as a distinct mental faculty, separate from general intelligence, which fits the idea of an innate language module. Option b is right because reinforcement-based learning is the behaviourist view Chomsky rejected. Exam trap: in \"not\" questions students pick the least familiar wording; here the three Chomskyan options are just different phrasings of nativism, and only reinforcement belongs to Skinner.", "traps": {"a": "Universal grammar is Chomsky's central claim: all languages share deep structural principles that are built into the human mind.", "c": "Chomsky did argue that language is genetically wired, through the innate language acquisition device that matures in every normal child.", "d": "An innate, specialised language faculty separate from general cognition is part of Chomsky's view (later generalised in Jerry Fodor's The Modularity of Mind, 1983), so it is something he did argue."}}, {"q": "What are the two types of declarative memory given by Edel Tulving?", "concept": "The answer is a: Endel Tulving divided declarative memory into episodic and semantic memory. The paper spells his name \"Edel\"; the correct spelling is Endel Tulving, an Estonian-born Canadian psychologist who proposed the distinction in 1972. Episodic memory stores personal experiences tied to a specific time and place: what you ate at your birthday dinner, or where you were when exam results came out. Tulving linked it to autonoetic consciousness, the sense of mentally travelling back in time. Semantic memory stores general facts and knowledge, independent of when you learned them: that Delhi is India's capital, what a neuron is, the meanings of words. Both are declarative (explicit) memory, because their contents can be consciously recalled and put into words. Larry Squire's taxonomy places declarative memory opposite non-declarative (implicit) memory, which includes procedural skills like riding a bicycle, priming and classical conditioning. The distinction matters clinically: after hippocampal damage and in early Alzheimer's disease, episodic memory fails first while much semantic knowledge survives, and the patient H.M. could not form new episodic memories yet still learned new motor skills such as mirror drawing. Option a is right because episodic and semantic are exactly the two kinds Tulving named. Exam trap: procedural memory is long-term but not declarative; it is the classic implicit memory, so any option pairing it with semantic memory is wrong.", "traps": {"b": "Reconstructive memory is Frederic Bartlett's idea (Remembering, 1932) that recall rebuilds the past using schemas, as in the War of the Ghosts study; it describes how recall works, not one of Tulving's declarative types.", "c": "Semantic memory is declarative, but procedural memory (skills and habits such as typing) is non-declarative or implicit, so this pair mixes the two branches.", "d": "Neither procedural nor reconstructive memory is a type of declarative memory: procedural is implicit skill memory and reconstruction is a process of recall."}}, {"q": "Which is not the component of Kluver-Bucy syndrome following the removal of anterior temporal lobes?", "concept": "The answer is a: rejection of anything edible is not a component of Kluver-Bucy syndrome; in fact the opposite happens. Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy (the paper writes \"Kluver\" without the umlaut) removed both anterior temporal lobes, including the amygdala, in rhesus monkeys in the late 1930s. The monkeys showed a striking cluster of changes. They became tame and lost their fear, approaching snakes and humans they would normally avoid, because the amygdala is central to fear. They showed hyperorality, putting every object in the mouth to explore it. They had psychic blindness (visual agnosia): they could see objects but not recognise what they meant. They showed hypermetamorphosis, compulsively attending to every new stimulus, and hypersexuality, sometimes directed at inappropriate objects or other species. Their diet changed: they ate indiscriminately, including meat and non-food objects that normal monkeys reject. In humans, a partial form appears after herpes simplex encephalitis, head injury or frontotemporal dementia, with placid emotion, mouthing of objects, overeating and disinhibited sexual behaviour. Option a is right because the syndrome brings increased, indiscriminate eating and mouthing of objects, not refusal of food. Exam trap: in this \"not\" question three options are genuine features, and the false one simply reverses the dietary change: the animals eat anything rather than reject food.", "traps": {"b": "Lack of fear (tameness, placidity) is a core feature, caused mainly by loss of the amygdala; the monkeys approached snakes and people without fear.", "c": "Investigating objects with the mouth is hyperorality, one of the defining features of the syndrome.", "d": "Increased and indiscriminate sexual activity (hypersexuality), including toward inappropriate objects or partners, is a classic component of the syndrome."}}, {"q": "Arrange the following tests in increasing order of their saturation on projective criteria.<br>1. Thematic Apperception test<br>2. Differential Aptitude Test<br>3. Rorschach Inkblot Test<br>4. Incomplete Sentence Blank", "concept": "The answer is d: 2-4-1-3, that is, Differential Aptitude Test, Incomplete Sentence Blank, Thematic Apperception Test, Rorschach. \"Saturation on projective criteria\" means how strongly a test has the features of a projective technique: an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus, minimal instructions, freedom of response and a disguised purpose. The projective hypothesis holds that the less structured the stimulus, the more people reveal their own needs, conflicts and ways of seeing. The Differential Aptitude Tests (George K. Bennett, Harold G. Seashore and Alexander G. Wesman, 1947) are objective, structured ability tests with right and wrong answers, so they have essentially no projective saturation. The Rotter Incomplete Sentences Blank (Julian Rotter and Janet Rafferty, 1950; the paper calls it the Incomplete Sentence Blank) gives the start of a sentence such as \"My father...\", so the response is partly guided and the purpose fairly transparent: a semi-structured projective. The Thematic Apperception Test (Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, 1935) shows ambiguous pictures of people and asks for a story, leaving much more room. The Rorschach (Hermann Rorschach, 1921) uses ten inkblots that depict nothing at all, making it the least structured and most projective. Option d is right because it runs from no projection to maximal projection. Exam trap: \"increasing order\" means least to most; option b is the correct sequence reversed.", "traps": {"a": "3-1-2-4 begins with the Rorschach, the most projective test, and then wrongly places the aptitude test between the TAT and sentence completion, so it follows no consistent order.", "b": "3-1-4-2 is the correct sequence in decreasing order (most to least projective), but the question asks for increasing order.", "c": "2-4-3-1 starts correctly with the aptitude test and sentence completion but puts the Rorschach before the TAT, although inkblots are less structured than TAT pictures of people."}}, {"q": "Biofeedback therapy is based on which of the following?", "concept": "The answer is b: biofeedback therapy is based on operant conditioning. In biofeedback, a device measures a bodily process we normally cannot sense, such as muscle tension (EMG), skin temperature, heart rate, blood pressure or brain waves (EEG), and turns it into an immediate signal: a tone, a light or a moving line on a screen. When the person shifts the process in the desired direction, the signal changes, and that change acts as a reinforcer. Over sessions the person learns to produce the change voluntarily, and eventually without the machine. The approach grew from the work of Neal E. Miller in the 1960s, who reported that rats could learn to change heart rate and other autonomic responses when those changes were rewarded, challenging the belief that only voluntary skeletal responses could be operantly conditioned (later replications were weaker, but clinical biofeedback continued). Clinical uses include EMG biofeedback for tension headache, thermal (hand-warming) biofeedback for migraine and Raynaud's disease, and EEG neurofeedback for ADHD. Example: a stressed student watches the tension reading from her forehead fall as she relaxes, and the falling tone rewards her success. Option b is right because the response (the physiological change) is shaped by its consequence, the feedback signal. Exam trap: because biofeedback works on involuntary, autonomic responses, students assume classical conditioning; but nothing is being paired, and the response is reinforced after it occurs.", "traps": {"a": "Classical conditioning pairs a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned stimulus to elicit a reflex; biofeedback involves no such pairing, only reinforcement of a response after it happens.", "c": "Observational learning (Albert Bandura) is learning by watching a model; in biofeedback the person learns from feedback about their own body, not from a model.", "d": "Cognitive learning, such as latent learning or insight, centres on mental representations; biofeedback may involve cognition, but its defining mechanism is reinforcing feedback, an operant principle."}}, {"q": "According to Sigmund Freud, different types of anxiety are:<br>1. Neurotic Anxiety<br>2. State Anxiety<br>3. Moral Anxiety<br>4. Reality Anxiety", "concept": "The answer is b: 1, 3 and 4 only; Sigmund Freud described neurotic, moral and reality anxiety. In his structural model the ego must serve three masters: the outside world, the id and the superego. Anxiety, which Freud in Inhibitions, Symptoms and Anxiety (1926) treated as the ego's danger signal, arises when any one of them threatens the ego, so there are three types. Reality (objective) anxiety is fear of a real danger in the outside world, such as a snarling dog or a speeding truck; it is useful and fades when the threat passes. Neurotic anxiety arises from conflict between the id and the ego: the unconscious fear that one's sexual or aggressive impulses will break through and bring punishment. Moral anxiety arises from conflict between the ego and the superego: guilt or shame at doing, or even thinking of, something that violates one's conscience. Example: a student who has cheated feels moral anxiety, while one who becomes tense without knowing why whenever angry at a parent may be showing neurotic anxiety. Anxiety then sets defence mechanisms such as repression in motion. Option b is right because neurotic, moral and reality anxiety are the three Freud named. Exam trap: state anxiety comes from Charles Spielberger's state-trait theory and his State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (STAI); it is not Freudian, so any option containing 2 is wrong.", "traps": {"a": "1, 2 and 3 includes state anxiety, Charles Spielberger's term for temporary anxiety in a particular situation, and leaves out reality anxiety, which Freud did describe.", "c": "1, 2 and 4 includes state anxiety, which is not Freudian, and omits moral anxiety, the ego-superego conflict that Freud named.", "d": "2, 3 and 4 drops neurotic anxiety, the most clinically important Freudian type, and adds Spielberger's state anxiety."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a cause of forgetting?", "concept": "The answer is b: selective attention is not a cause of forgetting. Forgetting is the loss of, or inability to retrieve, information that was once stored, and the main explanations are these. Decay theory, traced to Edward L. Thorndike's law of disuse, holds that memory traces fade with time if unused. Interference theory holds that other learning gets in the way: proactive interference when old learning disrupts new, and retroactive interference when new learning disrupts old. Retrieval failure, studied by Endel Tulving, holds that the memory is still there but the right cue is missing. Retrieval inhibition, or retrieval-induced forgetting, shown by Michael C. Anderson, Robert A. Bjork and Elizabeth Ligon Bjork (1994), holds that practising some items suppresses related ones. Freud's motivated forgetting (repression) and consolidation failure complete the usual list. Selective attention, studied by Donald Broadbent with his filter model (1958), is an encoding process: choosing which input to process and ignoring the rest. Information you never attended to was never stored, so it cannot be forgotten. Option b is right because selective attention operates before storage, not on stored memories. Exam trap: some texts list \"encoding failure\" as a reason we cannot remember, as when you cannot draw a coin you have handled thousands of times; that is a failure to store, and selective attention itself is not a theory of forgetting.", "traps": {"a": "Interference is a major cause of forgetting: proactive interference (old learning blocks new) and retroactive interference (new learning blocks old).", "c": "Decay, related to Thorndike's law of disuse, explains forgetting as the fading of unused memory traces over time.", "d": "Retrieval inhibition (retrieval-induced forgetting) is a documented cause of forgetting: retrieving some items suppresses access to related, competing items."}}, {"q": "Awareness of one's own thought processes enabling effective learning through correction is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: awareness of one's own thought processes, used to learn more effectively by correcting oneself, is metacognition. The American developmental psychologist John H. Flavell introduced the term in the 1970s (key paper 1979), describing it as \"thinking about thinking\". It has two parts. Metacognitive knowledge is what you know about your own mind, tasks and strategies: for example, that you remember diagrams better than lists. Metacognitive regulation is the active use of that knowledge: planning how to study, monitoring whether you understand, and correcting your approach when you do not. Its memory part, metamemory, includes the feeling of knowing and judging how well you have learned something. Metacognition improves through childhood and adolescence and predicts academic success. Example: while preparing for an entrance exam, you notice you keep getting psychophysics questions wrong, realise that rereading notes is not working, and switch to self-testing with past papers; noticing and fixing the problem is metacognition in action. In clinical work, Adrian Wells's metacognitive therapy targets beliefs about worrying itself, such as \"worry keeps me safe\". Option b is right because the question describes metacognition exactly: monitoring and correcting one's own thinking. Exam trap: metacognition is not ordinary cognition or general intelligence; \"meta\" means one level up, so it is cognition about cognition.", "traps": {"a": "An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure that guarantees a solution if followed correctly, like a mathematical formula; it is a problem-solving method, not awareness of one's own thinking.", "c": "Analogies solve a new problem by applying the solution of a similar, familiar one; this is a problem-solving strategy, not self-monitoring.", "d": "Constructive process refers to memory or perception actively building and filling in information (as in Frederic Bartlett's reconstructive memory), not awareness of one's own thought."}}, {"q": "How many subjects would be required in a 3 × 3×2 factorial design with ten subjects per cell?", "concept": "The answer is d: 180 subjects. A factorial design studies two or more independent variables (factors) at once, crossing every level of each factor with every level of the others. The notation tells you the structure: each number is one factor, and its value is that factor's number of levels. So 3 × 3 × 2 means three factors, two with three levels each and one with two levels. The number of cells (conditions) is the product of the levels: 3 × 3 × 2 = 18. With ten subjects in each cell, the total is 18 × 10 = 180. Example: a researcher studies anxiety reduction by therapy type (CBT, mindfulness, waiting list), session frequency (weekly, twice weekly, daily) and gender (male, female); that gives 18 combinations, and with ten participants in each, 180 people are needed. Factorial designs, analysed with factorial analysis of variance (ANOVA), are efficient because one study tests every main effect and every interaction. Option d is right because you multiply the levels to get the cells and then multiply by the subjects per cell. Exam trap: students add the levels (3 + 3 + 2 = 8) or stop at the number of cells (18, option c). The calculation assumes a between-subjects design, the standard reading, in which each participant serves in only one cell.", "traps": {"a": "10 is the number of subjects in one cell; it would be the total only if every participant served in all 18 conditions (a fully within-subjects design), which the question does not suggest.", "b": "90 multiplies only part of the design (3 × 3 × 10), ignoring the third factor with two levels.", "c": "18 is the number of cells (3 × 3 × 2), not the number of subjects; it still has to be multiplied by ten subjects per cell."}}, {"q": "In an experiment, if a light is repeatedly presented just before the electric shock is given the animal learns:", "concept": "The answer is a: the animal learns operant escape first and then operant avoidance. This is the classic signalled (discriminated) avoidance experiment, usually run in a shuttle box with two compartments. A warning light comes on, and a few seconds later the floor delivers a shock. On early trials the animal ignores the light; when the shock arrives, it scrambles over the barrier, and that response ends the shock. This is escape learning: the response terminates an aversive stimulus already present and is strengthened by negative reinforcement. After repeated pairings the animal starts crossing as soon as the light comes on, before the shock begins. This is avoidance learning: the response prevents the aversive stimulus from arriving at all. Richard L. Solomon and Lyman C. Wynne (1953) showed that dogs trained this way kept avoiding for hundreds of trials after the shock was switched off. Orval Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory explains why: through classical conditioning the light comes to evoke fear, and through operant conditioning the jump is reinforced by the reduction of that fear. Clinically, this is why phobic avoidance persists: the person never stays long enough to learn that the feared outcome does not happen. Option a is right because escape always develops before avoidance. Exam trap: you cannot avoid a shock you have not first learned to escape, so reversed orders and avoidance-only options are wrong.", "traps": {"b": "Only operant escape describes the early trials; once the light reliably predicts the shock, the animal goes on to respond to the light and avoid the shock altogether.", "c": "Avoidance cannot come before escape: the animal must first learn that the response ends the shock before it can learn to make that response to the warning signal.", "d": "Only operant avoidance skips the escape phase; on the first trials the animal cannot know that the light signals shock, so it can only escape."}}, {"q": "The contention that our facial expressions influence our emotional experience attests to which of the following hypotheses?", "concept": "The answer is d: the facial feedback hypothesis. This idea says the face does not just display an emotion; feedback from the facial muscles to the brain also helps create or strengthen the feeling. Charles Darwin and William James hinted at it in the nineteenth century, and Silvan Tomkins and Carroll Izard developed it in the 1960s and 1970s. In a well-known 1988 study, Fritz Strack, Leonard Martin and Sabine Stepper had people hold a pen between their teeth, which forces a smile, or between their lips, which blocks one; the 'smilers' rated cartoons as funnier. Paul Ekman's lab similarly found that people told to move particular facial muscles, without being told which emotion they were posing, showed autonomic changes and reported a trace of that emotion. Everyday example: forcing a smile at a tiring party can make you feel slightly better. A 17-lab replication (Wagenmakers and colleagues, 2016) found no reliable pen effect, but a 2019 meta-analysis found small facial feedback effects overall and the Many Smiles Collaboration (2022) found that posed smiles do raise happiness a little, so the effect is real but small. Option d is right because the stem says our expressions influence our emotional experience, which is exactly the direction this hypothesis proposes. Exam trap: students pick universality because it also concerns faces, but universality is about expressions being produced and recognised the same way across cultures, not about faces changing feelings.", "traps": {"a": "The universality hypothesis (Paul Ekman, building on Charles Darwin) says basic expressions such as happiness, fear and disgust are produced and recognised the same way across cultures; it treats the face as a universal signal, not as a cause of feelings.", "b": "The emotional specificity hypothesis says each emotion has its own distinct pattern of autonomic nervous system activity, a view linked to William James and Carl Lange; it concerns bodily arousal, not facial muscles.", "c": "The emotional generality hypothesis says autonomic arousal is much the same for every emotion, as Walter Cannon and later Stanley Schachter assumed; it too is about the ANS, not facial expressions."}}, {"q": "We sometimes attempt to solve problems by applying techniques that worked in similar situations in the past. What type of method are we using?", "concept": "The answer is c: analogy, using a solution that worked for a similar problem in the past. Textbooks such as Baron and Ciccarelli group problem-solving strategies into trial and error, algorithms, heuristics and analogies. An analogy maps the structure of an old, solved problem onto a new one. The classic research is by Mary Gick and Keith Holyoak (1980), who used Karl Duncker's radiation problem: a tumour must be destroyed by rays that also harm healthy tissue at full strength. People who had first read a story about a general who split his army into small groups converging on a fortress were more likely to find the solution of many weak rays converging on the tumour, especially when hinted to use the story. Everyday example: having fixed a jammed printer by reseating its cartridge, you try reseating the toner when the photocopier jams. Clinically, a therapist may help a client apply the coping plan that worked for exam anxiety to a coming job interview. Option c is right because the stem describes transferring a past technique to a similar situation, which is the definition of reasoning by analogy. Exam trap: many students choose heuristics because analogy is sometimes listed as one kind of heuristic; when analogy is offered as a separate option, it is the precise answer.", "traps": {"a": "Trial and error means trying one possible solution after another until something works, as Edward L. Thorndike's cats did in the puzzle box; it does not rely on recalling a similar past problem.", "b": "An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure that guarantees a solution if followed, like a maths formula or trying every combination of a lock; it is certain but slow, and not based on similarity to past problems.", "d": "A heuristic is a rule of thumb or mental shortcut, such as means-end analysis or working backward, that often but not always works; analogy can be counted as one, but the stem describes analogy specifically."}}, {"q": "The fact that a nearer object may obscure a more distant object from the view is due to:", "concept": "The answer is b: interposition, also called overlap or occlusion. Interposition is a monocular depth cue: when one object partly covers another, we see the covering object as nearer. It is one of the pictorial cues that painters have used for centuries, along with linear perspective, relative size, texture gradient, aerial perspective, shading and height in the visual field. Because it needs only one eye, it works in photographs and paintings. It tells us only relative order (which object is in front), not absolute distance. Everyday example: at a cricket match, a spectator's head blocks part of the scoreboard, so you instantly know the head is closer without judging any distance in metres. In a class photo, the students whose bodies cut off others look nearer. Hermann von Helmholtz treated such cues as the basis of unconscious inference about depth, whereas James J. Gibson argued that the information lies directly in the light reaching the eye. Binocular cues, by contrast, need two eyes: retinal disparity and convergence. Option b is right because the stem describes a nearer object hiding part of a farther one, which is the exact definition of interposition. Exam trap: students confuse it with figure and ground or closure because these are also perceptual terms, but those are Gestalt principles of organising shapes, not cues for depth.", "traps": {"a": "Figure and ground is the Gestalt principle that we split a scene into an object (figure) standing out against a background (ground), as in Edgar Rubin's vase-faces picture; it organises the scene but is not the overlap cue to distance.", "c": "Proximity is the Gestalt law that elements close together are grouped as a unit, such as dots spaced in pairs; it concerns grouping, not depth.", "d": "Closure is the Gestalt tendency to fill gaps and see incomplete figures, such as a broken circle, as whole; it concerns completing form, not distance."}}, {"q": "In a 3 × 4 contingency table, using the chi-square test, the degrees of freedom associated with the chi-square will be:", "concept": "The answer is d: 6 degrees of freedom. The chi-square test was introduced by Karl Pearson in 1900 to compare observed frequencies with the frequencies expected if the null hypothesis is true. In a contingency table, the test of independence asks whether two categorical variables are related, for example diagnosis (three categories) and treatment setting (four categories). Degrees of freedom (df) are the number of cell values that are free to vary once the row and column totals are fixed. For a contingency table the formula is df = (r - 1)(c - 1), where r is the number of rows and c the number of columns. Here, (3 - 1)(4 - 1) = 2 × 3 = 6. Intuition: if all row and column totals are known, you can fill only 2 cells in each of the first 3 columns freely; every remaining cell is then fixed by the totals. The df matter because the critical value depends on them: at the .05 level, chi-square needs to exceed 12.59 when df is 6. Option d is right because applying (r - 1)(c - 1) to a 3 × 4 table gives 6. Exam trap: students multiply rows by columns (12, the number of cells), add them (7), or subtract one from only one dimension (9), instead of subtracting one from each dimension before multiplying.", "traps": {"a": "12 is simply 3 × 4, the number of cells in the table; it ignores the constraints imposed by the fixed row and column totals.", "b": "7 is 3 + 4, the number of rows plus columns; degrees of freedom for a contingency table are never found by adding the dimensions.", "c": "9 comes from subtracting one from only one dimension, (4 - 1) × 3; the formula requires subtracting one from both rows and columns, giving 2 × 3 = 6."}}, {"q": "Why do individuals with a high need for achievement quit early when faced by a very difficult task?", "concept": "The answer is d: they have a high need for efficiency, meaning they want their effort to pay off. David C. McClelland defined the need for achievement (nAch) as a concern with doing things well and better than before. John William Atkinson's 1957 risk-taking model explains task choice: the tendency to approach success equals motive to succeed × probability of success (Ps) × incentive value (1 - Ps). The product peaks when Ps is about 0.5, so achievers prefer moderately difficult tasks where success is realistic and clearly due to their own skill. On a very difficult task Ps is tiny, so the expected return on effort is low, and Norman Feather's 1961 persistence study found that achievement-oriented people gave up sooner on a task described as very hard than on one described as easy. Example: an ambitious student drops an impossible extra-credit puzzle and puts the time into a challenging but winnable project. Option d is right because, of the four choices, only efficiency (spending effort where it can realistically succeed) matches McClelland's and Atkinson's account. Exam trap: high anxiety and poor perseverance describe people high in fear of failure, who oddly pick very easy or very hard tasks. Key note: 'need for efficiency' is not textbook wording; d is keyed as the best fit, and the real reason is the very low probability of success.", "traps": {"a": "Getting tired easily is a matter of fatigue or stamina, not motivation; achievement motivation theory says nothing about high achievers tiring faster.", "b": "High anxiety (Atkinson's motive to avoid failure, measured with test anxiety scales) marks people low in achievement motivation, who tend to avoid moderate tasks and choose very easy or very hard ones.", "c": "Poor perseverance is the opposite of the high achiever's profile; they persist strongly on challenging but attainable tasks and quit very hard ones only because success is improbable."}}, {"q": "A man reflects his anger from his boss to his child, is an example of", "concept": "The answer is b: displacement. Displacement is an ego defence mechanism, first described by Sigmund Freud and systematised by his daughter Anna Freud in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936). The ego redirects an impulse, usually anger or sexual desire, from the person who aroused it to a safer, less threatening substitute. The original target is dangerous to attack (a boss who could sack you, a parent you depend on), so the energy is discharged on someone weaker or on an object. The man in the stem cannot shout at his boss, so he snaps at his child at home: the classic 'kick the dog' chain, in which the child may in turn kick the dog. Other examples are slamming a door after an argument, or a student scolded by a teacher who then bullies a younger sibling. Freud also explained phobias this way: in the case of Little Hans, fear of the father was displaced onto horses. Scapegoating of minority groups is displacement at the social level. Option b is right because the feeling stays the same (anger) while only its target changes, from the boss to the child. Exam trap: students pick projection, but projection means attributing your own unacceptable feeling to someone else ('my boss hates me'), whereas displacement means expressing your own feeling towards a different target.", "traps": {"a": "Projection attributes one's own unacceptable impulses to another person, for example a hostile man believing his boss is hostile to him; the man here does not disown his anger or locate it in someone else.", "c": "Reaction formation turns an unacceptable feeling into its exaggerated opposite, such as being excessively sweet to a boss one hates; here the anger is expressed, not reversed.", "d": "Regression is a retreat to behaviour typical of an earlier developmental stage, such as a stressed adult sulking or throwing a tantrum; it does not involve redirecting a feeling to a new target."}}, {"q": "The visual signals from each retina reach striate cortex via which of the following?", "concept": "The answer is a: the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) of the thalamus. The visual pathway runs from the photoreceptors to bipolar cells to retinal ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve. At the optic chiasm, fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross, so each side of the brain receives information about the opposite visual field. These fibres continue as the optic tract, and most of them synapse in the LGN, which has six layers: two magnocellular layers (motion and coarse form) and four parvocellular layers (colour and fine detail). From the LGN, the optic radiations carry signals to the primary visual cortex in the occipital lobe, called striate cortex (V1, Brodmann area 17) because of a visible stripe of myelinated fibres. John P. J. Pinel calls this the retina-geniculate-striate pathway. Clinically, a stroke damaging the LGN or the optic radiations causes loss of the opposite half of the visual field in both eyes (a homonymous defect). Option a is right because the LGN is the thalamic relay through which signals from each retina reach striate cortex. Exam trap: the medial geniculate nucleus is the thalamic relay for hearing, not vision; remember 'lateral for light, medial for music'. The paper prints 'Medical geniculate', a misspelling of medial geniculate.", "traps": {"b": "The medial geniculate nucleus is the thalamic relay of the auditory pathway, sending signals to primary auditory cortex in the temporal lobe; it plays no part in carrying retinal signals.", "c": "Inferotemporal cortex lies at the end of the ventral 'what' stream and helps recognise objects and faces; it receives visual information after striate cortex, not on the way to it.", "d": "Posterior parietal cortex is the end point of the dorsal 'where' or 'how' stream, handling spatial location and visually guided action; it too comes after striate cortex."}}, {"q": "When stress is prolonged, the most likely result is", "concept": "The answer is a: a generalised weakening of the immune system. Hans Selye's general adaptation syndrome (1936) describes three stages: alarm, resistance and exhaustion. Selye's chronically stressed rats showed enlarged adrenal glands, ulcers and shrunken immune tissue. In prolonged stress the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis keeps releasing cortisol, and sustained cortisol suppresses immune activity: fewer and less active lymphocytes, lower natural killer cell activity and slower wound healing. The field that studies these links is psychoneuroimmunology, founded when Robert Ader and Nicholas Cohen (1975) conditioned immune suppression in rats. A meta-analysis by Suzanne Segerstrom and Gregory Miller (2004) found that brief stressors can briefly boost some natural immune responses, but chronic stressors such as caring for a relative with dementia, unemployment or long-term marital conflict suppress immunity broadly. Janice Kiecolt-Glaser showed that dementia caregivers' wounds healed more slowly and that medical students had weaker immune measures during examinations. Everyday example: many students catch a cold just after a long, stressful exam season. Option a is right because the best-supported effect of prolonged stress is broad immune suppression, which makes infections and slower recovery more likely. Exam trap: students pick d because acute stress can briefly mobilise natural killer cells, but prolonged stress reduces their activity rather than intensifying it; and endorphin release (option c) is a short-term stress response that numbs pain, not the typical outcome of chronic stress.", "traps": {"b": "The pons and medulla are brainstem areas controlling breathing, heart rate and sleep; long-term cortisol exposure is linked to changes in the hippocampus, not to brainstem damage.", "c": "Endorphins (endogenous opioids) are released during acute stress and cause stress-induced analgesia, as when an injured athlete feels little pain mid-match; this is a short-term response, not the typical result of prolonged stress.", "d": "Natural killer cells are immune cells that destroy virus-infected and tumour cells; acute stress can briefly raise their numbers, but prolonged stress lowers their activity."}}, {"q": "A 40-item Vocabulary test was administered to a group of students. A second similar test of vocabulary terms was administered to this same group of students approximately one week later. The researcher reported that the correlation between these two tests was r = 0.90. What type of reliability is represented in this example?", "concept": "The answer is c: alternate (parallel) forms reliability. Reliability is the consistency of test scores. Classical test theory, built on the work of Charles Spearman, treats each observed score as a true score plus error, and a reliability coefficient estimates how much of the score variation is true rather than error. Alternate-forms reliability uses two equivalent versions of a test, with different items sampling the same content at the same difficulty, given to the same people; the correlation between the forms is called the coefficient of equivalence. When the second form is given after a gap, as here (one week), Anne Anastasi calls it delayed alternate-form reliability, which reflects both content-sampling error and changes over time. In the stem, a 40-item vocabulary test is followed a week later by a 'second similar test' of vocabulary, and the two correlate 0.90, showing that both forms rank the students consistently. Clinically, parallel forms are used when a patient must be retested soon, as the original Wechsler Memory Scale offered two forms, so that remembering the items does not inflate the second score. Option c is right because the key clue is a different but similar test, not the same test repeated. Exam trap: the one-week gap tempts students to choose test-retest, but test-retest requires giving the identical test twice; a new equivalent form makes it alternate forms.", "traps": {"a": "Test-retest reliability gives the same test twice to the same group and correlates the scores (the coefficient of stability); here the second test is a different, similar test, so it is not a simple retest.", "b": "Internal consistency estimates reliability from a single administration by checking how well the items hang together, using Kuder-Richardson formulas or Cronbach's alpha; it needs only one test session.", "d": "Split-half reliability divides one test given once into two halves (often odd and even items), correlates them and corrects with the Spearman-Brown formula; no second test or second session is involved."}}, {"q": "The idea that people cope with stress by moving towards people, away from people or against people is most consistent with the views of", "concept": "The answer is c: Karen Horney. Karen Horney, a German-born psychoanalyst who later worked in the United States, broke with Sigmund Freud's emphasis on instincts and stressed social and cultural forces. She proposed that basic anxiety, a child's feeling of being isolated and helpless in a potentially hostile world, grows out of parental indifference, inconsistency or domination. To cope with it, people develop neurotic needs, which she grouped into three trends in Our Inner Conflicts (1945). Moving toward people is the compliant type, who seeks love and approval ('if you love me, you will not hurt me'). Moving against people is the aggressive type, who seeks power and control ('if I have power, no one can hurt me'). Moving away from people is the detached type, who seeks independence and privacy ('if I withdraw, nothing can hurt me'). Healthy people use all three flexibly; the neurotic person relies rigidly on one. Example: a student who agrees with everyone to avoid any conflict is moving toward people. Option c is right because moving toward, against and away from people are Horney's three neurotic trends. Exam trap: the paper prints 'Aron Beck', a misspelling of Aaron T. Beck, the founder of cognitive therapy; his cognitive triad also has three parts (self, world and future), which tempts students, but it describes depressive thinking, not interpersonal coping styles.", "traps": {"a": "Aaron T. Beck founded cognitive therapy and described the cognitive triad of negative views of the self, the world and the future in depression; he did not describe moving toward, against or away from people.", "b": "Carl Rogers founded person-centred therapy and explained distress as incongruence between self-concept and experience, created by conditions of worth; his theory has no three-direction coping scheme.", "d": "Erik Erikson proposed eight psychosocial stages, each built around a crisis such as trust versus mistrust; he described developmental conflicts across the lifespan, not three interpersonal coping styles."}}, {"q": "Alexander Thomas and Stella Chase described three categories of infants: easy, difficult and slow to warm up. These are categories of", "concept": "The answer is d: temperament. Temperament is the biologically based, early-appearing style of responding to the world: activity level, mood, adaptability and reactivity. The psychiatrists Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess (the paper misspells her name as 'Chase') began the New York Longitudinal Study in 1956, following a group of children from infancy into adulthood. They rated nine dimensions: activity level, rhythmicity, approach or withdrawal, adaptability, intensity of reaction, threshold of responsiveness, quality of mood, distractibility, and attention span with persistence. From these they described three types. Easy children (about 40 percent) are regular, cheerful and adapt quickly. Difficult children (about 10 percent) are irregular, react intensely and negatively, and adapt slowly. Slow-to-warm-up children (about 15 percent) are mildly negative and inactive at first but adapt gradually. Their key idea was goodness of fit: outcomes depend on how well parenting matches the child's temperament, so a patient, consistent parent can help a difficult child adjust well. Example: a slow-to-warm-up toddler clings to her parent at a new playgroup but joins in after a few visits. Option d is right because easy, difficult and slow-to-warm-up are the temperament categories of the New York Longitudinal Study. Exam trap: students pick emotion because the labels sound like moods, but they describe stable behavioural styles, not single emotions; do not confuse them with Mary Ainsworth's attachment types either.", "traps": {"a": "Play categories come from Mildred Parten, who described stages of social play such as solitary, parallel, associative and cooperative play; easy, difficult and slow-to-warm-up are not play types.", "b": "Toilet training belongs to Freud's anal stage and to parenting practice; Thomas and Chess did not classify infants by toilet training.", "c": "Emotion refers to specific feeling states such as joy, fear or anger; the three categories describe a child's stable overall behavioural style, which is temperament, not a particular emotion."}}, {"q": "Which of the following types of cognitive abilities is LEAST likely to show a decline in late life?", "concept": "The answer is d: semantic memory. Endel Tulving (1972) divided long-term declarative memory into episodic memory, for personal events tied to a time and place (what you ate at a wedding last month), and semantic memory, for general facts, word meanings and knowledge (that Delhi is India's capital). Research on ageing, summarised in lifespan texts such as Santrock's, shows a clear pattern. Working memory, the ability to hold and manipulate information briefly, declines with age. Processing speed slows steadily from early adulthood, and Timothy Salthouse's processing-speed theory argues that this slowing explains much of the other age-related loss. Fluid intelligence, the ability to solve novel problems, also declines. Semantic memory, by contrast, is largely preserved: vocabulary and general knowledge stay stable or even grow into the seventies. Its main weakness in old age is retrieval, such as more frequent tip-of-the-tongue states for names, rather than loss of the knowledge itself. Procedural memory, such as cycling or typing, is also well preserved. Example: a 75-year-old grandfather may be slow to learn a new phone app but can still explain rare words and recall history accurately. Option d is right because semantic memory is the most stable of the four abilities listed. Exam trap: students treat all memory as declining with age, but the decline falls mainly on episodic and working memory, while semantic and procedural memory hold up.", "traps": {"a": "Working memory, Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch's system for holding and manipulating information, shows clear age-related decline, especially on demanding tasks such as backward digit span.", "b": "Processing speed is among the earliest and most consistent declines, beginning in early adulthood; Timothy Salthouse sees it as the root of many other cognitive losses.", "c": "Fluid intelligence, Raymond Cattell's ability to reason with new material, peaks in early adulthood and declines thereafter, unlike crystallised knowledge."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is an atypical developmental pattern characterised by stereotyped motor response and poor communication skills?", "concept": "The answer is c: autism spectrum disorder (ASD). Leo Kanner described 'early infantile autism' in 1943, and Hans Asperger described a related group of children in 1944. DSM-5 (2013) merged autistic disorder, Asperger's disorder, childhood disintegrative disorder and pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified into one neurodevelopmental diagnosis, ASD, retained in DSM-5-TR. It has two core domains. The first is persistent deficits in social communication and interaction: poor social-emotional reciprocity, limited eye contact and gesture, and difficulty forming relationships; many children also have delayed or absent speech, echolalia or an unusual tone of voice. The second is restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests or activities: stereotyped motor movements such as hand flapping, rocking or spinning objects, insistence on sameness, intense narrow interests and unusual sensory responses. Symptoms appear in the early developmental period. Example: a three-year-old who does not respond to his name, lines up toy cars for hours and flaps his hands when excited would be referred for ASD assessment, which in India often uses the ISAA or CARS. Option c is right because stereotyped motor responses plus poor communication are exactly ASD's two diagnostic domains. Exam trap: tardive dyskinesia also involves repetitive movements, but it is a drug side effect in adults, not a developmental pattern; and ADHD involves restlessness and impulsivity rather than stereotypies and social-communication deficits.", "traps": {"a": "Tardive dyskinesia is a late side effect of long-term antipsychotic use, with involuntary lip-smacking and tongue and limb movements; it is acquired, not developmental, and does not involve poor communication.", "b": "Down syndrome is a chromosomal disorder (trisomy 21) causing intellectual disability and characteristic physical features; affected children are often sociable, and stereotypies are not a defining feature.", "d": "ADHD is a neurodevelopmental disorder of persistent inattention, hyperactivity and impulsivity; it does not feature stereotyped movements or the core social-communication deficits of autism."}}, {"q": "Which theorist revised Sigmund Freud's stages of psychosexual development, replacing it with psychosocial stages?", "concept": "The answer is b: Erik Erikson. Erik H. Erikson, a German-born psychoanalyst who was analysed by Anna Freud in Vienna, accepted much of Sigmund Freud's theory but shifted the focus from sexual drives to the ego and social relationships. In Childhood and Society (1950) he replaced Freud's five psychosexual stages (oral, anal, phallic, latency and genital) with eight psychosocial stages that span the whole lifespan. Each stage is a crisis between two poles: trust versus mistrust (infancy), autonomy versus shame and doubt, initiative versus guilt, industry versus inferiority, identity versus role confusion (adolescence), intimacy versus isolation, generativity versus stagnation, and integrity versus despair (old age). Resolving each crisis yields a virtue, such as hope from trust. The early stages map onto Freud's: trust versus mistrust parallels the oral stage, and autonomy parallels the anal stage. Unlike Freud, Erikson argued that personality keeps developing after adolescence. Example: a teenager trying out different career plans and friend groups is working through identity versus role confusion. Option b is right because Erikson is the theorist who recast the psychosexual stages as psychosocial ones. Exam trap: other neo-Freudians, such as Karen Horney (misspelt 'Karen Horneys' in the paper) and Alfred Adler, also stressed social factors, but neither built a stage theory; only Erikson's name goes with 'psychosocial stages'.", "traps": {"a": "Karen Horney stressed basic anxiety, culture and neurotic trends, and challenged Freud's view of women with ideas such as womb envy; she did not propose a set of psychosocial stages.", "c": "Alfred Adler founded individual psychology, centred on inferiority feelings, striving for superiority, style of life and birth order; he did not build a stage theory.", "d": "Viktor Frankl founded logotherapy, built on the will to meaning and shaped by his years in Nazi concentration camps; he did not revise Freud's developmental stages."}}, {"q": "Rapid Eye Movement is most closely associated with which of the following?", "concept": "The answer is c: dreaming. In 1953 Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman at the University of Chicago noticed periods of rapid, darting eye movements during sleep; people woken during these periods usually reported vivid dreams, far more often than when woken from other stages. William Dement extended the work. REM sleep is called paradoxical sleep because the EEG shows fast, low-amplitude, desynchronised activity resembling wakefulness, with characteristic sawtooth waves, yet the body's muscles are almost paralysed (atonia) apart from the eyes and breathing muscles. Heart rate and breathing become irregular. REM periods recur in cycles of about 90 minutes and get longer towards morning, so most REM sleep falls late in the night; adults spend about 20 to 25 percent of sleep in REM. REM dreams are story-like, emotional and bizarre, while non-REM reports tend to be brief and thought-like. Example: when your alarm rings near dawn, you often wake in the middle of a vivid dream because REM periods are longest then. Clinically, in REM sleep behaviour disorder the atonia fails and people act out their dreams. Option c is right because REM is the stage most closely associated with vivid dreaming. Exam trap: students choose alpha waves because the REM EEG looks wakeful, but alpha belongs to relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed; REM shows fast, beta-like activity and sawtooth waves.", "traps": {"a": "Alpha waves (8 to 13 Hz) mark relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed, just before sleep onset; they are not the hallmark of REM sleep.", "b": "Delta waves are slow (below 4 Hz), high-amplitude waves of deep non-REM slow-wave sleep (stage N3), when dreaming is least vivid and sleepwalking and night terrors occur.", "d": "Apnea means pauses in breathing during sleep, as in obstructive sleep apnea; it is a breathing disorder (sometimes worse in REM), not the defining feature of REM sleep."}}, {"q": "If a psychological disorder has a genetic basis, one would expect to find the highest concordance rate for the illness between which of the following?", "concept": "The answer is c: identical twins. A concordance rate is the percentage of pairs of relatives in which, if one member has a disorder, the other also has it. Identical (monozygotic, MZ) twins come from one fertilised egg that splits, so they share essentially all their genes. Fraternal (dizygotic, DZ) twins come from two eggs and share about 50 percent of their genes on average, the same as ordinary siblings and the same as a parent and child. If genes contribute to a disorder, concordance should rise with genetic similarity, so MZ concordance should exceed DZ concordance. The twin method goes back to Francis Galton in the 1870s. Irving Gottesman's summary of schizophrenia studies is the classic example: lifetime risk is about 1 percent in the general population, about 13 percent for the child of one affected parent, about 17 percent for a DZ co-twin and about 48 percent for an MZ co-twin. Bipolar disorder and autism show similar MZ advantages. Because MZ concordance is never 100 percent, the environment must also matter, which supports the diathesis-stress model. Option c is right because identical twins share the most genes and so show the highest concordance for a genetically influenced disorder. Exam trap: fraternal twins share a womb and an upbringing, but genetically they are no closer than ordinary siblings, so they rank below identical twins.", "traps": {"a": "A mother and child are first-degree relatives sharing about 50 percent of their genes, so their concordance is well below that of identical twins.", "b": "A father and child also share about 50 percent of their genes, so their genetic resemblance equals that of mother and child and is well below that of identical twins.", "d": "Fraternal (dizygotic) twins share about 50 percent of their genes, like ordinary siblings; their concordance (about 17 percent for schizophrenia) is far below that of identical twins (about 48 percent)."}}, {"q": "According to Sigmund Freud, a child who grabs food from another child because of hunger is driven by", "concept": "The answer is a: the id. In The Ego and the Id (1923), Sigmund Freud described personality as three interacting systems. The id is present at birth, wholly unconscious and the reservoir of instinctual energy (sexual and aggressive drives). It runs on the pleasure principle, demanding immediate gratification and the release of tension without regard for logic, morality or reality. The ego develops from the id to deal with the outside world; it follows the reality principle, finding realistic ways to meet needs. The superego, formed around age five or six as the child internalises parental standards while resolving the Oedipus complex, contains the conscience and the ego ideal. A child who snatches food from another because he is hungry is acting on raw biological need with no thought of consequences or fairness, which is pure id. If the ego were in charge, he might ask for some food or wait for snack time; if the superego dominated, he would feel guilty at the very thought of taking another child's food. Option a is right because grabbing food to satisfy hunger at once is driven by the id's pleasure principle. Exam trap: some choose the ego because grabbing is an action in the real world, but the ego would delay or find a realistic, acceptable way; impulsive, immediate gratification is always the id.", "traps": {"b": "The ego is the rational, reality-oriented executive that delays gratification and finds socially acceptable ways to meet needs; it would make the child ask or wait, not grab.", "c": "Environmental reinforcers belong to behaviourism, as in B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning, not to Freud's theory; the question asks for Freud's explanation.", "d": "The superego is the internalised moral voice of parents and society, producing guilt and ideals; it would stop the child from taking the food, not drive him to grab it."}}, {"q": "Which memory store is believed to have the largest capacity?", "concept": "The answer is c: the long-term store. Richard C. Atkinson and Richard M. Shiffrin's multi-store (modal) model (1968) describes three stores. The sensory store holds a brief, raw copy of input: iconic memory for vision lasts under a second, as George Sperling's partial-report experiments (1960) showed, and echoic memory for sound lasts a few seconds. It fades almost at once. Attended information moves to the short-term store, which holds about seven plus or minus two chunks (George A. Miller, 1956) for roughly 15 to 30 seconds without rehearsal. Rehearsed and meaningfully encoded information passes into the long-term store, which is thought to have essentially unlimited capacity and can hold information for a lifetime, such as your mother tongue, your school address or how to ride a bicycle. No one has found a point at which long-term memory is 'full'; forgetting there comes mainly from interference and retrieval failure, not lack of space. Example: you can keep learning new words and faces all your life without old ones being pushed out. Option c is right because, of the stores listed, long-term memory has the largest, practically limitless capacity. Exam trap: some questions say 'largest capacity but shortest duration', which points to sensory memory, but 'largest capacity' alone points to long-term memory. The phonological loop is only a small part of Alan Baddeley's working memory.", "traps": {"a": "The sensory store holds a fleeting copy of incoming sensations, under a second for vision and a few seconds for sound; it can briefly register a lot, but it is not the largest store in the model.", "b": "The short-term store holds about seven plus or minus two items (George A. Miller) for under half a minute without rehearsal; it is the most limited store.", "d": "The phonological loop is the speech-based component of Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch's working memory model (1974), holding roughly two seconds' worth of speech; its capacity is very small."}}, {"q": "The inability to recognize a once-familiar face, while still being able to accurately describe elements of the face, is a characteristic symptom of", "concept": "The answer is c: prosopagnosia, often called face blindness. Prosopagnosia is a visual agnosia, a failure to recognise familiar stimuli despite intact basic vision and intellect. People with it can see a face clearly, describe its parts (a long nose, grey eyes, a moustache) and even match two photographs, yet cannot tell whose face it is, sometimes not even a spouse's or their own in a mirror. They usually recognise people by voice, gait, hairstyle or clothing instead. The German neurologist Joachim Bodamer coined the term in 1947 from the Greek prosopon (face) and agnosia (not knowing). Acquired prosopagnosia usually follows damage to the fusiform gyrus on the underside of the occipital and temporal lobes, the region Nancy Kanwisher named the fusiform face area, typically on both sides or on the right. A developmental form, present from birth without known injury, affects roughly 2 percent of people; the neurologist Oliver Sacks had it. Clinically, a stroke patient may greet her own daughter politely as a stranger until the daughter speaks. Option c is right because the stem describes intact perception of facial features with lost recognition of identity, the defining feature of prosopagnosia. Exam trap: amnesia also involves failing to recognise people, but the amnesic patient has lost the memory itself, whereas the prosopagnosic still knows the person and recognises them at once by voice.", "traps": {"a": "Amnesia is a loss of memory for new information (anterograde) or past events (retrograde); an amnesic person fails to recognise someone because the memory is gone, not because faces in particular cannot be identified while voices can.", "b": "Blindsight is the ability of people with damage to primary visual cortex to point to or respond to objects in their blind field while denying any conscious sight, studied by Lawrence Weiskrantz.", "d": "Sensory (hemispatial) neglect, usually after right parietal damage, is a failure to attend to the left side of space or the body, such as eating only from the right half of the plate; it is not a selective failure to recognise faces."}}, {"q": "Ability derived from previous experience is known as", "concept": "The answer is a: crystallised intelligence. Raymond B. Cattell proposed in the 1940s, and later developed with his student John L. Horn, that general intelligence has two broad parts. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason, see relationships and solve new problems without relying on prior learning; Raven's Progressive Matrices and novel puzzles tap it. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the knowledge, vocabulary, skills and judgement acquired through schooling, culture and experience; vocabulary, general information and word-problem tests measure it. In Cattell's investment theory, Gc is fluid ability invested in learning, so it is built from previous experience. The two follow different age curves: Gf peaks in early adulthood and then declines, while Gc holds steady or keeps growing into late adulthood. Their model later merged with John B. Carroll's three-stratum theory into the Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) model, which underlies modern tests such as the Woodcock-Johnson and the Stanford-Binet Fifth Edition. Example: a retired judge who instantly spots the legal flaw in a contract is drawing on crystallised intelligence built over decades. Option a is right because ability derived from previous experience is the definition of crystallised intelligence. Exam trap: students mix up the two; remember that fluid intelligence handles new problems on the spot, while crystallised intelligence is the stored product of experience. Formal and concrete operations are Jean Piaget's stages, not kinds of intelligence.", "traps": {"b": "Formal operations is Jean Piaget's fourth stage, from about age 11 or 12, marked by abstract and hypothetical-deductive reasoning; it is a developmental stage, not an ability defined by past experience.", "c": "Fluid intelligence is the ability to reason and solve novel problems independently of learned knowledge, the opposite of what the stem describes.", "d": "Concrete operations is Piaget's third stage, roughly ages 7 to 11, when children reason logically about concrete objects and master conservation; it is not a type of intelligence."}}, {"q": "Personality disorders are characterised by which specific symptoms", "concept": "The answer is c: long-term maladaptive traits and behaviour. DSM-5-TR defines a personality disorder as an enduring pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from the expectations of the person's culture, is pervasive and inflexible, begins in adolescence or early adulthood, is stable over time, and leads to distress or impairment. The pattern shows in at least two of four areas: cognition (ways of seeing self and others), affectivity, interpersonal functioning and impulse control. The ten specific disorders fall into three clusters: Cluster A, odd or eccentric (paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal); Cluster B, dramatic, emotional or erratic (antisocial, borderline, histrionic, narcissistic); and Cluster C, anxious or fearful (avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive). Unlike an episode of depression or psychosis, these patterns are usually ego-syntonic: the person sees the traits as simply 'how I am' and often blames others for the problems they cause. Example: a woman with borderline personality disorder has had unstable relationships, intense fear of abandonment and impulsive self-harm since her teens, across many jobs and partners. ICD-11 has replaced the separate types with a single personality disorder diagnosis graded by severity, plus trait domains. Option c is right because enduring, inflexible and maladaptive traits are the defining feature of every personality disorder. Exam trap: loss of contact with reality tempts students who think of schizotypal or paranoid types, but personality disorders do not involve sustained psychosis.", "traps": {"a": "Permanent, severe deficits in cognitive functioning describe intellectual disability or major neurocognitive disorder (dementia); in personality disorders intelligence is usually normal.", "b": "Loss of contact with reality is psychosis, as in schizophrenia or psychotic mood disorders; people with personality disorders keep reality testing, apart from brief stress-related episodes in some.", "d": "Unexplained physical symptoms point to somatic symptom disorder or conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder), not to personality disorder."}}, {"q": "Somatic symptoms of severe anxiety include all of the following except:", "concept": "The answer is d: hypoventilation. Severe anxiety activates the sympathetic nervous system and the fight-or-flight response that Walter B. Cannon described. This produces a recognisable set of bodily (somatic) symptoms, which Kaplan and Sadock's textbook and the Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale (Max Hamilton, 1959) list by body system. Cardiovascular: palpitations and chest discomfort. Respiratory: tightness or choking feelings, frequent sighing and over-breathing (hyperventilation). Gastrointestinal: dry mouth, nausea, abdominal discomfort, loose motions or diarrhoea, and sometimes constipation. Others: tremor, sweating, frequent urination, muscle tension and dizziness. Hyperventilation matters most here: rapid breathing blows off carbon dioxide, causing tingling around the mouth and fingers, light-headedness and still more panic, which is why slow-breathing training is part of panic treatment. Hypoventilation, breathing too slowly or shallowly so that carbon dioxide builds up, is the opposite; it occurs with opioid or sedative overdose, severe obesity or neuromuscular disease, not with anxiety. Example: before a viva, an anxious student sighs repeatedly, feels her heart pounding and keeps needing the toilet. Option d is right because anxiety drives breathing up, not down, so hypoventilation is not one of its somatic symptoms. Exam trap: the two words look alike; remember that anxious people over-breathe (hyper), while a drug overdose causes under-breathing (hypo).", "traps": {"a": "Diarrhoea, or loose motions, is a common somatic symptom of anxiety caused by autonomic stimulation of the gut, as in the familiar nervous stomach before an exam.", "b": "Sighing respiration is a recognised respiratory symptom of anxiety and is listed on the Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale along with chest tightness and choking feelings.", "c": "Constipation is also listed among the gastrointestinal symptoms of anxiety on the Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale; anxiety can disturb bowel habit in either direction."}}, {"q": "All of the following are normal experiences except:", "concept": "The answer is d: hallucinations. The other three are common, brief experiences in healthy people. Déjà vu (French for 'already seen') is the feeling that a new situation has been lived before; surveys suggest about two thirds of people have had it. Jamais vu ('never seen') is the reverse: something familiar suddenly feels strange, as when a common word written many times starts to look wrong. Derealisation is a sense that the surroundings are unreal, flat or dreamlike; it occurs briefly in healthy people under exhaustion, sleep loss or acute stress, and becomes a disorder only when persistent and distressing (depersonalisation-derealisation disorder). A hallucination, as Jean-Etienne Esquirol defined it, is a perception without an external object; it has the full vividness of a real perception and is located in outer space. Hallucinations are a hallmark of psychosis, as in schizophrenia, or of organic states such as delirium. Option d is right because hallucinations are the only one of the four that is a recognised psychopathological symptom rather than a normal variant. Exam trap: students pick derealisation because it sounds abnormal, but brief derealisation is common under fatigue. Key note: brief hallucinations can also occur in healthy people (hypnagogic images while falling asleep, or hearing a dead spouse's voice in grief), but the key stands because hallucinations are the clearest abnormal experience listed.", "traps": {"a": "Déjà vu is the feeling that a new experience has happened before; most healthy people have it occasionally, so it is a normal experience unless frequent, as in temporal lobe epilepsy.", "b": "Derealisation is the feeling that the world is unreal or dreamlike; transient episodes are common in healthy people under fatigue, sleep deprivation or stress, and it is pathological only when persistent and distressing.", "c": "Jamais vu is the sense that something familiar is strange or new, such as a well-known word suddenly looking wrong; occasional episodes are normal."}}, {"q": "Psychotic depression is characterised by:", "concept": "The answer is b: nihilistic delusions. Psychotic depression, called major depressive disorder with psychotic features in DSM-5-TR and severe depressive episode with psychotic symptoms in ICD-10, is a severe depression accompanied by delusions or hallucinations. The psychotic content is usually mood-congruent, matching depressive themes of guilt, worthlessness, disease, poverty, deserved punishment and death. Nihilistic delusions are the extreme case: the patient believes that she, part of her body or the world does not exist, is dead or is rotting, for example that her intestines have stopped working and rotted away, or that her family is already dead. The French neurologist Jules Cotard described this picture in 1880, and its fullest form is called Cotard's syndrome, seen especially in severe depression in older adults. Hallucinations in psychotic depression are similarly mood-congruent, such as voices calling the patient a sinner who deserves to die. Psychotic depression carries a high suicide risk and usually needs an antidepressant combined with an antipsychotic, or electroconvulsive therapy, which works well for it. Option b is right because nihilistic delusions are the classic mood-congruent delusions of psychotic depression. Exam trap: flight of ideas sounds like a psychotic sign, but it belongs to mania, the opposite pole; circumstantiality is a long-winded way of speaking seen in obsessional personalities and epilepsy, not a marker of depression.", "traps": {"a": "Circumstantiality is a thought-process disturbance in which speech takes long, detailed detours but finally reaches the point; it is seen in obsessional or pedantic people and epilepsy, not specifically in psychotic depression.", "c": "Flight of ideas is rapid speech jumping between ideas linked by chance associations, puns or rhymes; it is a hallmark of mania, the opposite pole of depression.", "d": "Hypnagogic hallucinations occur while falling asleep and are common in healthy people; frequent, vivid ones are a feature of narcolepsy, not of psychotic depression."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a Schneider first-rank symptom?", "concept": "The answer is d: thought block is not a first-rank symptom. The German psychiatrist Kurt Schneider listed first-rank symptoms (FRS) that, in the absence of brain disease, strongly suggest schizophrenia; his Clinical Psychopathology appeared in English in 1959. They are usually grouped as: three kinds of auditory hallucination (thought echo or audible thoughts, voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, and voices giving a running commentary on his actions); thought alienation (thought insertion, thought withdrawal and thought broadcasting); passivity experiences, meaning made feelings, made impulses, made actions and somatic passivity (bodily sensations imposed by an outside agency); and delusional perception (a normal perception suddenly given a private delusional meaning). Thought block is a sudden interruption of the train of thought, so the patient stops mid-sentence and cannot continue; it is an observed disorder of the stream of thought, also common in schizophrenia, but it is not on Schneider's list. What matters is the patient's explanation: if he says someone has pulled the thoughts out of his head, that is thought withdrawal, which is first rank. Option d is right because thought block describes only the interruption, without the delusional belief that an outside force removed the thoughts. Exam trap: thought block and thought withdrawal look identical from outside; withdrawal is first rank only because of the delusional explanation attached to it.", "traps": {"a": "Thought withdrawal, the belief that thoughts are being taken out of one's mind by an outside agency, is one of Schneider's thought-alienation first-rank symptoms.", "b": "Third-person auditory hallucinations, voices discussing or arguing about the patient as 'he' or 'she', are first-rank symptoms.", "c": "Somatic passivity, the experience that bodily sensations are imposed by an outside force, is one of Schneider's passivity first-rank symptoms."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about obsessional thoughts?", "concept": "The answer is c: obsessional thoughts are ego-dystonic. An obsession is a recurrent, intrusive thought, image or impulse that the person experiences as unwanted, senseless and out of character, yet recognises as a product of his or her own mind. Kurt Schneider's classic definition describes contents of consciousness that carry a feeling of subjective compulsion and cannot be got rid of, although on quiet reflection they are seen as senseless. \"Ego-dystonic\" means the experience clashes with the person's values and self-image, so it causes distress and is resisted. Think of a devoted young mother who keeps getting the thought \"I might stab my baby\" while cutting vegetables: she is horrified, hides the knives and fights the thought, which is exactly why it is an obsession and not a wish. ICD-10 adds that at least one thought or act is still resisted, even if unsuccessfully, and that the thought of carrying out the act is not in itself pleasurable. Obsessive-compulsive personality traits such as perfectionism are different: they feel ego-syntonic, natural and right to the person. Option c is right because distress, resistance and the sense that the thought is alien to the self are the core of an obsession. Exam trap: \"recognised as one's own\" (obsession) is not \"inserted from outside\" (thought insertion in schizophrenia); the obsessional patient owns the thought but rejects its content.", "traps": {"a": "Thought-stopping (saying \"Stop!\" when the thought appears) is an older behavioural technique, not the treatment of choice, and suppressing a thought often makes it rebound. Exposure and response prevention and SSRIs are the evidence-based treatments.", "b": "Obsessions often lead to compulsions, but not always; some patients have predominantly obsessional thoughts or ruminations without overt rituals (ICD-10 F42.0).", "d": "Sexual themes are among the commonest obsessions, alongside contamination, harm, symmetry and religious themes, so they are not rare."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is true regarding delusions?", "concept": "The answer is b: for the patient, a delusion is reality. A delusion is a false, firmly held belief, out of keeping with the person's cultural and educational background, that is not changed by evidence or argument. Karl Jaspers, in his General Psychopathology (1913), gave three external marks: extraordinary conviction (subjective certainty), imperviousness to counter-argument (incorrigibility) and impossible or false content. The clinical core is that the patient does not hold the belief as an idea that might be wrong; he lives it as a plain fact about the world, as real as the chair he sits on. A man convinced that his neighbours are poisoning his water will stop drinking it and may move house; a clean laboratory report does not help, because it is absorbed into the delusion (\"they bribed the lab\"). This separates a delusion from an overvalued idea, held strongly but with some room for doubt, and from an obsession, which the person recognises as senseless. Option b is right because lived certainty, the belief being reality for the patient, is the defining quality of a delusion. Exam trap: conviction in a delusion is unshakable, never shakable, and most delusions are not bizarre. Key note: most delusions do concern the self, so d is partly true and might be marked true in a true/false format; as a single best answer, b is the key.", "traps": {"a": "Most delusions are not bizarre; persecutory delusions, the commonest type, are usually possible in principle (being followed, being poisoned). Bizarre delusions, which are clearly impossible, are a subset typical of schizophrenia.", "c": "This reverses the definition: delusions are held with unshakable conviction that does not yield to evidence. A belief held with incomplete conviction is closer to an overvalued idea or an idea of reference.", "d": "Most delusions do involve the self (persecution, reference, grandeur, guilt), so this is partly true, but it is a common feature rather than the defining property that the question is after."}}, {"q": "Obsessions of obsessive-compulsive disorder may present as", "concept": "The answer is c: obsessions may present as mental images. Obsessions are not only verbal thoughts. ICD-10 describes obsessional thoughts as ideas, images or impulses that enter the mind again and again in a stereotyped form, and Frank Fish's Clinical Psychopathology lists several forms: obsessional thoughts, images, ruminations, doubts, impulses and fears. An obsessional image is a vivid picture that intrudes unbidden, such as a mother repeatedly \"seeing\" her child lying dead after an accident, or a religious man seeing a blasphemous scene during prayer. Like other obsessions, the image is recognised as coming from the person's own mind, is felt as distressing and senseless, and is resisted; the patient may try to cancel it with a neutralising thought, a prayer or a ritual. DSM-5-TR likewise defines obsessions as recurrent and persistent thoughts, urges or images experienced as intrusive and unwanted. Option c is right because the image is one of the standard forms an obsession takes. Exam trap: a vivid image is not a hallucination. A hallucination is perceived as coming through the senses from outside and as real; an obsessional image is experienced in the mind's eye and known to be one's own mental product.", "traps": {"a": "Hallucinations are perceptions without an external stimulus, experienced as real and outside the self; obsessional images are recognised as products of the person's own mind.", "b": "Delusions are false beliefs held with unshakable conviction, whereas obsessions are usually recognised as senseless and resisted. DSM-5-TR allows a \"with absent insight/delusional beliefs\" specifier for OCD, but that does not make the obsession a delusion.", "d": "Panic attacks are sudden surges of intense fear with palpitations, breathlessness and fear of dying. A feared trigger can provoke one in OCD, but a panic attack is not a form that an obsession takes."}}, {"q": "The most commonly seen symptom of schizophrenia is", "concept": "The answer is c: auditory hallucinations are the most commonly seen symptom among these options. The best-known data come from the World Health Organization's International Pilot Study of Schizophrenia (IPSS), published in 1973, which examined patients in nine countries, including India. Ranked by frequency, lack of insight came first (about 97 percent of patients), then auditory hallucinations (about 74 percent), verbal hallucinations and ideas of reference (about 70 percent each), and delusions of persecution (about 64 percent). Thought alienation (insertion, withdrawal or broadcasting) was found in roughly half. In schizophrenia these hallucinations are usually voices that talk to the patient, comment on what he is doing or argue about him in the third person; Kurt Schneider counted voices commenting and voices arguing among his first-rank symptoms. A typical patient hears a voice saying \"now he's getting up, now he's going out\" through the day. Visual hallucinations are much less common in schizophrenia and should make you think of delirium, drugs or epilepsy. Option c is right because, after lack of insight (not offered here), auditory hallucinations top the IPSS list. Exam trap: the commonest hallucination is auditory in schizophrenia but visual in organic states. Key note: some texts argue that delusions of one kind or another are near universal, making a the main rival, but the IPSS ranking supports c.", "traps": {"a": "Delusions are very common (delusions of persecution in about 64 percent in the IPSS), but in that study they ranked below auditory hallucinations, which makes this the strongest distractor.", "b": "Visual hallucinations are relatively uncommon in schizophrenia and are more typical of organic states such as delirium, drug intoxication and epilepsy.", "d": "Thought withdrawal, the experience that thoughts are being taken out of one's head, is a Schneiderian first-rank symptom, but it is far less frequent than hearing voices."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is one of the four A's identified by Eugene Bleuler as the primary symptoms of schizophrenia?", "concept": "The answer is d: all of the above, since abnormal association, autism and ambivalence are each one of Bleuler's four A's. The Swiss psychiatrist Eugen Bleuler (the paper spells it \"Eugene\"; the correct spelling is Eugen) coined the word schizophrenia in a 1908 lecture and in his 1911 monograph Dementia Praecox or the Group of Schizophrenias. He replaced Emil Kraepelin's \"dementia praecox\" because not every patient deteriorated or fell ill young. The name means a splitting of mental functions, thought, emotion and behaviour losing their connection, not a split personality. Bleuler held that four fundamental symptoms were present in every case: loosening of Associations (thoughts lose their logical links, so speech drifts from topic to topic); blunted or inappropriate Affect (smiling while describing a parent's death); Ambivalence (opposite feelings or wishes towards the same thing at once, such as loving and hating a brother, a term Bleuler coined); and Autism (withdrawal into a private inner world, quite different from today's autism spectrum disorder). Hallucinations and delusions he called accessory symptoms, because they also occur in other illnesses. Option d is right because three of the four A's are listed, and only Affect is missing. Exam trap: hallucinations are not one of the four A's, and alogia, avolition and anhedonia are later negative-symptom A's, not Bleuler's.", "traps": {"a": "True but incomplete: loosening of associations is one of the four A's, but autism and ambivalence are also listed, so \"all of the above\" is the better answer.", "b": "True but incomplete: autism, in Bleuler's sense of withdrawal into an inner world, is one of the four A's, but it is not the only one listed here.", "c": "True but incomplete: ambivalence is one of the four A's, a word Bleuler himself coined, but the other two options are also correct."}}, {"q": "Which of the following forms of schizophrenia has the best prognosis?", "concept": "The answer is a: paranoid schizophrenia has the best prognosis among these subtypes. The classical subtypes go back to the nineteenth century: Ewald Hecker described hebephrenia in 1871, Karl Ludwig Kahlbaum described catatonia in 1874, and Emil Kraepelin grouped these with a paranoid form under dementia praecox. ICD-10 kept paranoid, hebephrenic, catatonic, undifferentiated, residual and simple types. Paranoid schizophrenia, the commonest type, is dominated by fairly stable persecutory or grandiose delusions and voices, while speech, affect and behaviour stay relatively intact. Onset tends to be later, after the person has finished education, worked and formed relationships, so there is more premorbid functioning to return to. A 32-year-old clerk who believes his colleagues are plotting against him may still dress well, talk coherently and, with treatment, keep his job. Hebephrenic (disorganised) schizophrenia, by contrast, starts early with incoherence and silly, shallow affect, and has the worst outlook. Option a is right because later onset, preserved personality and predominantly positive symptoms all predict a better course. Exam trap: hebephrenic has the worst prognosis, not the best. Key note: some Indian review books give catatonic as the best prognosis, because acute catatonia often responds dramatically to lorazepam or ECT; and DSM-5 (2013) and ICD-11 have dropped the subtypes altogether.", "traps": {"b": "Catatonic schizophrenia (stupor, posturing, negativism or excitement) often responds well to lorazepam or ECT, and some review books call its prognosis the best, but most texts rank paranoid first.", "c": "Hebephrenic (disorganised) schizophrenia begins early, with incoherence, shallow or silly affect and negative symptoms, and carries the worst prognosis.", "d": "Undifferentiated schizophrenia is the category for cases that meet general criteria without fitting one subtype; it has no special claim to a good prognosis."}}, {"q": "A patient who presents to a clinic describes seeing ghosts when he hears classical music. What is the phenomenon described by the patient?", "concept": "The answer is d: seeing ghosts whenever classical music is heard is a reflex hallucination. In descriptive psychopathology, as set out in Frank Fish's Clinical Psychopathology and Andrew Sims's Symptoms in the Mind, a reflex hallucination occurs when a real stimulus in one sensory modality produces a hallucination in a different modality. Here a genuine auditory stimulus (the music) triggers a false visual perception (the ghosts). It is a morbid form of synaesthesia, the normal experience some people have of \"seeing\" colours when they hear sounds. Reflex hallucinations are reported in schizophrenia and with hallucinogens such as LSD. Its close cousin is the functional hallucination, in which a real stimulus triggers a hallucination in the same modality and both are perceived together: a patient hears voices only while the tap is running, and hears both the water and the voices. A simple way to remember: functional means the same sense, reflex means a jump to another sense. Option d is right because the trigger (music, heard) and the hallucination (ghosts, seen) are in different senses. Exam trap: students choose functional hallucination whenever a real stimulus is involved; check whether the hallucination stays in the same sense (functional) or crosses to another (reflex).", "traps": {"a": "A haptic hallucination is a false perception of touch, such as insects crawling under the skin (formication) in cocaine use or alcohol withdrawal; no touch is involved here.", "b": "Hypnagogic hallucinations occur while falling asleep (hypnopompic ones on waking); they are common in healthy people and in narcolepsy, and are tied to sleep, not to music.", "c": "An autoscopic hallucination is seeing one's own double, reported in epilepsy and organic brain states; this patient sees ghosts, not himself."}}, {"q": "Which of the following did Vaughn and Leff consider to be a component of expressed emotion”?", "concept": "The answer is d: all of the above, since critical comments, hostility and emotional overinvolvement are all components of expressed emotion (EE). The idea grew from George Brown's work in London, which showed that patients with schizophrenia discharged to certain families relapsed more often. Christine Vaughn and Julian Leff refined it in their 1976 study, using the Camberwell Family Interview, a taped semi-structured interview with a relative about the patient. Raters score critical comments, hostility and emotional overinvolvement (the three that define high EE), plus warmth and positive remarks. A mother who says \"he just lies in bed all day to annoy me\" is making critical comments; one who rejects him as a person shows hostility; one who has given up her job, will not let him go out alone and weeps through the interview shows overinvolvement. Vaughn and Leff found that patients returning to high-EE homes relapsed far more often within nine months, especially with more than 35 hours a week of face-to-face contact and no medication. Option d is right because all three listed elements are EE components. Exam trap: EE predicts relapse, not the onset of schizophrenia; it is not Frieda Fromm-Reichmann's \"schizophrenogenic mother\" or Gregory Bateson's double bind, and family work that lowers EE reduces relapse.", "traps": {"a": "True but incomplete: critical comments are the most important single EE component, but hostility and overinvolvement are components too.", "b": "True but incomplete: emotional overinvolvement (overprotection, self-sacrifice, exaggerated emotional response) is one EE component, not the only one.", "c": "True but incomplete: hostility, criticism of the patient as a person rather than of a particular behaviour, is one EE component alongside the other two."}}, {"q": "The term “dementia praecox” was coined by", "concept": "The answer is a: Emil Kraepelin. The German psychiatrist Emil Kraepelin (1856 to 1926) is the name attached to \"dementia praecox\", Latin for early (precocious) dementia. He used the term in the fourth edition of his textbook in 1893, and in the sixth edition of 1899 he drew his famous dividing line: dementia praecox, which begins in youth and runs a deteriorating course, versus manic-depressive insanity, which comes in episodes with good recovery between them. He united pictures described by others, hebephrenia (Ewald Hecker), catatonia (Karl Ludwig Kahlbaum) and paranoid states, as forms of one illness defined above all by its course. Think of a 19-year-old student who withdraws, hears voices and over the years loses his drive and social skills: to Kraepelin, the downhill course itself was diagnostic. In 1908 Eugen Bleuler renamed the group schizophrenia (his monograph followed in 1911) because not every patient deteriorated or fell ill young. Option a is right because Kraepelin made the Latin term famous and built the concept around it. Exam trap: the French physician Benedict Morel used the French phrase \"démence précoce\" earlier, in the 1850s, for young patients who deteriorated, and Arnold Pick used the Latin term in 1891; neither is offered here. Key note: strictly, Kraepelin popularised rather than coined the term, but among these options he is the standard answer.", "traps": {"b": "Eugen Bleuler (spelled \"Eugene\" in the paper) did not coin dementia praecox; he replaced it in 1908 with the new name schizophrenia (monograph 1911) and described the four A's.", "c": "Karl Jaspers wrote General Psychopathology (1913), founding the phenomenological method and the idea of primary delusions and understandability, but he did not coin dementia praecox.", "d": "Adolf Meyer, the Swiss-American founder of psychobiology, saw schizophrenia as a \"reaction type\" to life stresses and introduced the life chart; he did not coin the term."}}, {"q": "All of the following are features of endogenous depression except", "concept": "The answer is c: irritability is not a classic feature of endogenous depression. \"Endogenous\" means arising from within, and the term was used for depression that seemed biologically driven rather than a reaction to events. In mid-twentieth-century British psychiatry, Aubrey Lewis argued that depression was a single continuum, while Martin Roth's Newcastle group argued that endogenous and reactive (neurotic) depression were separate types. Today the endogenous picture survives as the DSM-5-TR specifier \"with melancholic features\" and the ICD-10 \"somatic syndrome\". Its hallmarks are profound loss of pleasure (anhedonia) with lack of mood reactivity, a distinct quality of low mood, depression worse in the morning, early morning waking, marked psychomotor retardation or agitation, significant loss of appetite and weight, and excessive guilt. Picture a 55-year-old teacher who wakes at 3 a.m., cannot enjoy even her grandchildren's visit, has lost 6 kg and blames herself for imagined failures, with no life event to explain it. Irritability, by contrast, is more typical of reactive or neurotic depression, of depression in children and adolescents (where DSM-5-TR allows irritable mood in place of sad mood) and of mixed states. Option c is right because irritability is not part of the melancholic or somatic syndrome. Exam trap: in this \"except\" question the three biological features (appetite, weight, anhedonia) belong together; the odd one out is the reactive, emotional feature.", "traps": {"a": "Loss of appetite is a classic biological symptom of endogenous depression, listed in both the DSM-5-TR melancholic specifier and the ICD-10 somatic syndrome.", "b": "Marked weight loss (ICD-10 suggests 5 percent or more of body weight in the past month) is a core somatic feature of endogenous depression.", "d": "Anhedonia, the pervasive loss of pleasure, is the central feature of melancholic depression; DSM-5-TR requires it, or lack of mood reactivity, for the specifier."}}, {"q": "Who proposed the theory of “Learned helplessness” to explain depression?", "concept": "The answer is b: Martin Seligman. Learned helplessness was discovered in the late 1960s, when Martin Seligman and Steven Maier gave dogs electric shocks they could not escape or control. Later, in a shuttle box where jumping over a low barrier would end the shock, many of these dogs did not even try; they lay down and whimpered, while dogs without the earlier experience quickly learned to escape. The animals had learned that their actions made no difference. Seligman saw a parallel with human depression: passivity, low motivation, slowed learning and sad mood after repeated uncontrollable bad events, and he set this out in his 1975 book Helplessness. A student who fails maths several times despite real effort may stop studying and conclude \"nothing I do matters\". In 1978 Lyn Abramson, Seligman and John Teasdale reformulated the theory in attributional terms: people at risk explain bad events as internal (\"my fault\"), stable (\"it will last\") and global (\"it ruins everything\"). This later grew into the hopelessness theory and Seligman's work on explanatory style and learned optimism. Option b is right because Seligman proposed learned helplessness as a model of depression. Exam trap: Beck's cognitive triad is also a theory of depression, so read the term carefully: learned helplessness is Seligman, the negative triad is Beck.", "traps": {"a": "Joseph Wolpe developed systematic desensitisation from the principle of reciprocal inhibition, a behaviour therapy for phobias and anxiety, not learned helplessness.", "c": "Sigmund Freud explained depression psychodynamically in \"Mourning and Melancholia\" (1917), as the loss of a loved object with anger turned inward against the self.", "d": "Aaron Beck proposed the cognitive model of depression, with its negative cognitive triad, schemas and cognitive distortions, not learned helplessness."}}, {"q": "The “Negative Cognitive Triad” was proposed by Beck to explain", "concept": "The answer is c: depression. The American psychiatrist Aaron Beck, originally trained in psychoanalysis, noticed in the 1960s that his depressed patients' thinking was dominated by consistently negative, distorted thoughts. He proposed the negative cognitive triad: a negative view of the self (\"I am worthless\"), of the world or present experience (\"nothing ever goes right for me\") and of the future (\"it will always be like this\"). These views are driven by dysfunctional schemas formed early in life, activated by stress, and kept alive by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, selective abstraction, arbitrary inference, personalisation and all-or-nothing thinking. A young employee who receives one piece of criticism may think \"I'm useless\" (self), \"my boss hates me\" (world) and \"I'll never succeed in any job\" (future). Beck built cognitive therapy on this model, teaching patients to catch their automatic thoughts, test them against evidence and replace them with balanced ones; his 1979 book Cognitive Therapy of Depression described the method. Option c is right because the triad was proposed specifically to explain depression. Exam trap: Beck also wrote a cognitive model of anxiety, but it centres on overestimating danger and underestimating one's coping, not on the triad; and the triad is self, world and future, with no corner for the past.", "traps": {"a": "Beck's cognitive model of anxiety is built on danger schemas, overestimating threat and underestimating one's ability to cope, rather than the negative triad.", "b": "Bereavement is a normal grief reaction, described in phases by John Bowlby and Colin Murray Parkes; self-esteem is usually preserved in uncomplicated grief, and the triad was not built to explain it.", "d": "Adjustment disorder is a distress reaction beginning within 3 months of an identifiable stressor (DSM-5-TR); it was not the target of Beck's triad."}}, {"q": "According to ICD-10, for a diagnosis of a depressive episode, the symptoms should be present for a minimum of", "concept": "The answer is b: 2 weeks. ICD-10, the World Health Organization's classification published in 1992, requires that a depressive episode last at least two weeks, although a shorter period may be accepted if the symptoms are unusually severe and of rapid onset. DSM-5-TR uses the same two-week rule for a major depressive episode. The two weeks separate a clinical episode from ordinary bad days: everyone has a sad weekend, but a fortnight of almost continuous symptoms is a different matter. ICD-10 lists three typical symptoms (depressed mood, loss of interest and enjoyment, and reduced energy with increased fatigability) and seven others, including poor concentration, low self-esteem, guilt, pessimism, ideas of self-harm, disturbed sleep and reduced appetite. Severity is graded by count: a mild episode needs two typical plus two other symptoms, a moderate episode two typical plus three or four others, and a severe episode all three typical plus at least four others. A woman who has felt low, joyless and exhausted for three weeks, with poor sleep and guilt, meets the time rule. Option b is right because two weeks is the ICD-10 minimum. Exam trap: one week is the minimum for a manic episode and one month is the ICD-10 duration for schizophrenia, so match each number to its disorder.", "traps": {"a": "One week is the minimum duration for a manic episode in ICD-10 and DSM-5-TR (or any duration if hospitalisation is needed), not for depression.", "c": "One month is the ICD-10 duration for schizophrenia symptoms and the DSM-5-TR duration for PTSD symptoms, not for a depressive episode.", "d": "Two months appears elsewhere, for example in the old DSM-IV bereavement exclusion and the DSM-5-TR rule for full remission of major depression, but not as the minimum for an episode."}}, {"q": "Who first described autism?", "concept": "The answer is a: Leo Kanner. The Austrian-American child psychiatrist Leo Kanner, working at Johns Hopkins in Baltimore, published \"Autistic Disturbances of Affective Contact\" in 1943, describing 11 children who from early life showed extreme aloneness, an inability to relate normally to people, odd or delayed language (echolalia, pronoun reversal, very literal speech) and an \"anxiously obsessive desire for the maintenance of sameness\". Such a child might become distressed if a single piece of furniture was moved. He gave it the name early infantile autism in a 1944 paper, and for decades it was known as Kanner's syndrome. In 1944, a year after Kanner's first paper, the Austrian paediatrician Hans Asperger independently described boys with \"autistic psychopathy\": fluent speech and normal intelligence but poor social understanding, narrow interests and clumsiness. His German-language paper became widely known only after Lorna Wing's 1981 paper on \"Asperger's syndrome\". DSM-5-TR now merges both pictures into autism spectrum disorder. Option a is right because Kanner's 1943 paper is the first standard description of autism as a childhood syndrome. Exam trap: the word \"autism\" was coined earlier by Eugen Bleuler (1910, then in his 1911 book on schizophrenia), but for withdrawal in schizophrenia, not for the developmental disorder; and Asperger came after Kanner, not before.", "traps": {"b": "Hans Asperger described \"autistic psychopathy\" in 1944, a year after Kanner, in boys with good language and intelligence but poor social skills.", "c": "Stella Chess, with Alexander Thomas, ran the New York Longitudinal Study of temperament (easy, difficult and slow-to-warm-up children); she also studied autism after congenital rubella, but she did not first describe it.", "d": "Benham is linked with Benham's top, a spinning black-and-white disc that produces illusory colours; the name has nothing to do with autism."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the person with oppositional defiant disorder?", "concept": "The answer is d: all of the above. Oppositional defiant disorder (ODD) entered the DSM in 1980 (DSM-III, as oppositional disorder) and took its present name in DSM-III-R (1987). DSM-5-TR defines it as a pattern of angry or irritable mood, argumentative or defiant behaviour, or vindictiveness lasting at least six months, shown by at least four of eight symptoms with at least one person who is not a sibling. Angry or irritable mood covers often losing temper, being touchy or easily annoyed, and being angry and resentful. Argumentative or defiant behaviour covers arguing with authority figures, refusing rules and requests, deliberately annoying others, and blaming others for one's own mistakes or misbehaviour. Vindictiveness means being spiteful at least twice in six months. A nine-year-old who explodes when told to switch off the TV, hides his sister's books to provoke her and says \"the teacher made me fail\" fits the picture. Conduct disorder is different: it violates others' rights through aggression, destruction, theft and serious rule-breaking. Option d is right because temper loss, blaming others and deliberately annoying others are all DSM-5-TR symptoms. Exam trap: ODD is defiance without serious aggression; once a child is cruel, destructive or stealing, think conduct disorder. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "traps": {"a": "True but incomplete: often losing temper is one of the angry or irritable mood symptoms of ODD, but the other options are also criteria.", "b": "True but incomplete: blaming others for one's own mistakes or misbehaviour is an ODD symptom in the argumentative or defiant group, alongside the others listed.", "c": "True but incomplete: deliberately annoying others is an ODD symptom, but temper loss and blaming others are criteria too."}}, {"q": "Raven's Standard Progressive Matrices provide a direct measure of", "concept": "The answer is c: abstract intelligence. Raven's Standard Progressive Matrices (SPM) was published in 1938 by the British psychologist John C. Raven, a student of Charles Spearman. Each item shows a matrix of designs with one piece missing, and the person picks the piece that completes it from six or eight alternatives. The SPM has 60 items in five sets (A to E) of 12, growing harder as it goes, hence \"progressive\". No words, reading or general knowledge are needed: to solve an item you must spot the rule running across rows and columns and apply it. Raven designed it to measure Spearman's general factor g, specifically eductive ability, the capacity to draw out relations and make sense of new material; in modern terms it is a classic test of fluid, abstract, non-verbal reasoning. Being largely free of language and schooling, it is used as a culture-reduced test, including in India. Related forms are the Coloured Progressive Matrices for young children and older adults and the Advanced Progressive Matrices for able adults. Option c is right because the SPM directly taps abstract reasoning with non-verbal symbols. Exam trap: \"non-verbal\" does not mean \"concrete\"; the matrices are abstract patterns, not real objects to handle. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "traps": {"a": "Verbal intelligence is measured by tests that use words, such as the vocabulary, similarities and comprehension subtests of the Wechsler scales; Raven's needs no language at all.", "b": "Concrete (mechanical) intelligence, in Edward Thorndike's three-way split, is skill in handling objects and things, measured by performance tests such as form boards; the matrices are abstract patterns.", "d": "Emotional intelligence, proposed by Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990) and popularised by Daniel Goleman, is measured by tools such as the MSCEIT, not by Raven's matrices."}}, {"q": "Problem-solving comprises four stages. Choose the correct sequence of stages:", "concept": "The answer is b: preparation, incubation, illumination, verification. This sequence comes from the English social psychologist Graham Wallas in his 1926 book The Art of Thought, drawing on Henri Poincare's and Hermann von Helmholtz's accounts of discovery. Wallas described it as the creative process, and textbooks often teach it as the stages of creative problem-solving. In preparation you define the problem and gather information, working on it consciously. In incubation you set the problem aside, but work seems to continue outside awareness. In illumination the solution appears suddenly, the \"aha\" moment, often during something unrelated: Poincare reported that a mathematical solution came to him just as he stepped onto a bus. In verification you test and refine the idea to check that it really works. A student stuck on a statistics problem who studies it for an hour (preparation), goes for a walk (incubation), suddenly sees the answer in the shower (illumination) and then checks it on paper (verification) has gone through all four. Option b is right because it is the only option that gives Wallas's stages in his order. Exam trap: incubation always comes after preparation, since you cannot incubate a problem you have not worked on, and before illumination. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "traps": {"a": "This puts incubation before preparation and verification before illumination; you cannot check an idea before it has arrived.", "c": "This starts with incubation, but a problem can only incubate after it has been studied in the preparation stage.", "d": "This swaps incubation and illumination; the insight comes after the period of rest, not before it."}}, {"q": "The child who says “Milk gone” engages in ______. This type of utterance demonstrates that children are actively experimenting with the rules of ______.", "concept": "The answer is b: telegraphic speech, syntax. Between about 18 and 24 months, children move from single words to two-word utterances such as \"Milk gone\", \"Daddy car\". Roger Brown, the Harvard psychologist who studied early language in detail, called this telegraphic speech because, like an old telegram where every word cost money, it keeps the content words and drops function words and endings such as \"the\", \"is\" and \"-ed\". These short utterances are not random. The words come in a consistent order that follows the grammar of the child's language: the child says \"Milk gone\", not \"Gone milk\". Ordering words to express who did what to whom is syntax, the set of rules for combining words into sentences. Since adults rarely say \"Milk gone\", the child is not just copying but actively experimenting with syntactic rules. A later sign of the same rule learning is overregularisation, as in \"I goed\" or \"two mouses\". Option b is right because \"Milk gone\" is a two-word telegraphic utterance that shows the child using word-order rules. Exam trap: babbling (repeated syllables like \"ba-ba-ba\" from about six months) comes before real words, and semantics is about meaning, while the point of telegraphic speech is rule-governed word order. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "traps": {"a": "Babbling, repeated consonant-vowel syllables such as \"ba-ba\" from about six months, comes before first words and has no grammar; \"Milk gone\" is a two-word sentence.", "c": "Both parts are wrong: babbling is pre-linguistic, and semantics (word meaning) is not what this utterance is meant to demonstrate.", "d": "Telegraphic speech is right, but semantics refers to meaning; errors like overextension (calling every man \"daddy\") show semantic learning, whereas consistent word order shows syntax."}}, {"q": "This area contains “Somatosensory Cortex”", "concept": "The answer is a: the parietal lobe. The primary somatosensory cortex lies on the postcentral gyrus, the strip of cortex just behind the central sulcus, which divides the frontal from the parietal lobe. It corresponds to Brodmann areas 3, 1 and 2 and receives touch, pressure, temperature, pain and body-position information from the opposite side of the body. The Canadian neurosurgeon Wilder Penfield mapped it during epilepsy surgery by stimulating the exposed cortex of awake patients, who reported tingling in particular body parts. The result is the sensory homunculus, a distorted body map in which the lips, tongue and fingers get huge areas because they are densely supplied with receptors, while the back and legs get little. Just in front, on the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe, lies the primary motor cortex. Parietal damage causes loss of fine touch and position sense, astereognosis (failing to recognise an object such as a key by touch) and, with right-sided lesions, neglect of the left side of space. Option a is right because the postcentral gyrus is part of the parietal lobe. Exam trap: motor is in front of the central sulcus (frontal), sensory is behind it (parietal); students often swap them. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "traps": {"b": "The frontal lobe contains the primary motor cortex (precentral gyrus), Broca's area and the prefrontal cortex for planning and self-control, not the somatosensory cortex.", "c": "The temporal lobe contains the primary auditory cortex (Heschl's gyrus), Wernicke's area and, on its inner surface, the hippocampus for memory.", "d": "The occipital lobe contains the primary visual cortex (striate cortex, area 17); damage causes visual field loss or cortical blindness."}}, {"q": "When a child gets two chocolates once every week for its performance in class, it is an instance of", "concept": "The answer is b: fixed ratio, fixed interval. Schedules of reinforcement were studied by Burrhus Frederic (B. F.) Skinner with Charles Ferster in their 1957 book Schedules of Reinforcement. A ratio schedule rewards after a number of responses: fixed ratio (FR) after a set number, as in piece-rate pay, and variable ratio (VR) after an unpredictable number, as in a slot machine. An interval schedule rewards the first response after a period of time: fixed interval (FI) after a set time, as in a weekly allowance or monthly salary, and variable interval (VI) after unpredictable times, as in surprise quizzes. FI produces a \"scallop\": little effort just after a reward and a burst as the next one approaches, like students who study mostly before exams. Here the child is rewarded once every week, a fixed time, so the core schedule is fixed interval. The amount is also fixed, two chocolates every time, which is what the setter calls \"fixed ratio\". Option b is right because it is the only option that pairs fixed interval with a fixed reward. Exam trap: ratio means number of responses and interval means time; a fixed amount of reward is not, strictly, a ratio schedule. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts; b is the only defensible choice.", "traps": {"a": "Variable interval would mean rewards at unpredictable times, but the chocolates come predictably once every week.", "c": "Variable ratio means reward after an unpredictable number of responses, as in gambling; here both the timing and the amount (two chocolates) are fixed.", "d": "Both parts are wrong: nothing about the reward is variable, since it is always two chocolates, always weekly."}}, {"q": "Feeling touchy or hypersensitive following an upsetting experience is a form of", "concept": "The answer is c: sensitization. Sensitization is, with habituation, the simplest kind of learning, and is called non-associative because it involves a single stimulus rather than a link between two. After a strong, upsetting or painful stimulus, the organism becomes more responsive to a whole range of other stimuli, even mild ones. Eric Kandel, who shared the 2000 Nobel Prize, traced its cellular basis in the sea slug Aplysia: after a shock to the tail, a light touch to the siphon produced a much bigger gill-withdrawal reflex than before. Human examples are everyday. After a near-miss on the road at night, you jump at every horn and flash of headlights; after a frightening film, every creak in the house makes your heart race. That touchy, hypersensitive state is sensitization. Its near opposite is habituation, in which the response to a repeated, harmless stimulus decreases, as when you stop noticing the trains outside a new hostel room after a few nights. Option c is right because the heightened reactivity follows an upsetting experience, which is exactly how sensitization arises. Exam trap: habituation means reacting less to a repeated harmless stimulus, sensitization means reacting more after an upsetting one; textbooks pair them, so students swap them. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "traps": {"a": "Imprinting, studied by Konrad Lorenz in goslings, is rapid attachment to the first moving object seen during a critical period early in life, not hypersensitivity after an upset.", "b": "Habituation is the opposite: a decreased response to a repeated, harmless stimulus, such as no longer noticing a ticking clock.", "d": "A reflex is an automatic, unlearned response to a specific stimulus, such as the knee jerk or eye blink; feeling touchy after an upset is a learned change in responsiveness."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is the final destination for much of the brain's information about emotion before action is taken?", "concept": "The answer is b: the prefrontal cortex. Emotional information passes through several stations. Joseph LeDoux showed that sensory signals reach the amygdala by a fast, crude \"low road\" straight from the thalamus and by a slower, detailed \"high road\" through the cortex, so the amygdala can raise the alarm before we know what we have seen. Deciding what to do about the feeling happens at the front of the brain. The prefrontal cortex, especially its ventromedial and orbitofrontal parts, receives input from the amygdala, the insula and the senses, weighs it against goals, rules and likely consequences, and then guides action. Antonio Damasio's somatic marker hypothesis (1994) proposes that these regions use bodily emotional signals to steer decisions; patients with such damage, like the railway foreman Phineas Gage in 1848, keep their intelligence but make reckless, socially inappropriate choices. If a stranger shouts at you on a bus, your amygdala fires and your heart races, but the prefrontal cortex decides whether to shout back, walk away or laugh. Option b is right because the prefrontal cortex is the final integration point for emotional information before action is chosen. Exam trap: the amygdala is the emotional alarm, not the decision-maker. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "traps": {"a": "The amygdala detects threat and tags stimuli with emotional significance, especially fear, but it is an early station that passes information on to the prefrontal cortex, not the final destination.", "c": "The anterior cingulate cortex monitors conflict and errors and adds the unpleasantness to pain; it belongs to the emotion network but is not its final destination.", "d": "The hypothalamus carries out the bodily expression of emotion through the autonomic nervous system and hormones (as Philip Bard's sham rage work showed), rather than choosing an action."}}, {"q": "Amit met with an accident and later turned impulsive, irresponsible and less concerned with the consequences of his actions. He must have suffered damage in:", "concept": "The answer is b: the frontal lobe. The frontal lobes, especially the prefrontal cortex in front of the motor areas, handle planning, judgement, inhibition of impulses and foreseeing consequences, together called executive functions. The classic case is Phineas Gage, a railway foreman whose left frontal lobe was pierced by an iron tamping rod in an explosion in 1848. He survived and could walk, talk and remember, but his physician, John Harlow, reported that he became fitful, impulsive, profane, obstinate and unable to carry out plans; his friends said he was \"no longer Gage\". Modern neuropsychology describes several frontal syndromes. Orbitofrontal damage produces disinhibition: impulsivity, tactlessness, poor social judgement and indifference to consequences. Dorsolateral damage produces a dysexecutive syndrome with poor planning, rigidity and perseveration, and medial damage produces apathy. Amit's new impulsiveness, irresponsibility and lack of concern for consequences after an accident fit the disinhibited picture, a common result of head injury because the undersides of the frontal lobes bruise easily against the bony skull base. Option b is right because personality change with impulsivity and poor judgement is the hallmark of frontal damage. Exam trap: memory loss points to the temporal lobes; personality change and poor judgement point to the frontal lobes. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "traps": {"a": "Parietal damage disturbs touch, body sense and spatial attention, producing astereognosis or neglect of one side of space, not impulsive personality change.", "c": "Occipital damage causes visual problems such as field defects, cortical blindness or visual agnosia, not changes in judgement or impulse control.", "d": "Temporal damage typically affects memory, hearing and language comprehension (Wernicke's aphasia), and bilateral lesions can cause Kluver-Bucy syndrome, but consequence-blind, irresponsible behaviour is the frontal picture."}}, {"q": "Learning disabilities in Mathematics can be assessed most appropriately by which of the following tests?", "concept": "The answer is b: diagnostic tests. Anne Anastasi, in Psychological Testing, separates the survey achievement test, which gives an overall level of attainment, from the diagnostic test, which analyses a person's specific strengths and weaknesses within a subject and points to the source of difficulty. A learning disability in mathematics (dyscalculia) is not just low marks; it is a particular pattern of breakdown, for example in number sense, memory for arithmetic facts, place value, carrying and borrowing, or word problems. A diagnostic test breaks arithmetic into these sub-skills and analyses the errors. Suppose a Class 4 child scores 40 percent in maths. An achievement test only confirms she is behind. A diagnostic test, such as KeyMath or the arithmetic section of the NIMHANS Index for Specific Learning Disabilities, can show that she adds well but always takes the smaller digit from the larger (52 minus 17 gives 45), which tells the teacher exactly what to remedy. Option b is right because diagnostic tests are built to pinpoint the nature of a specific learning difficulty. Exam trap: achievement tests show how much has been learned and confirm low attainment, but do not show why the child is failing; diagnostic tests do. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts.", "traps": {"a": "Aptitude tests predict how well a person is likely to learn a skill in future (for example the Differential Aptitude Tests); they do not analyse an existing difficulty in mathematics.", "c": "Screening tests are short tools given to many children to flag those who need a closer look; they suggest risk but cannot establish or describe a learning disability.", "d": "Achievement tests measure how much has been learned and can show that attainment is below expectation, but a general achievement score does not reveal which specific skills are failing."}}, {"q": "Following a head injury, a victim's performance dropped in a language test but not a spatial one. This may be due to a possible injury in the following brain area", "concept": "The answer is b: Broca's area. In about 95 percent of right-handed people, and most left-handers, language depends mainly on the left hemisphere, while the right hemisphere is relatively better at spatial tasks, face recognition and the emotional tone of speech. The first clear evidence came from the French surgeon Paul Broca, who in 1861 examined a patient named Leborgne, nicknamed \"Tan\" because it was almost the only syllable he could say. At autopsy Broca found a lesion in the left inferior frontal gyrus, now called Broca's area (Brodmann areas 44 and 45). Damage here causes Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia: slow, effortful, telegraphic speech with comprehension relatively preserved. In the question, a head-injured person does worse on a language test but not on a spatial one. That pattern points to the left hemisphere, and among the options only Broca's area is a left-hemisphere language area. Option b is right because a Broca's area injury impairs language while sparing spatial abilities, which rely more on the right hemisphere. Exam trap: right hemisphere damage would do the reverse, hitting spatial skills more than language. Key note: the coaching book's answer page for Q90 to Q100 is missing from the pack, so this key is set from standard texts; strictly the pattern points to the left hemisphere generally (Wernicke's area would also fit), and Broca's area is the best option offered.", "traps": {"a": "Right hemisphere damage would mainly impair spatial skills, face recognition and prosody while leaving language largely intact, the reverse of this pattern.", "c": "The reticular activating system in the brainstem controls arousal and wakefulness; damage causes drowsiness or coma, not a selective language deficit.", "d": "The medulla controls vital functions such as breathing, heart rate and blood pressure; serious damage there is life-threatening rather than language-specific."}}]});
